\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/USD Retreats;May See 0.9500 Before 1.0200-BNP\b0\par\par\uc2 1,812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 06:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD pulls back off post-float highs today as USD traders pare some short positions, with risk being further declines could be coming, says Rob Ryan, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst with \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "At the moment, everyone has assumed the U.S. dollar is totally toast and it's a good idea to buy anything else. But the market is pricing in an enormous amount of (quantitative easing) and the risk now is we don't get quite as much as we were hoping for," says Ryan, adding biggest risk to AUD/USD isn't China or the Aussie economy, but positioning. "It just needs a washout badly, a thinning where we may see 0.95 before 1.02." AUD/USD recently at 0.9792, down from day's high of 0.9843. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] STI down 0.2% at 3155.55 at midday break, tracking falls across region after flat session on Wall Street; losers on broad market outpacing gainers about 2.2 to 1. Benchmark index consolidating after Wednesday rising to highest level since June 2 2008; still, DBS Group Research says rally that started late August (around 2900 level) sustainable, tips index laggards such as UOB (U11.SG), Capitaland (C31.SG), SIA (C6L.SG), \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2  (BN4.SG) should benefit from sustained liquidity inflows into region, including Singapore. Raises STI technical objective to 3438, says near-term resistance at 3250. Small-cap FreightLinks (F01.SG) +13.3% at S$0.085 in good volume on news of sale, leaseback of 5 properties; \uc2 Neptune Orient Lines\uc2  (N03.SG) down 1.9% at S$2.03 after ops data show fall in freight rates, Biosensors (B20.SG) off 3.7% at S$1.04 after yesterday's 8.0% gain. For today, support expected at 3132 (Oct. 1 high). (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.5% at 23,087.42 midday, as profit-taking finally sets in after 6-day winning streak; volume tad lower than recent average at HK$45.49 billion, suggesting selling pressure not heavy. "Hong Kong market's current round of rallying is fast and furious. Many investors have not been able to get in and are eying pullback as chances to buy, which in turn may result in a less significant correction if any," says Alvin Cheung, associate director at Prudential Brokerage; tips index to consolidate around 23,000 in near term, before more upside in 4Q. HK developers succumb to profit-taking: \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) down 1.4% at HK$136.30, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) off 2.4% at HK$118.50, but quasi-property MTRC (0066.HK) +4.1% at HK$30.80 after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  upgrades stock to Buy from Neutral. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.1% at 1486.10 as weakness in regional markets weighs, though decline limited by gains in plantation, infrastructure stocks. Dealers, analysts say weakness likely temporary as underlying sentiment still strong, buoyed by rally in palm oil prices, expectations government's 2011 budget, to be unveiled Friday, will be market-friendly. These factors "could pave the way for the KLCI...to retake the psychological level of 1500, before gearing for an eventual retest of the all-time high level of 1524.69," RHB Research says. Among gainers, palm oil producer Genting Plantations (2291.KU) +5.7% at MYR8.60 after palm oil prices hit 26-month high, builder MRCB (1651.KU) +1.5% at MYR2.05. Among decliners, Parkson (5657.KU) down 1.7% at MYR5.90, AMMB (1015.KU) down 1.3% at MYR5.89, both on profit-taking after strong gains recently. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 1.6% at 9430, extending losses in afternoon as USD/JPY falls below 82 (now 81.92), market analysts say. "While (USD/JPY at) 82 mark had cast concerns over exporters' earnings, now that it has breached that threshold, more selling may be possible," says Japanese brokerage manager. Puts support for futures at 9250 if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair nears 81.50. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 1.8% at 9421.80 vs morning close of 9504.39 (off 0.9%), with volume picking up as USD/JPY falls under 82, inches closer to 15-year low of 81.37 (now 81.92). "It's unclear when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market), but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to happen, and that is providing some support that further drastic yen appreciation will not occur," says Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities. Adds investors paying close attention to whether Nikkei support will hold at 9400 through close; if that support is breached, index could fall below 9300. 32/33 Topix subindexes lower; \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) now off 8.8% at Y11,300 after FY profit forecast cut. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), or "Tepco", off 3.9% at Y1,912 in heavy volume on selling by hedge funds with share offer pricing window opening today through Thursday. Consumer lenders off; Aiful (8515.TO) down 12% at Y58. Insurance subindex only gainer on board; \uc2 Tokio Marine Holdings\uc2  (8766.TO) up 0.7% at Y2,369. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares extend retreat as investors continue to take profit following market's substantial gains last month. Main gauge PSEi ends 1.2% lower at 4166.52 amid heavy trading. "We believe the market was overdue for a correction," says Erwin Balita, SB Equities research head, adding some investors may also be reallocating their funds to accommodate IPO of budget carrier Cebu Air; IPO opens Oct. 12. Tips market likely to continue consolidation, may stay within 4100-4200 range. Leading market slide are SM Investments (SM.PH), off 2.0% at PHP525; Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), off 2.3% at PHP72.75; PLDT (TEL.PH), off 1.2% at PHP2,698. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold and silver under profit-taking pressure in Asia as U.S. dollar looking for direction. Spot gold at $1,349/oz, down $4.90 since NY close; spot silver at $23.19/oz, down 9 cents. Overnight bout of USD strength against both JPY, EUR has stalled precious metals' stellar run, with FOMC meeting minutes at 1800 GMT likely to be key to next moves. Support for gold at $1,320, silver at $22.90, says ScotiaMocatta. Many in market hoping for correction to get better entry point given strong consensus view renewed quantitative easing is a done deal. Dips have been eagerly bought in recent days; "All dips in both gold and silver are met by a wall of buying and both precious metals keep roaring back time and time again," says Bill Murphy, gold commentator at lemetropolecafe website. However, correction can't be ruled out as some traders view QE as somewhat priced in to gold, USD is oversold. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 1.1% at 454.32, vs day's high 460, with local investors selling after some brokerage firms, including Hochiminh Securities, forecasting many companies will post weak third-quarter earnings. "Sentiment, which was weak because of high interest rate concerns, took a further hit on disappointment over the weak outlook for profits in the previous quarter," says analyst with Tan Viet Securities. Volume thin with 28.6 million shares valued at VND686 billion traded. Among actives, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 2.0% at VND29,500, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends down 1.2% at VND16,600, REE (REE.VH) ends down 1.3% at VND15,600. Trader tips more losses tomorrow with support for key index at 450. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says global advance in equities and rise in U.S. bond prices have pushed up major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cross pairs vs yen while undermining value of USD. "We think that puts the spotlight on the next major trend in the U.S., and whether an earnings-driven market (rise in stocks, fall in bonds) or a negative earnings-driven market (fall in stocks, rise in bonds) replaces the recent liquidity-driven market (rise in both stocks and bonds)." Says the former would likely lead to gains in USD vs JPY, as well as broad weakness in the latter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds, as global economy improves, thinks investors may choose to sell yen in light of Japan's relatively weak economic outlook (now that eco-car incentives have expired). If so, says combination of global resurgence in risk appetite and efforts to reduce exposure to Japan could pave the way for more meaningful decline in yen (helping Japan stocks, as exporter shares are particularly sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements). Nikkei off 1.6% at 9431.73 as USD/JPY falls under 92 (now 81.91).(bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY pares gains midday as ICE Dollar Index falls from early highs. Pair last at 6.6703 vs intraday high of 6.6760, 6.6678 yesterday; dollar index at 77.484 vs 77.590 earlier. "Though the central parity was set higher (from yesterday) in the morning, it failed to reflect how much the dollar gained against other majors overnight. This probably suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants the yuan to appreciate a bit," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Central parity at 6.6775 vs 6.6732. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 1.6% at 4619.0 hits fresh 5-day low of 4618.9 in thin trading. Offshore markets are down in Asia, despite a lack of fundamental triggers for selling. S&P/ASX 200 is clearly underperforming other markets in Asia, suggesting AUD/USD strength is playing a part in the selling. S&P/ASX 200 certainly jolted lower before lunch when AUD/USD broke 0.9800. "The Aussie dollar has pulled back a bit and everyone's waiting for QE2 (quantitative easing in U.S.) to come out," says an RBS trader. "The market's had a good run and could be due for a few days' pullback. It's very thin and I'm not reading too much into it." Expect some support at the minor uptrend line at 4600 but developing 5-point reversal pattern could see key support at 4559 tested this week, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 0056ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
