\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Off 1.8% As USD/JPY Falls Below 82\b0\par\par\uc2 1,783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 06:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 1.8% at 9421.80 vs morning close of 9504.39 (off 0.9%), with volume picking up as USD/JPY falls under 82, inches closer to 15-year low of 81.37 (now 81.92). "It's unclear when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market), but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to happen, and that is providing some support that further drastic yen appreciation will not occur," says Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities. Adds investors paying close attention to whether Nikkei support will hold at 9400 through close; if that support is breached, index could fall below 9300. 32/33 Topix subindexes lower; \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) now off 8.8% at Y11,300 after FY profit forecast cut. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO), or "Tepco", off 3.9% at Y1,912 in heavy volume on selling by hedge funds with share offer pricing window opening today through Thursday. Consumer lenders off; Aiful (8515.TO) down 12% at Y58. Insurance subindex only gainer on board; \uc2 Tokio Marine Holdings\uc2  (8766.TO) up 0.7% at Y2,369. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares extend retreat as investors continue to take profit following market's substantial gains last month. Main gauge PSEi ends 1.2% lower at 4166.52 amid heavy trading. "We believe the market was overdue for a correction," says Erwin Balita, SB Equities research head, adding some investors may also be reallocating their funds to accommodate IPO of budget carrier Cebu Air; IPO opens Oct. 12. Tips market likely to continue consolidation, may stay within 4100-4200 range. Leading market slide are SM Investments (SM.PH), off 2.0% at PHP525; Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH), off 2.3% at PHP72.75; PLDT (TEL.PH), off 1.2% at PHP2,698. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold and silver under profit-taking pressure in Asia as U.S. dollar looking for direction. Spot gold at $1,349/oz, down $4.90 since NY close; spot silver at $23.19/oz, down 9 cents. Overnight bout of USD strength against both JPY, EUR has stalled precious metals' stellar run, with FOMC meeting minutes at 1800 GMT likely to be key to next moves. Support for gold at $1,320, silver at $22.90, says ScotiaMocatta. Many in market hoping for correction to get better entry point given strong consensus view renewed quantitative easing is a done deal. Dips have been eagerly bought in recent days; "All dips in both gold and silver are met by a wall of buying and both precious metals keep roaring back time and time again," says Bill Murphy, gold commentator at lemetropolecafe website. However, correction can't be ruled out as some traders view QE as somewhat priced in to gold, USD is oversold. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 1.1% at 454.32, vs day's high 460, with local investors selling after some brokerage firms, including Hochiminh Securities, forecasting many companies will post weak third-quarter earnings. "Sentiment, which was weak because of high interest rate concerns, took a further hit on disappointment over the weak outlook for profits in the previous quarter," says analyst with Tan Viet Securities. Volume thin with 28.6 million shares valued at VND686 billion traded. Among actives, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 2.0% at VND29,500, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends down 1.2% at VND16,600, REE (REE.VH) ends down 1.3% at VND15,600. Trader tips more losses tomorrow with support for key index at 450. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says global advance in equities and rise in U.S. bond prices have pushed up major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cross pairs vs yen while undermining value of USD. "We think that puts the spotlight on the next major trend in the U.S., and whether an earnings-driven market (rise in stocks, fall in bonds) or a negative earnings-driven market (fall in stocks, rise in bonds) replaces the recent liquidity-driven market (rise in both stocks and bonds)." Says the former would likely lead to gains in USD vs JPY, as well as broad weakness in the latter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds, as global economy improves, thinks investors may choose to sell yen in light of Japan's relatively weak economic outlook (now that eco-car incentives have expired). If so, says combination of global resurgence in risk appetite and efforts to reduce exposure to Japan could pave the way for more meaningful decline in yen (helping Japan stocks, as exporter shares are particularly sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements). Nikkei off 1.6% at 9431.73 as USD/JPY falls under 92 (now 81.91).(bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY pares gains midday as ICE Dollar Index falls from early highs. Pair last at 6.6703 vs intraday high of 6.6760, 6.6678 yesterday; dollar index at 77.484 vs 77.590 earlier. "Though the central parity was set higher (from yesterday) in the morning, it failed to reflect how much the dollar gained against other majors overnight. This probably suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants the yuan to appreciate a bit," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Central parity at 6.6775 vs 6.6732. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 1.6% at 4619.0 hits fresh 5-day low of 4618.9 in thin trading. Offshore markets are down in Asia, despite a lack of fundamental triggers for selling. S&P/ASX 200 is clearly underperforming other markets in Asia, suggesting AUD/USD strength is playing a part in the selling. S&P/ASX 200 certainly jolted lower before lunch when AUD/USD broke 0.9800. "The Aussie dollar has pulled back a bit and everyone's waiting for QE2 (quantitative easing in U.S.) to come out," says an RBS trader. "The market's had a good run and could be due for a few days' pullback. It's very thin and I'm not reading too much into it." Expect some support at the minor uptrend line at 4600 but developing 5-point reversal pattern could see key support at 4559 tested this week, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 0.4% at 20,267.70, in-line with weak trade in most Asian peers, caution after run-up lately with Sensex +13% since Sept. 1. HDFC (500010.BY) down 2.0% at 705.90, \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  (500900.BY) +1.6% at INR182.25. "Its a subdued start to the session. Financials after recent outperformance and select index heavyweights are starting to see some profit-booking," says local dealer. Adds, global cues, \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 2Q numbers, FY guidance due Friday to be watched for direction near term. Until, then tips trading to be rangebound; tips support at 20,150, resistance at 20,380. \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  (500180.BY) down 0.8% at INR2,366.95, \uc2 Larsen & Toubro\uc2  (500510.BY) down 0.9% at INR2,020.05. RIL (500325.BY) down 0.6% at INR1,061.90. But TCS (532540.BY) +1.4% at INR954.(gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds mixed as traders exit 10-year note in favor of 8.13% 2022 paper on growing view 10-year benchmark note likely near end of its on-the-run life, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.53 vs 98.67 last close, 8.13% 2022 bond at 100.54 vs 100.48 last close. Market currently favoring plays between notes rather than outright directional calls on bonds in view of data heavy week with both industrial output data (due later in session) and monthly WPI (due Friday) lined up, expected to shape medium-term direction for market. And with inclusion of 7.80% 2020 bond in weekly INR110 billion auction, market betting on note to soon hit informal outstanding cap of INR600 billion per bond, and then gradually drift into illiquidity. Still, broader directional punts likely to find favor again once industrial output print gives market something to play with. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0401 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY retreats below 82.00 again; now traders target pushing pair below 81.00 on back of view that U.S. Fed's additional easing in November quite likely, say senior dealers at major banks in Tokyo. "The dollar should keep falling, at least until November's FOMC," says Jun Kato, senior manager of investment at Shinkin Asset Management. Pair last 81.91. A dealer adds, EUR/JPY also falling due to weak Japan share prices, may fall to 113.00; last at 113.67.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares lower midday, with banks leading losses after Reuters reports PBOC temporarily hiked RRR on 6 Chinese lenders by 50bps. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2799.23 midday, with firm support at 2750. Still, "further downside for banks is limited as the RRR hike is only temporary and (it) only affects a small number of banks. It also suggests that a deposit rate hike is unlikely," says Li Lei at Gold State Securities. Adds, valuations in sector look attractive. Bank of China (601988.SH) down 1.5% at CNY3.37, ICBC (601398.SH) down 1.0% at CNY4.16. Shenzhen Composite Index +0.2% at 1214.57. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR higher as EUR correction, weak local stocks support pair in relatively thin early trade, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.52 vs 44.41 late Monday, trader tips pair to trade in 44.40-44.60 band in session. "With yesterday being a New York holiday, we didn't see too much by way of portfolio driven inflows, which could imply a bit of an upside for the pair," says trader. But near-term downside still likely for pair as foreign funds expected to chase massive $3 billion-$3.5 billion \uc2 Coal India Ltd\uc2 . share sale this month, which could help stall any rally in pair in current session. Key industrial output data due later in session also likely to shape pair's direction. (bijou.george@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 0031ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
