\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/USD Off Low But Cap Likely At 1.3920-Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 05:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD up as short-term players buy on earlier dip to intraday low at 1.3845, but any further rises would likely face resistance from profit-taking around 1.3920, says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at major Japan brokerage. Says pair's inability again Monday to hold its rise above 1.4000 has left investors expecting upward momentum to wane further this week, prompting them to look to lock in profits on any rises. Pair last 1.3876. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS down slightly as Reuters report that China temporarily raises reserve requirements on 6 banks to drain liquidity priced in. 1-year down 1 bp at 2.14%-2.17%, 5-year also down 1 bp at 2.97%-2.99%, 1-to-5-year spread stays at 77 bps. "The market digests that quickly as it in some way suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unwilling to hike interest rates now," says Beijing-based local bank trader. But notes swaps have further upside in near term as September CPI (due October 21) expected to rise further from 3.5% in August. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open flat with a negative bias, tracking weak Asia peers and a flat overnight close on Wall Street. "Indian markets are expected to open flat on subdued global cues and flat to negative opening among Asian peers. The Indian market ended a range-bound session modestly higher on Monday, tracking positive global cues," ICICI direct said in a client note. Another local brokerage says crucial trend-deciding level for Sensex at 20,350, and if benchmark trades above this level in first 3 minutes, then there may be a further rally up to 20,451-20,561 levels, but if it falls below 20,350, it may correct to 20,240-20,139 levels. Local dealer says short positions are building up steadily in the market as it's "overdue for correction," so tips narrow movement until negatives are triggered. Foreign funds were net buyers of a provisional INR8.10 billion worth of Indian stocks Monday, when Sensex closed +0.4% at 20,339.89. (john.kumar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares now up 0.4% at 3562.554 in light volume, off earlier low of 3542.934, led by bargain buying by institutional funds amid hopes for strong 9-month earnings (to be announced later in October), traders say; tip resistance at 3580. "But mild selling by foreign funds has limited gains of the main index," says trader at Reliance Securities. Foreigners net sellers of IDR24 billion worth of shares so far. Among gainers, food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) +2.1% at IDR4,975 on bargain buying after falling more than 5% in previous 2 sessions, car maker Astra (ASII.JK) up 1.7% at IDR57,350, while toll road operator Citra Marga (CMNP.JK) up 4.5% at IDR1,400. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] Merger & acquisition activity involving Singapore companies expected to increase as sellers now more willing to negotiate following improved stock valuations, says DBS Vickers. Notes buyers encouraged by better visibility of Singapore companies as worst of global crisis now over; "in addition to growth, Singapore companies are sitting on high net cash with 65% of them capable of generating free cash flow." Notes Singapore firms sitting on S$146.5 billion of net cash, thanks to capex discipline, capital restructuring in last 2 years. Says private equity firms now in fund raising mode, getting ready to invest from next year. Tips companies in consumer, oil & gas, technology sectors as potential targets, including Asiatravel.com (5AM.SG), Hi-P (H17.SG), Meiban (M24.SG), \uc2 Mermaid Maritime\uc2 , (DU4.SG), JES (EG0.SG), Biosensors (B20.SG), OKP (5CF.SG), Wheelock Properties (M35.SG). (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to stay in range despite losses in most regional markets as high yield stocks provide support, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson; "It appears there is quite a bit of interest in high yield stocks while our interest rates remain low and therefore that's helping to underpin our market somewhat." Property stocks in focus due to yield; AMP Office Trust (APT.NZ) +1.2% at NZ$0.82, Kiwi Income Property +1.0% at NZ$1.06, Property For Income +0.8% at NZ$1.22. Says Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +0.5% at NZ$8.10 as bargain-hunters pick up stock after recent weakness. NZX-50 last at 3233.41. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP surrendering early gains, with some banks cashing some gains on USD; pair last at 43.59 in moderate volume after settling at 43.60 late Monday and rising to 43.73 early today; support 43.30, resistance 43.75. Traders expect USD/PHP to mimic USD move vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly against EUR. Marcelo Ayes, senior vice president at Rizal Commercial Bank, says USD/regionals broadly correcting higher, helped by efforts of Asian central banks (which traders suspect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too fast; "What is being awaited now is the minutes of the Fed, which could show that (expected) QE2 (second round quantitative easing) won't be as large as the market expected. If that is confirmed, we may see the dollar rebound a bit." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely lower as inclusion of bonds at weekly INR110 billion bond auction likely to weigh on sentiment ahead of key data print later in session, says trader with foreign bank; tips 7.80% 2020 bond to open at around 98.50 vs 98.67 last close, tread with slight downward bias ahead of industrial output data mid-session. "A high industrial output will translate directly into higher expectations of a hike (at the next RBI policy meeting) on November 2, " says trader. Also, supply of INR40 billion of benchmark 2020 note at auction likely to feed supply concerns on note as it edges closer to end of on-the-run life cycle. India August industrial output growth likely decelerated to 9.9% on year, according to Dow Jones poll of 17 economists, vs 13.8% on-year growth in July. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open higher tracking bounce in global USD, then tread water ahead of key industrial output data later in session, says trader with foreign bank; tips pair to open around 44.50 vs 44.41 late Monday, trade in 44.30-44.65 band in session. "Unlike previous sessions, we don't really see much incremental U.S. dollar supply coming into the market near term. Industrial output will be what drives the pair today," says trader. India August industrial output growth likely decelerated to 9.9% on year, according to Dow Jones poll of 17 economists; compares with July's +13.8%. Foreign funds were net buyers of provisional INR8.10 billion worth of Indian stocks Monday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says copper prices being driven by macroeconomic factors rather than physical dynamics, noting 8% increase in copper inventory at Shanghai Futures Exchange over past week ignored. "While we expect that there could be a near-term pullback in prices after the strong rally over the past month, we expect that prices could continue higher into the New Year as the effects of QE keep industrial demand supported and investment demand potential grows as a driver." FOMC meeting minutes at 1800 GMT likely to be watched for further indications on quantitative easing. LME 3-month copper at $8,250/ton, down $35 with 1,043 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered Bank recommends taking profit on short USD/PHP via 3-month NDFs, with outright exit rate pegged at 43.77; "technicals, positioning and declining risk appetite suggest that USD/PHP may correct higher...fundamentally, we like this trade and will look to re-enter at better levels." Fundamentally, Standard Chartered has \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating of Overweight on PHP on growth. Spot last at 43.62. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 0.9% at 9504.39 midday with investor sentiment soured by firmer yen (USD/JPY at 82.09) vs Friday, and surprise FY profit forecast cut by \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO); decline in Chinese shares also weighing on market following Reuters report that reserve requirements on 6 big banks temporarily raised to drain cash from economy. "There is going to be a tug of war (in the stock market) between the dollar's weakness (vs the yen) and wariness about further \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market)," says Mizuho Securities equity strategist Tsuyoshi Segawa, pegging today's Nikkei support at 9450 (vs intraday low of 9487.43). 30/33 Topix subindexes lower; \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  down 7.7% at Y11,440. Exporters down on strong yen worries; \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) off 1.3% at Y2,950 and \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 0.8% at Y2,621. Consumer lenders and brokerages among worst performing sectors (off 2.2% and off 2.6%, respectively); \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) down 2.7% at Y437, Aiful (8515.TO) down 12% at Y58. \uc2 Central Japan Railway\uc2  (9022.TO) up 3.2% at Y647,000 on GS rating upgrade. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 2332ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
