 thin trade amid concerns that banks unlikely to cut interest rates from next week as government so far hasn't given any incentive to banks to do so, traders say. "Most players are hesitant to enter the market these days because of low liquidity, and they are likely to wait for companies to announce their third quarter results next week," says analyst at FPT Securities; adds, net foreign buying recently helping boost local sentiment. Foreign investors net buyers of VND3.58 billion of Vietnamese stocks Monday out of total VND638.5 billion traded. Tips key index in 457-463 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Reuters report citing unnamed sources that China authorities have temporarily raised the reserve ratio requirement for six biggest banks suggests lending accelerated in September, warranting further, yet modest, measures to curb credit growth, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics says; forecasts China end-September M2 +19.5% on year, new loans at CNY500 billion. Doesn't expect sharp tightening by Beijing in final months of 2010; government likely will rather try to strike a balance between insulating China's economy from global weakness and keeping domestic housing market from rebounding.(victoria.ruan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting slightly lower, last 0.7496 vs 0.7532 early in NZ; "It has only come off 20 or 30 points and in the scheme of things that's just a little bit of noise. It is probably a little bit of profit-taking," says Cliff Brown, clients' advisor for Bancorp Treasury in Auckland. Says Kiwi has been hovering around 0.7500-0.7550 for past day or two "and doesn't seem to have much momentum one way or the other." Focus now firmly on FOMC minutes later in global day with USD weakness remaining main driver of Kiwi strength. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.3% at 23,146.95, shallow pullback given 3.8% gain in past 6 sessions, while index overall up 23