cases in same period in 2009, according to analysis by Teikoku Databank. Of 31 cases, 11 went under because of losses related to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging, 11 due to reduction of orders received as strong JPY prods companies to move manufacturing bases overseas, 5 due to lower exports as strong JPY weighs on competitiveness of Japanese products overseas, 4 due to other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related losses. "The impact of the yen strength on business management is significant," Teikoku report says. "If the yen continues to strengthen, more companies, particularly export-related businesses, may go out of business." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last flat at 459.26 in thin trade amid concerns that banks unlikely to cut interest rates from next week as government so far hasn't given any incentive to banks to do so, traders say. "Most players are hesitant to enter the market these days because of low liquidity, and they are likely to wait for companies to announce their third quarter results next week," says analyst at FPT Securities; adds, net foreign buying recently helping boost local sentiment. Foreign investors net buyers of VND3.58 billion of Vietnamese stocks Monday out of total VND638.5 billion traded. Tips key index in 457-463 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Reuters report citing unnamed sources that China authorities have temporarily raised the reserve ratio requirement for six biggest banks suggests lending accelerated in September, warranting further, yet modest, measures to curb credit growth, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics says; forecasts China end-September M2 +19.5% on year, new loans at CNY500 billion. Doesn't expect sharp tightening by Beijing in final months of 2010; government likely will rather try to strike a balance between insulating China's economy from global weakness and keeping domestic housing market from rebound