\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Related Bankruptcies Rise - Teikoku\b0\par\par\uc2 1,488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 04:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] In sign of increasing trouble continued JPY strength causing for Japan economy, number of Japanese companies with debt more than Y10 million that went bankrupt due to impact from JPY strength rises by 14.8% to 31 cases in period from January to end of September 2010, compared to 27 cases in same period in 2009, according to analysis by Teikoku Databank. Of 31 cases, 11 went under because of losses related to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedging, 11 due to reduction of orders received as strong JPY prods companies to move manufacturing bases overseas, 5 due to lower exports as strong JPY weighs on competitiveness of Japanese products overseas, 4 due to other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -related losses. "The impact of the yen strength on business management is significant," Teikoku report says. "If the yen continues to strengthen, more companies, particularly export-related businesses, may go out of business." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last flat at 459.26 in thin trade amid concerns that banks unlikely to cut interest rates from next week as government so far hasn't given any incentive to banks to do so, traders say. "Most players are hesitant to enter the market these days because of low liquidity, and they are likely to wait for companies to announce their third quarter results next week," says analyst at FPT Securities; adds, net foreign buying recently helping boost local sentiment. Foreign investors net buyers of VND3.58 billion of Vietnamese stocks Monday out of total VND638.5 billion traded. Tips key index in 457-463 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Reuters report citing unnamed sources that China authorities have temporarily raised the reserve ratio requirement for six biggest banks suggests lending accelerated in September, warranting further, yet modest, measures to curb credit growth, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics says; forecasts China end-September M2 +19.5% on year, new loans at CNY500 billion. Doesn't expect sharp tightening by Beijing in final months of 2010; government likely will rather try to strike a balance between insulating China's economy from global weakness and keeping domestic housing market from rebounding.(victoria.ruan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting slightly lower, last 0.7496 vs 0.7532 early in NZ; "It has only come off 20 or 30 points and in the scheme of things that's just a little bit of noise. It is probably a little bit of profit-taking," says Cliff Brown, clients' advisor for Bancorp Treasury in Auckland. Says Kiwi has been hovering around 0.7500-0.7550 for past day or two "and doesn't seem to have much momentum one way or the other." Focus now firmly on FOMC minutes later in global day with USD weakness remaining main driver of Kiwi strength. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.3% at 23,146.95, shallow pullback given 3.8% gain in past 6 sessions, while index overall up 23 of last 27 sessions since September, "has become severely overbought... meaning a consolidation is overdue," says Taifook Securities. Cnooc (0883.HK) down 1.8% at HK$16.50 on profit-taking after 47.5% rally yesterday spurred by news of Texas shale project stake buy. China banks mostly lower after Reuters reports PBOC hikes RRR on 6 banks: ICBC (1398.HK) down 1.0% at HK$5.92, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) off 0.3% at HK$6.97. Market volume remains solid at HK$8.03 billion in first 15 minutes. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares down early led by metal stocks, as market takes breather after rising 7.3% in past 3 sessions. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2797.99, with strong support at 2750. "We expect the index to trade around 2800 for a period before rising further," says Hao Guomei at Huatai Securities. Adds banks unlikely to be sold heavily on media reports that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temporarily hike reserve requirement ratio by 50 bps for 6 Chinese banks, including four biggest ones. China Securities Journal report, citing unnamed sources, says move was prompted by robust lending at 6 banks; if true, strong loans growth likely to support their shares, says Hao. Among actives, Chalco (601600.SH) down 2.7% at CNY11.21, Inner Mongolia Baotou Steel Rare-Earth (600111.SH) down 1.5% at CNY75.88. Bank of China (601988.SH) down 0.9% at CNY3.39. Shenzhen index down 0.04% at 1211.69. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds, bond futures tad higher, supported by weak stocks (Kospi last down 1.0%), but sharp gains unlikely ahead of BOK's monetary policy meeting Thursday; investors failed to reach consensus on policy rate forecast, they are not sure whether \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike or keep key rate steady later this week. 3-year yield flat at 3.28%, 5-year yield down 2 bps at 3.63%, 10-year down 1 bp at 4.05%, December futures +10 ticks at 112.78. Yields likely to stay within recent range until rate decision later in week, says bond manager at local securities firm. Notes, 3-year bonds relatively weak today after outperforming yesterday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely lower, tracking falls in many Asian markets, and amid absence of fresh leads, traders say; tip support at 3530 after shares end up 0.1% yesterday at 3548.746. "Worries that foreign funds may continue to take profit may drive the main index lower," says trader at Lautandhana Securities; foreigners yesterday net sellers of IDR105 billion. Coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK), nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK), tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) may lead falls on profit-taking, lower commodity prices. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to remain in downtrend this week as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may face mounting difficulty conducting JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as such step could be seen as impediment to efforts of authorities in Europe, U.S., other countries to pressure China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise more rapidly, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japanese bank. After European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Rehn, others stepped up calls for stronger CNY over weekend at IMF, World Bank meetings, says pressure may mount further ahead of meeting of G20 finance ministers later this month, G20 summit next month. USD/JPY fell early Monday in Asian trade to fresh 15-year low at 81.37; dealer says fall came amid options-related selling by major European bank. Says if that level again broken, pair may fall to 81.00, with likely accumulation of stop-loss selling orders around 81.00, 80.50, 80.00 on further falls. Pair last at 82.13. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down early at 30.900 vs 30.998 yesterday as some exporters sold USD, says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 30.800-30.900 range for session. "TWD is unlikely to rise a lot today due to Taiex's weakness." Taiex last down 0.3%. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures extend overnight slide into Asia amid mixed regional equities, stronger USD vs EUR; EUR/USD at 1.3856 this morning vs 1.3877 late Monday in New York. Crude "still fighting resistance in the $83 range," says Mike Sander at Sander Capital Advisors; adds, "upsurge from $74 to $83 was on the back of an up-surging U.S. equities market (Friday), and with equities staying flat (Monday), oil has lost its fizzle." Says Nymex crude futures could easily fall back below $80/bbl; Nymex November crude down 61 cents at $81.60 on Globex after closing 0.5% cents lower overnight. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tad higher, lifted by investors' accumulation of mining, utility stocks; benchmark PSEi +0.01% at 4219.41 in moderate volume, with immediate support at 4200, resistance at 4240. Gainers, which swamping losers 56 to 29, led by \uc2 Atlas Consolidated Mining\uc2  (AT.PH) up 2.3% at PHP17.96, PLDT (TEL.PH) up 0.1% at PHP2,732. Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Markets, says market likely to consolidate in narrow band, possibly ending day lower as it did yesterday; "generally, our view is the market will consolidate for the meantime as investors await earnings results." Notes ongoing IPO of budget carrier Cebu Pacific - 2nd largest ever for bourse - may sap some liquidity from market; Cebu Pacific to list on October 26. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 2231ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
