\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Chances Of JPY  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Diminishing - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 04:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to remain in downtrend this week as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may face mounting difficulty conducting JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as such step could be seen as impediment to efforts of authorities in Europe, U.S., other countries to pressure China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise more rapidly, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japanese bank. After European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Rehn, others stepped up calls for stronger CNY over weekend at IMF, World Bank meetings, says pressure may mount further ahead of meeting of G20 finance ministers later this month, G20 summit next month. USD/JPY fell early Monday in Asian trade to fresh 15-year low at 81.37; dealer says fall came amid options-related selling by major European bank. Says if that level again broken, pair may fall to 81.00, with likely accumulation of stop-loss selling orders around 81.00, 80.50, 80.00 on further falls. Pair last at 82.13. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down early at 30.900 vs 30.998 yesterday as some exporters sold USD, says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 30.800-30.900 range for session. "TWD is unlikely to rise a lot today due to Taiex's weakness." Taiex last down 0.3%. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures extend overnight slide into Asia amid mixed regional equities, stronger USD vs EUR; EUR/USD at 1.3856 this morning vs 1.3877 late Monday in New York. Crude "still fighting resistance in the $83 range," says Mike Sander at Sander Capital Advisors; adds, "upsurge from $74 to $83 was on the back of an up-surging U.S. equities market (Friday), and with equities staying flat (Monday), oil has lost its fizzle." Says Nymex crude futures could easily fall back below $80/bbl; Nymex November crude down 61 cents at $81.60 on Globex after closing 0.5% cents lower overnight. (gurdeep.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tad higher, lifted by investors' accumulation of mining, utility stocks; benchmark PSEi +0.01% at 4219.41 in moderate volume, with immediate support at 4200, resistance at 4240. Gainers, which swamping losers 56 to 29, led by \uc2 Atlas Consolidated Mining\uc2  (AT.PH) up 2.3% at PHP17.96, PLDT (TEL.PH) up 0.1% at PHP2,732. Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Markets, says market likely to consolidate in narrow band, possibly ending day lower as it did yesterday; "generally, our view is the market will consolidate for the meantime as investors await earnings results." Notes ongoing IPO of budget carrier Cebu Pacific - 2nd largest ever for bourse - may sap some liquidity from market; Cebu Pacific to list on October 26. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD drifts lower, back to around 0.9800, sliding in a controlled fashion with profit-taking featuring ahead of FOMC minutes. Phil Burke, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says technical factor also contributing to weakness. Notes double top for pair above 0.9900 over last week could be a factor, while EUR/USD has also weakened in retreat, threatening a move toward 1.3700. Says local factors not having large impact on AUD with commodity prices, USD weakness, talk of return to QE in U.S. holding center stage. AUD/USD last at 0.9801 vs 0.9870 in late Asia yesterday. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to extend yesterday's advance but sharp gains unlikely as investors turn cautious after Deputy Finance Minister Pradit Phataraprasit said yesterday government plans to impose 15% withholding tax on foreign investors on interest and capital gains made from their investment in local bond market. Withholding tax on capital gains, if implemented, may lead to fund repatriation, though investors are not exactly sure "whether it (the measure) will be ready for the Cabinet's approval today and whether this will be limited to the bond market (only)," says analyst with Far East Securities Parin Kitchatornpitak. Resistance tipped at 980-986, support at 970. SET index yesterday ended up 1.5% at 977.85. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India August industrial output growth likely decelerated to 9.9% on year, hurt by expected slowdown in capital goods output that had reached unsustainably high levels, according to Dow Jones poll of 17 economists; industrial output growth in July was at 13.8%. "The impact of the series of rate hikes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now be visible and besides, last month's high IIP (index of industrial production) reading was perhaps an aberration that is unlikely to sustain," says N.R. Bhanumurthy, economist at state-run think-tank National Institute of Public Finance and Policy. Data due 0530 GMT. (anant.kala@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY higher as PBOC sets central parity higher following rebound in ICE Dollar Index overnight. Pair at 6.6746 vs 6.6678 last close. Central parity set at 6.6775 vs 6.6732 yesterday, dollar index at 77.590 vs 77.500 late yesterday in NY. "A short-term rebound doesn't change the big trend that the dollar will be weaker," says Shanghai-based European bank trader. Tips pair has support at 6.6500 for next 2 weeks. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares down 0.3% at 8155.21 early, tracking regional weakness, also weighed by Reuters report that China temporarily raises 6 banks' reserve requirements yesterday to cool the economy, says Taiwan International Securities Assistant Vice President Andrew Teng. Tips Taiex in 8100-8200 range for session. "We see strong support at 8100 on bargain hunting by foreign funds, betting on continued appreciation of TWD." TSMC (2330.TW) last down 1.0% at NT$60.90 due to concerns that weak USD may hurt its export sales. HTC (2498.TW) down 1.0% at NT$708.00 on profit taking, but Teng says positive 4Q outlook remains intact. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains sharp gains with offshore players seen covering USD shorts, amid Kospi's 0.9% fall; pair now up at 1,126.2 vs 1,116.7 late yesterday in Seoul, with traders putting initial cap at 1,128, then at 1,130. "There seems to be an all-around short-dollar covering against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for today," with Japanese finance minister's comments earlier in day that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take "decisive" action, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary, to stem JPY's rise, again spurring concerns \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may heat up for Asian peers, says local bank trader. But notes, if pair fails to breach 1,130 later, it may scoot back down to low-1,120s. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI little changed at 1487.29, supported by gains in plantation stocks following rally in palm oil prices to 26-month highs; broader market mixed, with advancers, decliners about equal. Dealers, analysts say KLCI may retreat further in coming hours amid losses in regional markets weigh, but any pullback likely short-lived amid expectations of good news from government's 2011 budget to be unveiled Friday. "Infrastructure and construction stocks should shine for the rest of this week given the positive anticipation ahead of this Friday's Budget announcement," TA Research says. Dealers tip support for KLCI at 1484 (yesterday's low), with resistance at 1495. Among gainers, palm oil producer \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) +1.1% at MYR5.64, \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) +0.7% at MYR8.76; among decliners, AMMB (1015.KU) down 1.0% at MYR5.91. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB tad higher at 30.07 vs 30.05 late yesterday on concerns about possible fund repatriation after Deputy Finance Minister Pradit Phataraprasit said yesterday government plans to impose 15% withholding tax on foreign investors on interest and capital gains made from their investment in local bond market. "Investors don't want to take big positions right now as they want to digest details first. I think it may slow the capital inflows, but only in the near term (If the tax measures are implemented)," says Bangkok-based dealer. Reckons pair still biased down; says Asia remains preferred investment destination thus capital inflows will continue. Notes Phataraprasit said Monday he wasn't sure if tax measure will be ready for Cabinet's meeting approval today. Resistance tipped at 30.12, support at 30.03. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 2209ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
