\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:China Shares May Consolidate;Shanghai Support 2750\b0\par\par\uc2 1,597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 03:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate with downward bias, taking breather after rising 7.3% in past 3 sessions, also on news that reserve requirements on 6 big banks temporarily raised to drain cash from economy. Shanghai Composite Index may trade around 2750, after yesterday ending up 2.5% at 2806.94. "The index has risen too quickly, so there should be some pullback today. Steel, cement and property stocks are likely to fall back," said Jacky Zhang from Capital Securities. Shenzhen Composite Index ends +0.9% at 1212.14. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect very limited moves in Singapore government bond yields in Asian session. U.S. bond market shut yesterday for holiday, while flat Wall Street session likely means subdued moves in regional stocks. Likely to take cues from FOMC minutes due in U.S. session. Yesterday, 2-year yield ends flat at 0.40%, 10-year yield flat at 1.96%. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold price will surge in coming year, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , which today lifted its gold price forecasts to $1,400/oz, $1,525/oz, $1,650/oz on a 3, 6, 12 month horizon, respectively. Spot gold last bid at $1,351, down $2.90 from late New York level. Says "with U.S. real interest rates pushing lower off the slowdown in the pace of the U.S. economic recovery and the growing prospect of another round of quantitative easing, we expect gold prices to continue to climb," until U.S. monetary policy begins to tighten. U.S. Fed's expected to announce a return to quantitative easing measures as early as November, in its open markets committee meeting, according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , which also cites gold demand from ETFs, government central banks, which continue to exert large influence on prices. Adds "we see the risks as skewed to stronger demand then we currently expect and in turn see upside risk to our gold price forecast." (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped higher, taking cues from modest USD gains in region; pair may rise to 43.75 initial resistance after settling at 43.60 late yesterday, with support at 43.30; next resistance pegged at 43.85. "There wasn't any strong lead overnight and the euro topped out intraday, so we'll likely see a continuation (today) of the dollar's advance" vs PHP, says Manila-based trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.7% at 4666.4, tracking AUD/USD, which is down 0.5% at 0.9815 versus 0.9860 when the S&P/ASX 200 closed trading Monday. Potential profit taking from offshore investors is a concern as the AUD/USD turns down from post-float high of 0.9918. Traders are increasingly coming to the conclusion that the market has factored in potential US$500 billion quantitative easing announcement by the U.S. Fed on Nov. 3. "I think the market will pre-empt a sell on fact reaction by selling off before the event," says a trader who declines to be named. Others feel the Fed might try to impress the market with US$1 trillion worth of QE, although there's plenty of skepticism about the longer-term merits of QE2. "Certainly QE2 won't have the same magnitude of effect as QE1 and I suspect QE2 will have to be much bigger than US$500 billion to 'impress' the market," says Wesley Legrand, broker at Grand Private Equities. "Short-sighted central bankers and vested-interest politicians have no choice but to defer the inevitable pain as long as possible as they try to cling to power, so there is now a 'race to the bottom' as most countries of the world are committed to printing as much money as they can to try and devalue their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but each 'sugar hit' is having less and less effect...faith in the global fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is eroding such that even though the U.S. feels like it's in deflation because there is a shortage of money, they are actually very close to hyperinflation, which is a confidence event, unlike inflation. Unfortunately Australia is just a cork in the financial ocean, so fingers crossed for China." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to trade lower, may hit 8130 vs yesterday's close 8176.76 (down 0.8%), but technical rebound probably will soon kick in, says KGI Securities analyst Scott Hsu. "I would say the rebound will be very moderate. Unless there are new market catalysts, Taiwan stocks will probably remain in such a consolidating mode for some more time." Market trading volume continues to contract, reflecting that investors lack direction; volume yesterday at NT$96.15 billion, below NT$100 billion-mark eyed for weeks. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD likely to trade off recent lows in Asia session, some profit-taking on strong SGD after heavy buying of recent weeks. "The weak (U.S.) dollar (theme) is still in play but at this point it's harder to push especially after the aggressive selling in September," trader says. Expects support for pair around 1.3045, resistance at 1.3100; last 1.3089.(samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Although JGB market has already digested BOJ's drastic easing announced last week, "yields could still show some downside amid lingering expectations for further easing, including trends in (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign) exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; adds "with a 30-year auction set to take place on Thursday, the main focus (this week) will be whether the rapid steepening seen from the second half of last week shows further acceleration." 10-year cash JGB yield currently down 1.5 bps at 0.850%, while 20-year yield down 1.0 bp at 1.690%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi flat, last at 1889.92 as foreigners turn net sellers in early session. "There's some caution in the market ahead of Intel's third-quarter earnings and the FOMC meeting minutes (later in the global day)," says Shinhan Investment analyst Lee Sun-yup. But adds, major shifts unlikely as there's confluence of profit-taking and bargain hunting in market; tips 1894-1903 band for day. Automakers falling broadly amid worries that their profitability may be hurt by KRW's strength; \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 0.9% at KRW161,000, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) 0.1% lower at KRW39,650. Banks broadly higher on 3Q earnings hopes; \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) +2.7% at KRW15,450, \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) +2.8% at KRW37,200. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW breaches 1,120-level to trade higher at 1,126.5 vs 1,116.7 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking modest USD gains vs some majors overnight, with investors seen taking profits on riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (including KRW) after recent rallies, traders say. Local bank trader tips resistance in 1,127-1,128 range; says breach of 1,128 cap to 1,130-mark could trigger another bout of short covering on USD, giving pair another leg up, but that seems unlikely for now. "It's about time we saw some (upward) correction in the pair. But I don't believe the overall (downward) bias has been reversed yet," he says. Tips support at 1,120. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.1% at 9594.52 after opening 0.6% higher, then falling into negative territory. USD/JPY slightly weaker (pair now 82.28 after sliding on Monday to fresh 15-year low of 81.37) following Japanese Finance Minister Noda's reiteration government would take "decisive" action, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary. Index range for today pegged at 9500-9700, according to analysts. "It seems like Noda was able to obtain a certain level of understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (at the G-7 meeting)," says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. "If the government does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event the dollar falls below 82 (again), the dollar may break below 80 yen." Projects Nikkei to rise above 9700 resistance if prospective \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurs USD/JPY rebound to 84-85 level. 18/33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 Central Japan Railway\uc2  (9022.TO) up 2.4% at Y642,000 on GS rating upgrade. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) off 5.3% to Y11,730 after cutting its FY profit forecast. \uc2 Nippon Paper Group\uc2  (3893.TO) down 1.6% at Y2,151 on Nikkei's grim FY1H pre-earnings report. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to continue to slide; local trader tips pair to hit 30.600 early, with next near-term support at 30.300. Pair yesterday ends at 30.998 vs 31.095, after hitting new 2-year low of 30.645, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying about US$20 million to curb TWD rise, other traders say yesterday. "The trend of inflows is very firm and it doesn't look like the flows will abate in the near future." (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 2102ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal33