\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Singapore Govt Bond Yield Moves Likely Limited\b0\par\par\uc2 1,618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 02:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect very limited moves in Singapore government bond yields in Asian session. U.S. bond market shut yesterday for holiday, while flat Wall Street session likely means subdued moves in regional stocks. Likely to take cues from FOMC minutes due in U.S. session. Yesterday, 2-year yield ends flat at 0.40%, 10-year yield flat at 1.96%. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold price will surge in coming year, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , which today lifted its gold price forecasts to $1,400/oz, $1,525/oz, $1,650/oz on a 3, 6, 12 month horizon, respectively. Spot gold last bid at $1,351, down $2.90 from late New York level. Says "with U.S. real interest rates pushing lower off the slowdown in the pace of the U.S. economic recovery and the growing prospect of another round of quantitative easing, we expect gold prices to continue to climb," until U.S. monetary policy begins to tighten. U.S. Fed's expected to announce a return to quantitative easing measures as early as November, in its open markets committee meeting, according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , which also cites gold demand from ETFs, government central banks, which continue to exert large influence on prices. Adds "we see the risks as skewed to stronger demand then we currently expect and in turn see upside risk to our gold price forecast." (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped higher, taking cues from modest USD gains in region; pair may rise to 43.75 initial resistance after settling at 43.60 late yesterday, with support at 43.30; next resistance pegged at 43.85. "There wasn't any strong lead overnight and the euro topped out intraday, so we'll likely see a continuation (today) of the dollar's advance" vs PHP, says Manila-based trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.7% at 4666.4, tracking AUD/USD, which is down 0.5% at 0.9815 versus 0.9860 when the S&P/ASX 200 closed trading Monday. Potential profit taking from offshore investors is a concern as the AUD/USD turns down from post-float high of 0.9918. Traders are increasingly coming to the conclusion that the market has factored in potential US$500 billion quantitative easing announcement by the U.S. Fed on Nov. 3. "I think the market will pre-empt a sell on fact reaction by selling off before the event," says a trader who declines to be named. Others feel the Fed might try to impress the market with US$1 trillion worth of QE, although there's plenty of skepticism about the longer-term merits of QE2. "Certainly QE2 won't have the same magnitude of effect as QE1 and I suspect QE2 will have to be much bigger than US$500 billion to 'impress' the market," says Wesley Legrand, broker at Grand Private Equities. "Short-sighted central bankers and vested-interest politicians have no choice but to defer the inevitable pain as long as possible as they try to cling to power, so there is now a 'race to the bottom' as most countries of the world are committed to printing as much money as they can to try and devalue their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but each 'sugar hit' is having less and less effect...faith in the global fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is eroding such that even though the U.S. feels like it's in deflation because there is a shortage of money, they are actually very close to hyperinflation, which is a confidence event, unlike inflation. Unfortunately Australia is just a cork in the financial ocean, so fingers crossed 