\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/PHP Tipped Up As USD Rebounds; 43.75 May Cap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 02:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped higher, taking cues from modest USD gains in region; pair may rise to 43.75 initial resistance after settling at 43.60 late yesterday, with support at 43.30; next resistance pegged at 43.85. "There wasn't any strong lead overnight and the euro topped out intraday, so we'll likely see a continuation (today) of the dollar's advance" vs PHP, says Manila-based trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.7% at 4666.4, tracking AUD/USD, which is down 0.5% at 0.9815 versus 0.9860 when the S&P/ASX 200 closed trading Monday. Potential profit taking from offshore investors is a concern as the AUD/USD turns down from post-float high of 0.9918. Traders are increasingly coming to the conclusion that the market has factored in potential US$500 billion quantitative easing announcement by the U.S. Fed on Nov. 3. "I think the market will pre-empt a sell on fact reaction by selling off before the event," says a trader who declines to be named. Others feel the Fed might try to impress the market with US$1 trillion worth of QE, although there's plenty of skepticism about the longer-term merits of QE2. "Certainly QE2 won't have the same magnitude of effect as QE1 and I suspect QE2 will have to be much bigger than US$500 billion to 'impress' the market," says Wesley Legrand, broker at Grand Private Equities. "Short-sighted central bankers and vested-interest politicians have no choice but to defer the inevitable pain as long as possible as they try to cling to power, so there is now a 'race to the bottom' as most countries of the world are committed to printing as much money as they can to try and devalue their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but each 'sugar hit' is having less and less effect...faith in the global fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is eroding such that even though the U.S. feels like it's in deflation because there is a shortage of money, they are actually very close to hyperinflation, which is a confidence event, unlike inflation. Unfortunately Australia is just a cork in the financial ocean, so fingers crossed for China." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to trade lower, may hit 8130 vs yesterday's close 8176.76 (down 0.8%), but technical rebound probably will soon kick in, says KGI Securities analyst Scott Hsu. "I would say the rebound will be very moderate. Unless there are new market catalysts, Taiwan stocks will probably remain in such a consolidating mode for some more time." Market trading volume continues to contract, reflecting that investors lack direction; volume yesterday at NT$96.15 billion, below NT$100 billion-mark eyed for weeks. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD likely to trade off recent lows in Asia session, some profit-taking on strong SGD after heavy buying of recent weeks. "The weak (U.S.) dollar (theme) is still in play but at this point it's harder to push especially after the aggressive selling in September," trader says. Expects support for pair around 1.3045, resistance at 1.3100; last 1.3089.(samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Although JGB market has already digested BOJ's drastic easing announced last week, "yields could still show some downside amid lingering expectations for further easing, including trends in (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign) exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; adds "wi