e-bound rest of the day in subdued trade as participants maintain cautious stance awaiting clearer outlook for global economy, release of 2011 government budget Friday, dealers say. Pair tipped to stay in 3.0900-3.1150 range near-term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open slightly higher on JPY uptrend, growing speculation over further easing in Japan, U.S.; contract currently +0.09 at 144.04. Until it becomes clear whether Fed will take additional easing steps at November meeting, USD/JPY's falling trend expected to continue and keep supporting JGBs, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Adds although global economy slowing down, there're no fiscal, monetary policies which have immediate impact - lending downward pressure on JGB yields. 10-year cash bonds yet to change hands (yield at 0.855% at Friday's close). (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0004 GMT [Dow Jones] Flat session on Wall Street suggests Singapore stocks generally not expected to make much headway, although pockets of strength could still surface given positive newsflow from some companies. Resistance for STI, +0.3% at 3163.41 yesterday, expected at year-to-date 3197 high; support tipped at 3132 (Oct. 1 high). Keppel group may be in focus, with \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) winning S$341 million waste-recycling contract, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG) reaping S$321 million gain from selling its one-third stake in Singapore's Marina Bay Financial Centre to \uc2 K-REIT Asia\uc2  (K71U.SG), which in turn will sell 2 office properties to developer for S$573 million. Keppel +2.3% at S$9.63 yesterday, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  +0.2% at S$4.11, K-REIT +1.5% at S$1.37. Asiasons WFG Securities says strong liquidity, expectations of more quantitative easing could continue to support shares, but tips effect as short term "as we experienced in the past year;" expects high risk of sharp correction as economic conditions, especially in U.S., not showing sustained improvement. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound as investors continue to bide time until key events later this week - auction of September 2017 MGS Thursday, release of government's 2011 budget Friday; until then, dealers say trading activity, which has slowed since last week, likely to remain subdued. Last week, goverment bond trading volume fell by 34% from previous week to MYR7.6 billion. "Expectations of sustained low interest rates are supportive for bonds, but on the other hand, players are of the view that there may not be much more upside room left following strong gains over the past few months," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end steady to a tad higher, with August 2015 +1 bp at 3.26%, September 2017 unchanged at 3.55%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.66%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises as investors cover short positions following comments by Finance Minister Noda, who says G-7 authorities in D.C. over weekend showed some understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Tomohiro Nishida, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust And Banking. Comments reinforce view, which grew after USD/JPY fell yesterday to fresh 15-year low at 81.37, that Tokyo may soon step into markets again to curb JPY strength. Nishida adds USD/JPY may rise to 83.00 by end of global day vs last 82.29. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.3% with volume light as market looks for cues, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "We will likely follow Australia's lead. If they stay down, we will peg back slightly." Says \uc2 Goodman Property Trust\uc2  (GMT.NZ) +2.0% at NZ$1.01 after announcing, in exchange filing yesterday, \uc2 Downer EDI\uc2  had committed to purpose-built facility at its Glassworks development estate; "We all know Goodman is well run, we all know they've got development land and that they only build it when they have tenants sorted out so it's just a continuation of their business model and that could be viewed positively." Adds, all listed property companies higher as "the sector has been getting a good lift over the last few weeks;" Kiwi Income Property (KIP.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$1.07. Also adds, \uc2 Pike River Coal\uc2  (PRC.NZ) +1.7% at NZ$1.22 after saying yesterday it completed installation of its hydro monitor system; "It is also getting a lift off what has happened in Australia with resources (being strong) and investors are valuing up what Pike could be worth when it gets to full production." NZX-50 last at 3242.83. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.4% at 4679.4, hits 4673.9 in early trading. Lack of strength on Wall Street appears to have triggered profit taking after S&P/ASX 200 failed to close above technical resistance near 4700 yesterday. Mining and energy stocks reversing some of Monday's gains. In materials sector, BHP (BHP.AU) down 1.0%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 1.1%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) down 1.3%, Fortescue (FMG.AU) down 1.1%, while in energy sector, Woodside (WPL.AU) down 0.9%, Santos (STO.AU) down 0.9%. Financials outperforming, with major banks near flat, although Macquarie (MQG.AU) remains weak, down 0.9% amid caution ahead of \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  results on Wednesday. "You get the feeling the market is waiting for U.S. corporate earnings, economic data and the November FOMC meeting," says IG Markets Strategist Cameron Peacock. "I expect some heat to come out of resources in the short term." Other traders are focussing on reports suggesting US$500 billon worth of U.S. quantitative easing has been factored in by financial markets. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall as broad-based USD weakness persists amid expectations of further Fed quantitative easing in early November, but any falls likely to run aground around 81.80 due to view Tokyo authorities could soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to curb sharper drops, says senior dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. USD/JPY fell yesterday to fresh 15-year low at 81.37. But says even if Japan does step into market again, that may cause only brief bump, failing to prevent further medium-term USD/JPY weakness. For that reason, says breach of all-time low of 79.75, marked in April 1995, is just a matter of time. Adds pair's topside likely around 82.30 for rest of global day, as Japan exporter, other players may sell on any rises; last 82.18. EUR/USD biased down as pair loses momentum after failing recently to break key technical level at 1.4050; tips trade in 1.3800-1.3900 band vs last 1.3872. EUR/JPY biased down in 113.30-114.30 range vs last 113.91. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 2040ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
