\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: JGB Futures Open Tad Up On JPY Uptrend\b0\par\par\uc2 1,698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 02:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open slightly higher on JPY uptrend, growing speculation over further easing in Japan, U.S.; contract currently +0.09 at 144.04. Until it becomes clear whether Fed will take additional easing steps at November meeting, USD/JPY's falling trend expected to continue and keep supporting JGBs, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Adds although global economy slowing down, there're no fiscal, monetary policies which have immediate impact - lending downward pressure on JGB yields. 10-year cash bonds yet to change hands (yield at 0.855% at Friday's close). (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0004 GMT [Dow Jones] Flat session on Wall Street suggests Singapore stocks generally not expected to make much headway, although pockets of strength could still surface given positive newsflow from some companies. Resistance for STI, +0.3% at 3163.41 yesterday, expected at year-to-date 3197 high; support tipped at 3132 (Oct. 1 high). Keppel group may be in focus, with \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) winning S$341 million waste-recycling contract, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG) reaping S$321 million gain from selling its one-third stake in Singapore's Marina Bay Financial Centre to \uc2 K-REIT Asia\uc2  (K71U.SG), which in turn will sell 2 office properties to developer for S$573 million. Keppel +2.3% at S$9.63 yesterday, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  +0.2% at S$4.11, K-REIT +1.5% at S$1.37. Asiasons WFG Securities says strong liquidity, expectations of more quantitative easing could continue to support shares, but tips effect as short term "as we experienced in the past year;" expects high risk of sharp correction as economic conditions, especially in U.S., not showing sustained improvement. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound as investors continue to bide time until key events later this week - auction of September 2017 MGS Thursday, release of government's 2011 budget Friday; until then, dealers say trading activity, which has slowed since last week, likely to remain subdued. Last week, goverment bond trading volume fell by 34% from previous week to MYR7.6 billion. "Expectations of sustained low interest rates are supportive for bonds, but on the other hand, players are of the view that there may not be much more upside room left following strong gains over the past few months," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end steady to a tad higher, with August 2015 +1 bp at 3.26%, September 2017 unchanged at 3.55%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.66%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises as investors cover short positions following comments by Finance Minister Noda, who says G-7 authorities in D.C. over weekend showed some understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Tomohiro Nishida, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust And Banking. Comments reinforce view, which grew after USD/JPY fell yesterday to fresh 15-year low at 81.37, that Tokyo may soon step into markets again to curb JPY strength. Nishida adds USD/JPY may rise to 83.00 by end of global day vs last 82.29. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.3% with volume light as market looks for cues, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "We will likely follow Australia's lead. If they stay down, we will peg back slightly." Says \uc2