\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Malaysia Govt Bonds Likely Steady;Thu Auction Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 02:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound as investors continue to bide time until key events later this week - auction of September 2017 MGS Thursday, release of government's 2011 budget Friday; until then, dealers say trading activity, which has slowed since last week, likely to remain subdued. Last week, goverment bond trading volume fell by 34% from previous week to MYR7.6 billion. "Expectations of sustained low interest rates are supportive for bonds, but on the other hand, players are of the view that there may not be much more upside room left following strong gains over the past few months," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end steady to a tad higher, with August 2015 +1 bp at 3.26%, September 2017 unchanged at 3.55%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.66%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises as investors cover short positions following comments by Finance Minister Noda, who says G-7 authorities in D.C. over weekend showed some understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Tomohiro Nishida, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust And Banking. Comments reinforce view, which grew after USD/JPY fell yesterday to fresh 15-year low at 81.37, that Tokyo may soon step into markets again to curb JPY strength. Nishida adds USD/JPY may rise to 83.00 by end of global day vs last 82.29. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.3% with volume light as market looks for cues, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "We will likely follow Australia's lead. If they stay down, we will peg back slightly." Says \uc2 Goodman Property Trust\uc2  (GMT.NZ) +2.0% at NZ$1.01 after announcing, in exchange filing yesterday, \uc2 Downer EDI\uc2  had committed to purpose-built facility at its Glassworks development estate; "We all know Goodman is well run, we all know they've got development land and that they only build it when they have tenants sorted out so it's just a continuation of their business model and that could be viewed positively." Adds, all listed property companies higher as "the sector has been getting a good lift over the last few weeks;" Kiwi Income Property (KIP.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$1.07. Also adds, \uc2 Pike River Coal\uc2  (PRC.NZ) +1.7% at NZ$1.22 after saying yesterday it completed installation of its hydro monitor system; "It is also getting a lift off what has happened in Australia with resources (being strong) and investors are valuing up what Pike could be worth when it gets to full production." NZX-50 last at 3242.83. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.4% at 4679.4, hits 4673.9 in early trading. Lack of strength on Wall Street appears to have triggered profit taking after S&P/ASX 200 failed to close above technical resistance near 4700 yesterday. Mining and energy stocks reversing some of Monday's gains. In materials sector, BHP (BHP.AU) down 1.0%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 1.1%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) down 1.3%, Fortescue (FMG.AU) down 1.1%, while in energy sector, Woodside (WPL.AU) down 0.9%, Santos (STO.AU) down 0.9%. Financials outperforming, with major banks near flat, although Macquarie (MQG.AU) remains weak, down 0.9% amid caution ahead of \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  results on Wednesday. "You get the feeling the market is waiting for U.S. corporate earnings, economic data and the November FOMC meeting," says IG Markets Strategist Cameron Peacock. "I expect some heat to come out of resources in the short term." Other traders are focussing on reports suggesting US$500 billon worth of U.S. quantitative easing has been factored in by financial markets. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall as broad-based USD weakness persists amid expectations of further Fed quantitative easing in early November, but any falls likely to run aground around 81.80 due to view Tokyo authorities could soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to curb sharper drops, says senior dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. USD/JPY fell yesterday to fresh 15-year low at 81.37. But says even if Japan does step into market again, that may cause only brief bump, failing to prevent further medium-term USD/JPY weakness. For that reason, says breach of all-time low of 79.75, marked in April 1995, is just a matter of time. Adds pair's topside likely around 82.30 for rest of global day, as Japan exporter, other players may sell on any rises; last 82.18. EUR/USD biased down as pair loses momentum after failing recently to break key technical level at 1.4050; tips trade in 1.3800-1.3900 band vs last 1.3872. EUR/JPY biased down in 113.30-114.30 range vs last 113.91. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to extend consolidation phase for now after settling down 45 cents yesterday at $82.21/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics bearish at overbought level. Support at $80.30 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to $79.70 (Oct. 1 low), then $78.04 (previous cap set Sept. 13), $77.92 (200-day moving average) and $77.17 (55-day moving average). Resistance at $83.50 (yesterday's high); breach would target $84.43 (5-month high set Thursday) - above which would open way up to psychological round-numbered levels of $85.00, $86.00, $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). November crude last down 14 cents at $82.07/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may rise vs 1,116.7 late yesterday in Seoul with pair likely to take breather following recent steep falls amid lack of momentum to push pair beyond 5-month lows, traders say. Spot tipped to open up after 1-month NDFs last end around 1,121 in NY. Local bank trader expects pair to flirt around 1,120 level. Says corporate demand poised to grab USD on dips providing firm support, while market growing increasingly wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 1,110. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi may rise on expectations for strong 3Q corporate earnings results, ongoing KRW strength despite uninspiring performance from U.S. markets overnight. "Market liquidity is still good and there hasn't been any signals that foreign investors are switching their positions," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Lee Jae-mahn, who tips 1910 resistance for day; foreign inflows have boosted domestic market in recent sessions. Securities stocks like \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE), Woori Investment (005940.SE) may rise as strong trading activity lift their earnings, while automakers like \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) may also gain on expectations for strong 3Q earnings. Domestic earnings season kicks off this week. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 2.1 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 4th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 18.8 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 16.7 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 3.5% at HK$4.791 billion yesterday, 5.0% of total market volume vs Friday's 5.7%, suggesting bears still deterred by market strength, with HSI in 6-day winning streak, up 23 of last 27 sessions overall. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) led with HK$436.39 million, followed by \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$273.21 million, HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$272.80 million. China Life (2628.HK), Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$255.66 million, HK$212.84 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise despite weaker USD/JPY (now 82.05), due to speculation about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese government; investors also relieved about relative firmness in U.S. stocks despite weaker-than-expected September jobs data released on Friday. Nikkei range for today: 9500-9700 vs 9588.88 (down 1.0%) Friday (Japanese markets closed Monday for public holiday). "Since there was no (open) criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (September 15) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the G-7 (meeting), the government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the dollar falls (sharply and stays under 82 yen)," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Adds investors turning attention to U.S. earnings season with Intel (INTC) expected to report 3Q results later in global trading day. Commodity-linked shares likely to trade firmly after gold futures rose to fresh record high on Monday. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9625 yesterday vs Osaka close at 9580 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher, as JPY's sharp rise increase worries over export-driven Japan economy, boosts demand for safe-haven government bonds; weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data released Friday also raises expectations for additional easing from Fed, which could pressure down JGB yields, analysts say; \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano notes that even during volatile trade last week, dip-buying demand was solid when 10-year yield above 0.9%. Adds, however, oversupply concerns linger in superlong zone, expects yield curve to be biased toward steepening. Lead December JGB futures tipped in 143.81-144.22 band vs 143.95 last close on Friday, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.845%-0.870% vs 0.855%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 2002ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
