\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Rises Ahead Of FOMC Minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 11:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The dollar rallied and the euro fell further Tuesday as the market waited for the minutes of the latest Federal Open Market Committee meeting later in the day.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, however, the continued lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged market participants to push the dollar lower against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment is being dominated by speculation that the FOMC minutes will show that any plans for further quantitative easing by the Federal Reserve will prove smaller than expected.\par \par \uc2 If that is the case, the dollar, which reflects a much more extreme level of easing, could bounce back. One analyst reckoned that market participants had expected the Fed to increase its balance sheet by as much as $1 trillion,.\par \par \uc2 "In our view a very aggressive quantitative easing response from the Fed is currently priced into the dollar. Hence, minutes might disappoint the market, as the Fed may not be able to live to up to expectations. If that is the case, euro/dollar will most likely be pushed lower tonight," said Signe Roed-Frederiksen, a senior analyst with \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the dollar is benefiting from hopes that the Fed won't be as aggressive as first thought, the euro is suffering from a combination of lower risk appetite as well as from general profit-taking after its failure to break up over $1.40.\par \par \uc2 The decline in market appetite for risk was evident in Asian trading, with stock markets on the slide and the Nikkei losing as much as 2.1% by the close.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro has fallen back:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4723"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4723}}}\par \par \uc2 Despite its gains elsewhere, the dollar was pushed lower against the yen as the market was once again keen to test \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  resolve.\par \par \uc2 Although the dollar has fallen through mid-September's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level just under Y83, there is still no sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to launch a new exercise.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's latest comments had little impact on market sentiment. The minister repeated that he is watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with great interest and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to take decisive steps, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if needed.\par \par \uc2 The pound fell back sharply after Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee member David Miles admitted that the bank may yet come to use more quantitative easing. However, he did add that the future direction of monetary policy isn't clear.\par \par \uc2 Sterling was under pressure earlier Tuesday after data showed that U.K. inflation was 3.1% in September, unchanged, as expected, from August's level.\par \par \uc2 This followed earlier data from RICS that showed house prices continued to decline and a new quarterly economic survey from the BCC that showed a gloomy outlook for business.\par \par \uc2 Midmorning, the euro had fallen back to $1.3787 from $1.3877 late Monday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell to Y113.12 from Y113.97, while the dollar fell to Y82.06 from Y82.13.\par \par \uc2 The pound declined to $1.5806 from $1.5873, while the dollar rose to CHF0.9721 from CHF0.9647.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 12-10-10 0933GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101012e6ac000vt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
