\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZX-50 +0.4%; May Keep Gains On Aussie Lead - \uc2 UBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 01:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2347 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 last +0.4%, likely to hold onto gains as Australia rebounds after yesterday's weakness, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  NZ managing director Campbell Stuart; "It has been extremely quiet on the market since the open, there is not a lot of local news around, so we're looking for some momentum from Australia." Sentiment buoying Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +1.4% at NZ$8.20, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Contact Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  +4.0% at NZ$5.71. Adds Telecom, (TEL.NZ) +1.5% at NZ$2.05, news that government approved variations to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  separation undertakings to allow greater time for delivering new technologies was not huge surprise for analysts. Says retailers weaker, although on light volumes, after strong run last week; Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) down 1.1% at NZ$1.88, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hallenstein Glasson\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HLG.NZ) down 0.9% at NZ$4.31, Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) down 1.6% at NZ$1.86. NZX-50 last 3242.77. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may open higher taking cues from gains on Wall Street overnight; "as the U.S. is expected to take further action to promote quantitative easing and Nasdaq rose 0.7%, South Korean shares, particularly technology stocks, will likely get a boost," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; tips 1885 cap for day. Expects techs to trade in positive territory, helping broader market. Posco (005490.SE) expected to trade in negative territory after reporting lower-than-expected 3Q earnings after market close yesterday, but no sharp fall likely as results widely expected; Posco last ends down 2.3% at KRW519,000. Kospi ends down 1.2% at 1868.04 yesterday. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to fall vs 1,131.5 late yesterday in Seoul after USD loses ground overnight as Fed minutes from latest meeting indicated committee members believe new steps could be needed to stimulate growth. Spot tipped to fall at market open after 1-month NDFs last end around 1,120.5 in NY. But after an initial jump down, pair may tread water for few hours as players likely to remain wary over possible USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. Also, market participants unlikely to make aggressive bets ahead of BOK's rate setting monetary policy meeting tomorrow, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,115-1,130 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +15.6% at HK$5.539 billion yesterday, 6.7% of total market volume vs Monday's 5.0%, suggesting bears stepping up bets a little after HSI snapped 6-day winning streak. HKEx (0388.HK) topped list with HK$559.46 million, followed by \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$350.40 million -- duo led HSI rally starting September. Next Tencent (0700.HK) with HK$299.52 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$253.74 million, ICBC (1398.HK) with HK$248.96 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] A growing global deficit market in palladium likely from 2011-12, unless a double-dip scenario materializes, says Citi as it upgrades its price forecasts. Projects strong light vehicle production growth in gasoline-based markets in U.S. and China, plus likely depletion of above-ground stock including Russian inventory, underpinning bullish medium-term price outlook for palladium; upgrades 2010 forecast 10% to US$497/oz, 2011 by 28% to $575 and 2012 price estimate +63% to $650, after which it slowly declines. Spot palladium last up $3 at $584. Citi modelling also points to likely surplus market for platinum from 2011 as rising mine supply, growing auto catalyst recycling to outstrip demand growth; lifts 2010 platinum price forecast by 2% to $1,585, 2011 by 7% to $1,600. "A range-bound platinum price is likely in the medium term," Citi says. Platinum last up $5 at $1,681. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 18.6 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling after 4 consecutive days of net buying may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 34.7 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 16.1 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs may open lower, following U.S. Treasurys' weakness overnight; but JGBs expected to maintain solid bias until next FOMC meeting in early November, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano. Adds while JGB market likely to struggle in morning, yield curve expected to steepen ahead of 30-year JGB auction tomorrow. Lead December JGB futures tipped in 143.88-144.17 band vs 144.06 last close, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.845%-0.865% vs 0.850%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may mark fresh 15-year low, could fall below Monday's low of 81.37, on broad USD weakness as September FOMC meeting minutes released overnight strengthens view Fed may take additional quantitative easing measures soon, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. Says key levels will be 81.25, 81.00, but pair unlikely to fall past 81.00 today as investors still cautious about potential Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb sharp JPY strength. Says players looking out for comments on Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in major economies as G-7, IMF over weekend generated little such comment; "If somebody says something negative about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , (dollar/yen) may test the downside." Says pair may trade in 81.00-82.00 range in global day, last 81.81. EUR/USD biased up on broad USD weakness, may trade in 1.3850-1.4030, last 1.3915. EUR/JPY may trade in 113.40-114.50, last 113.85.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may rise tad, opening price may be pegged around CME close of 9440 (vs Osaka close at 9400) after falling 2.1% to 9388.64 Tuesday; Nikkei may move between 9300-9500, say market analysts. But they say weaker USD/JPY (now 81.78) casting worries over exporters' earnings, therefore dip-buying may be limited. "Since Intel's (earnings) results were positive, tech shares may outperform, but gains may be limited," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Adds futures market, which is (especially) sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, being closely eyed to see if government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if USD/JPY falls further. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] China September trade surplus likely narrowed slightly to $18.5 billion vs August's $20.03 billion, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 13 economists, but small decline not likely to reduce tensions on yuan exchange rate. Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist Lu Ting says China likely to allow "mild appreciation" of CNY vs USD near-term to smooth relations with U.S., but adds any CNY moves will take at least 6 months to effect trade data. Trade data due today from General Administration of Customs. See: "=PREVIEW: China September Trade Surplus Likely Narrowed To $18.5 Bln" (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 is widely expected to rise about 0.8% early Wednesday after it underperformed the S&P 500 by 2.1% on Tuesday and AUD/USD rose about 0.8% after domestic equities closed Tuesday, as dovish FOMC minutes rekindled expectations of a second round of quantitative easing in the U.S. Financials and materials should generate most of the strength locally after U.S. financial sector rose 1.2% and S&P/ASX 200 materials sector underperformed yesterday. China trade data could be the main swing factor, although Australian consumer sentiment data are also due. On the charts, there will be scope for further declines in line with potential 5-point reversal pattern while Monday's peak at 4713.0 caps, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. If the index were to fail near 4640.0 today, the 200-day moving average near 4620 could remain under pressure and a test of the uptrend line at 4600.0 might be possible. However, bullish inverse head & shoulders pattern continues to target 5025.0 while 4559.0 holds. Index last 4618.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to rise as Fed minutes Tuesday reinforce opinion that further quantitative easing on the way; but much of those expectations likely already priced in, so any gains may be minor; DJIA ends barely higher, +0.1%, while Nasdaq +0.7%, suggests Asian techs may also outperform. Intel posts slightly better than expected 3Q EPS, shares +0.9% in after-hours trade, could support Asian chip makers. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in relatively tight ranges, USD likely pressured lower by Fed minutes; JPY traders remain on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities with USD/JPY below 82 handle. USD/JPY last 81.79 vs 81.74 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3919 vs 1.3914, EUR/JPY at 113.82 vs 113.74. On data slate: Australia \uc2 Westpac\uc2 -Melbourne Institute consumer sentiment survey at 2330 GMT, Japan core machinery orders, bank lending at 2350 GMT, Australia lending finance at 0030 GMT, China trade, HK chief executive's policy address. Later there's French CPI, U.K. unemployment, BOE MPC member Sentance speaks, EU industrial production, U.S. MBA mortgage applications, import & export price indexes, Fed chairman Bernanke speaks, API oil report, Richmond Fed president Lacker speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 1947ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac000ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
