s market, which is (especially) sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, being closely eyed to see if government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if USD/JPY falls further. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] China September trade surplus likely narrowed slightly to $18.5 billion vs August's $20.03 billion, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 13 economists, but small decline not likely to reduce tensions on yuan exchange rate. Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist Lu Ting says China likely to allow "mild appreciation" of CNY vs USD near-term to smooth relations with U.S., but adds any CNY moves will take at least 6 months to effect trade data. Trade data due today from General Administration of Customs. See: "=PREVIEW: China September Trade Surplus Likely Narrowed To $18.5 Bln" (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 is widely expected to rise about 0.8% early Wednesday after it underperformed the S&P 500 by 2.1% on Tuesday and AUD/USD rose about 0.8% after domestic equities closed Tuesday, as dovish FOMC minutes rekindled expectations of a second round of quantitative easing in the U.S. Financials and materials should generate most of the strength locally after U.S. financial sector rose 1.2% and S&P/ASX 200 materials sector underperformed yesterday. China trade data could be the main swing factor, although Australian consumer sentiment data are also due. On the charts, there will be scope for further declines in line with potential 5-point reversal pattern while Monday's peak at 4713.0 caps, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. If the index were to fail near 4640.0 today, the 200-day moving average near 4620 could remain under pressure and a test of the uptrend line at 4600.0 might be possible. However, bullish inverse head & shoulders pattern continues to target 5025.0 while 4559.0 holds. Index last 4618.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to rise as Fed minutes Tuesday reinforce opinion that further quantitative easing on the way; but much of those expectations likely already priced in, so any gains may be minor; DJIA ends barely higher, +0.1%, while Nasdaq +0.7%, suggests Asian techs may also outperform. Intel posts slightly better than expected 3Q EPS, shares +0.9% in after-hours trade, could support Asian chip makers. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in relatively tight ranges, USD likely pressured lower by Fed minutes; JPY traders remain on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities with USD/JPY below 82 handle. USD/JPY last 81.79 vs 81.74 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3919 vs 1.3914, EUR/JPY at 113.82 vs 113.74. On data slate: Australia \uc2 Westpac\uc2 -Melbourne Institute consumer sentiment survey at 2330 GMT, Japan core machinery orders, bank lending at 2350 GMT, Australia lending finance at 0030 GMT, China trade, HK chief executive's policy address. Later there's French CPI, U.K. unemployment, BOE MPC member Sentance speaks, EU industrial production, U.S. MBA mortgage applications, import & export price indexes, Fed chairman Bernanke speaks, API oil report, Richmond Fed president Lacker speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2232 GMT [Dow Jones] Comments from U.S. Federal Reserve overnight "should see base metals open higher this morning as the USD falls and equities rally," says major market participant that requests anonymity. Base metals found some support in late trade overnight after minutes of Fed meeting showed it was moving towards an easier monetary policy stance, which pushed euro higher versus USD, lifted U.S. equities, says trading house, adding that Fed officials believed in September struggling U.S. recovery might soon need more help and they discussed several ways to provide support. Minutes increase confidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take further action to stimulate the economy. LME 3-month copper gained $55 on day yesterday at $8,345/metric ton, aluminum added $37 at $2,436, lead jumped $62 at $2,376, zinc rose $41 at $2,369, tin climbed $305 at $26,500, but nickel fell $275 to $24,050. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2221 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.8% at 4667.0 as offshore equities recovered from intraday weakness after dovish FOMC minutes. DJIA up 0.1% at 11020.40, S&P 500 up 0.4% at 1169.77, Nasdaq up 0.7% at 2417.92 after FOMC minutes showed Fed officials cut U.S. 2011 growth projections and maintained momentum for quantitative easing. In afterhours trading, Intel is up 0.8% after reporting 3Q EPS of 52 U.S. cents vs 50 cents expected, while forecasting 4Q gross margin of 65%-69% vs 66.1% consensus. In regular trading, S&P 500 Financials up 1.3%, with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  up 2.8%, \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  up 1.7%. S&P 500 Materials rose 0.3%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 2.0%, Vale up 1.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 1.7%, Barrick Gold down 0.6%. BHP ADRs at A$40.68 vs local close at A$40.74. London listed BHP down 1.0%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 0.8%. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.1%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.1%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 0.2%. Spot gold at US$1,350.00 vs US$1,353.59. Nymex crude oil down 0.7% at US$81.67. LME copper up 0.7%, zinc up 1.9%, aluminum up 1.8%, nickel down 1.3%. EUR/USD at 1.3923 vs 1.3876. AUD/USD at 0.9861 vs 0.9834. S&P/ASX 200 last 4618.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2213 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold bid $1,349.20/oz, down $1.30 from late NY. Commodity research unit at \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2  said late yesterday that gold remains a buy-on-dip opportunity. "There's still plenty of positive sentiment surrounding the safe-haven metal on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front (falling USD) and the financial front (uncertain U.S. and European economic outlook)," ANZ said in a weekly review, adding that dips should emerge as record CTFC long positions take profits but higher peaks and higher troughs suggest re-entry point will have to be north of $1,335. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 1933ET ]\par \par \uc2 2210 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Stocks eked out slim gain after minutes from September Fed meeting increased confidence \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take further action to stimulate economy; "What the Fed said today didn't surprise anybody," said Bill Vaughn, portfolio manager at Evercore Wealth Management. "Most people thought the comments were going to lay the groundwork for quantitative easing and they essentially did that." Financial, tech companies led gains in stocks as investors grew hopeful for earnings reports. Intel gained 1.1% ahead of result and added another 1.0% after close when quarterly result higher than Wall Street expectations. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , due to release report Wednesday morning added 1.7%. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  added 2.8%. Dow +0.1%, Nasdaq +0.7%,Philly Semicons +0.7%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 1933ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac000ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
