\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Foreign Net Sell Orders On 18.6M Japan Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,978 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 01:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 18.6 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling after 4 consecutive days of net buying may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 34.7 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 16.1 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs may open lower, following U.S. Treasurys' weakness overnight; but JGBs expected to maintain solid bias until next FOMC meeting in early November, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano. Adds while JGB market likely to struggle in morning, yield curve expected to steepen ahead of 30-year JGB auction tomorrow. Lead December JGB futures tipped in 143.88-144.17 band vs 144.06 last close, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.845%-0.865% vs 0.850%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may mark fresh 15-year low, could fall below Monday's low of 81.37, on broad USD weakness as September FOMC meeting minutes released overnight strengthens view Fed may take additional quantitative easing measures soon, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. Says key levels will be 81.25, 81.00, but pair unlikely to fall past 81.00 today as investors still cautious about potential Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb sharp JPY strength. Says players looking out for comments on Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in major economies as G-7, IMF over weekend generated little such comment; "If somebody says something negative about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , (dollar/yen) may test the downside." Says pair may trade in 81.00-82.00 range in global day, last 81.81. EUR/USD biased up on broad USD weakness, may trade in 1.3850-1.4030, last 1.3915. EUR/JPY may trade in 113.40-114.50, last 113.85.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may rise tad, opening price may be pegged around CME close of 9440 (vs Osaka close at 9400) after falling 2.1% to 9388.64 Tuesday; Nikkei may move between 9300-9500, say market analysts. But they say weaker USD/JPY (now 81.78) casting worries over exporters' earnings, therefore dip-buying may be limited. "Since Intel's (earnings) results were positive, tech shares may outperform, but gains may be limited," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Adds futures market, which is (especially) sensitive to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, being closely eyed to see if government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if USD/JPY falls further. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] China September trade surplus likely narrowed slightly to $18.5 billion vs August's $20.03 billion, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 13 economists, but small decline not likely to reduce tensions on yuan exchange rate. Bank of America-Merrill Lynch economist Lu Ting says China likely to allow "mild appreciation" of CNY vs USD near-term to smooth relations with U.S., but adds any CNY moves will take at least 6 months to effect trade data. Trade data due today from General Administration of Customs. See: "=PREVIEW: China September Trade Surplus Likely Narrowed To $18.5 Bln" (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 is widely expected to rise about 0.8% early Wednesday after it underperformed the S&P 500 by 2.1% on Tuesday and AUD/USD rose about 0.8% after domestic equities closed Tuesday, as dovish FOMC minutes rekindled expectations of a second round of quantitative easing in the U.S. Financials and materials should generate most of the strength locally after U.S. financial sector rose 1.2% and S&P/ASX 200 materials sector underperformed yesterday. China trade data could be the main swing factor, although Australian consumer sentiment data are also due. On the charts, there will be scope for further declines in line with potential 5-point reversal pattern while Monday's peak at 4713.0 caps, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. If the index were to fail near 4640.0 today, the 200-day moving average near 4620 could remain under pressure and a test of the uptrend line at 4600.0 might be possible. However, bullish inverse head & shoulders pattern continues to target 5025.0 while 4559.0 holds. Index last 4618.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2253 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to rise as Fed minutes Tuesday reinforce opinion that further quantitative easing on the way; but much of those expectations likely already priced in, so any gains may be minor; DJIA ends barely higher, +0.1%, while Nasdaq +0.7%, suggests Asian techs may also outperform. Intel posts slightly better than expected 3Q EPS, shares +0.9% in after-hours trade, could support Asian chip makers. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in relatively tight ranges, USD likely pressured lower by Fed minutes; JPY traders remain on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities with USD/JPY below 82 handle. USD/JPY last 81.79 vs 81.74 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3919 vs 1.3914, EUR/JPY at 113.82 vs 113.74. On data slate: Australia \uc2 Westpac\uc2 -Melbourne Institute consumer sentiment survey at 2330 GMT, Japan core machinery orders, bank lending at 2350 GMT, Australia lending finance at 0030 GMT, China trade, HK chief executive's policy address. Later there's French CPI, U.K. unemployment, BOE MPC member Sentance speaks, EU industrial production, U.S. MBA mortgage applications, import & export price indexes, Fed chairman Bernanke speaks, API oil report, Richmond Fed president Lacker speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2232 GMT [Dow Jones] Comments from U.S. Federal Reserve overnight "should see base metals open higher this morning as the USD falls and equities rally," says major market participant that requests anonymity. Base metals found some support in late trade overnight after minutes of Fed meeting showed it was moving towards an easier monetary policy stance, which pushed euro higher versus USD, lifted U.S. equities, says trading house, adding that Fed officials believed in September struggling U.S. recovery might soon need more help and they discussed several ways to provide support. Minutes increase confidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take further action to stimulate the economy. LME 3-month copper gained $55 on day yesterday at $8,345/metric ton, aluminum added $37 at $2,436, lead jumped $62 at $2,376, zinc rose $41 at $2,369, tin climbed $305 at $26,500, but nickel fell $275 to $24,050. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2221 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.8% at 4667.0 as offshore equities recovered from intraday weakness after dovish FOMC minutes. DJIA up 0.1% at 11020.40, S&P 500 up 0.4% at 1169.77, Nasdaq up 0.7% at 2417.92 after FOMC minutes showed Fed officials cut U.S. 2011 growth projections and maintained momentum for quantitative easing. In afterhours trading, Intel is up 0.8% after reporting 3Q EPS of 52 U.S. cents vs 50 cents expected, while forecasting 4Q gross margin of 65%-69% vs 66.1% consensus. In regular trading, S&P 500 Financials up 1.3%, with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  up 2.8%, \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  up 1.7%. S&P 500 Materials rose 0.3%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 2.0%, Vale up 1.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 1.7%, Barrick Gold down 0.6%. BHP ADRs at A$40.68 vs local close at A$40.74. London listed BHP down 1.0%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 0.8%. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.1%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.1%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 0.2%. Spot gold at US$1,350.00 vs US$1,353.59. Nymex crude oil down 0.7% at US$81.67. LME copper up 0.7%, zinc up 1.9%, aluminum up 1.8%, nickel down 1.3%. EUR/USD at 1.3923 vs 1.3876. AUD/USD at 0.9861 vs 0.9834. S&P/ASX 200 last 4618.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2213 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold bid $1,349.20/oz, down $1.30 from late NY. Commodity research unit at \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2  said late yesterday that gold remains a buy-on-dip opportunity. "There's still plenty of positive sentiment surrounding the safe-haven metal on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front (falling USD) and the financial front (uncertain U.S. and European economic outlook)," ANZ said in a weekly review, adding that dips should emerge as record CTFC long positions take profits but higher peaks and higher troughs suggest re-entry point will have to be north of $1,335. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2210 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Stocks eked out slim gain after minutes from September Fed meeting increased confidence \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take further action to stimulate economy; "What the Fed said today didn't surprise anybody," said Bill Vaughn, portfolio manager at Evercore Wealth Management. "Most people thought the comments were going to lay the groundwork for quantitative easing and they essentially did that." Financial, tech companies led gains in stocks as investors grew hopeful for earnings reports. Intel gained 1.1% ahead of result and added another 1.0% after close when quarterly result higher than Wall Street expectations. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , due to release report Wednesday morning added 1.7%. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  added 2.8%. Dow +0.1%, Nasdaq +0.7%,Philly Semicons +0.7%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 1920ET ]\par \par \uc2 2159 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD lost ground as EUR/USD spiked above 1.39 after minutes from Fed's latest meeting indicated members of policy-setting committee believe new steps could be needed to stimulate growth; "The [FOMC] minutes reiterated much of the same thing, and overall indicated a very dovish tone," says Mary Nicola, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in New York. "The Fed do seem ready to pull the trigger on quantitative easing, if deemed necessary." EUR/USD was at 1.3914 vs 1.3877 late Monday. USD/JPY was at 81.74 vs 82.13, EUR/JPY was at 113.74 vs 113.97, GBP/USD at 1.5796 vs 1.5873, USD/CHF was at 0.9570 vs 0.9647. U.S. dollar index was at 77.339 vs 77.500. Stocks eked out slim gain after FOMC minutes; \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  +1.7%. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  +0.6% after drug giant said it will buy \uc2 King Pharmaceuticals\uc2  for $3.6 billion. \uc2 King Pharmaceuticals\uc2  +39%. Dow +0.1%, Nasdaq +0.7%,Philly Semicons +0.7%. Treasurys were tripped up by a lukewarm sale of $32 billion three-year notes, minutes from Fed's September monetary policy meeting; 2-year yields +2.0 basis points at 0.367%; 10 year yields 1.6 basis points at 2.411%. Crude futures settled slightly lower as investors moved to lock in profits from recent rally ahead of meeting of oil ministers, crude inventory data due later this week; November Nymex crude settled down 54 cents at $81.67/bbl. December Comex fell $7.70 to $1,346.70 a troy ounce. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 10-12-10 1920ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101012e6ac000ee\par }\page {