\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei edges up but yen weighs; Intel helps techs\b0\par\par\uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 01:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Nikkei up on short-covering after 2.1 pct drop on Tuesday\par \par \uc2 * But gains capped as yen remains near 15-year high\par \par \uc2 * View on further yen rise sparks earnings worry -strategist\par \par \uc2 * Elliott Wave shows Nikkei may test 10,000 if 9,311 held\par \par \uc2 By Aiko Hayashi and Chikafumi Hodo\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 13 (Reuters) - Japan's Nikkei average inched up 0.2 percent on Wednesday as an advance in U.S. stocks and upbeat forecasts by \uc2 Intel Corp\uc2  brightened sentiment and prompted short-covering.\par \par \uc2 But investors were reluctant to chase the Nikkei aggressively higher as the yen lurked near a 15-year high versus the dollar hit earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The market mood improved after the Nikkei lost more than 2 percent on Tuesday, with chip-related stocks advancing after shares of Intel, the world's largest chipmaker, rose on strong fourth-quarter sales and margins.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei also received support after Japanese core machinery orders unexpectedly jumped 10 percent in August.\par \par \uc2 "The market recovered some ground after Japanese stocks fared much worse than overseas markets yesterday due to the strong yen. It also received some positive impact from Intel's earnings," said Masaru Hamasaki, senior strategist at Toyota Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "But after dollar/yen fell below 82 yen, a growing consensus in the market is that the yen will probably continue to advance little by little and that has sparked worries about the outlook for corporate earnings."\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Nikkei ended up 14.87 points at 9,403.51. On Tuesday, it fell 2.1 percent in its biggest daily loss in a month.\par \par \uc2 The broader Topix dropped 0.2 percent to 822.65.\par \par \uc2 Intel's forecasts raised hopes that the technology sector could end 2010 on a strong note, pushing up chip-related shares, though Hamasaki noted that the impact on the overall market was limited because it was not as if the company revised up their earnings outlooks.\par \par \uc2 Chip-gear maker \uc2 Tokyo Electron Ltd\uc2  gained 1.3 percent to 4,550 yen, while \uc2 Nikon Corp\uc2  rose 0.9 percent to 1,599 yen and \uc2 Yokogawa Electric Corp\uc2  climbed 2.6 percent to 599 yen.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Nikkei struggled to post strong gains due to the strength of the yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar gained 0.2 percent to 81.86 yen, not far away from a 15-year low of 81.37 struck on Monday, though it was supported by nervousness that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  the closer it gets to its record low of 79.75 yen.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said in parliament that he could not answer whether or not \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks hit fresh 5-month highs on Tuesday as details from the Federal Reserve's latest meeting showed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may once again flood markets with cheap cash "before long" to further boost growth.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has become very short dollars on QE expectations.\par \par \uc2 On the technical front, the Nikkei was expected to be supported by positive signals.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei faces strong support at the 38.2 percent retracement of its September-October rally and its 55-day moving average, both of which come in around 9,365.\par \par \uc2 Elliott Wave analysis shows the Nikkei could rally to 10,000 as long as it holds above 9,311.\par \par \uc2 EARNINGS IN FOCUS\par \par \uc2 With Japan's earnings season getting into full swing later this month, earnings stories have started to drive stock prices.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Best Denki Co\uc2  surged 11 percent to 222 yen after the operator of consumer electronics stores lifted its full-year operating profit forecast by 30 percent as Japan's hottest summer in a century helped sales of air conditioners.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Tokyo Steel Manufacturing Co\uc2  shed 5.4 percent to 921 yen after Japan's biggest construction steel maker revised its six-month parent operating forecast down to a 1.5 billion yen loss from a 1.7 billion yen profit.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Steel had to raise prices on all products in September to pass on higher steel scrap costs.\par \par \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  extended falls made since the operator of the Uniqlo discount clothing chain forecast its first profit fall in four years, shaking investor confidence in one of the few Japanese retailers thought to have strong growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 The stock fell 2.4 percent to 10,910 yen, after losing 9.8 percent on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Some 1.89 billion shares changed hands on the Tokyo exchange's first section, down from a five-month high of 2.88 billion booked last Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Declining stocks outnumbered advancers, 892 to 604. (Additional reporting by Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Krishna Kumar in Sydney; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-STOCKS (UPDATE 5)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101012e6ac001jg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
