\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Former Deputy BOJ Gov: Japan Must Act Quickly If Yen Rises -Nikkei\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 00:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Nikkei)--At a time when countries around the world are competing to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Toshiro Mutoh, chairman of \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research Ltd\uc2 ., says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market when the yen rapidly appreciates, the Nikkei reported.\par \par \uc2 Mutoh, former deputy governor at the Bank of Japan, discussed Japan's policy options and the global economic outlook in an interview with The Nikkei.\par \par \uc2 Excerpts from the interview follow.\par \par \uc2 Q: The Bank of Japan has reintroduced a zero-interest-rate policy to put an end to deflation in the country. Could you comment on this?\par \par \uc2 A: Preventing protracted deflation is the biggest task a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must undertake in a developed economy. The BOJ should be highly praised for adopting a comprehensive easy-money policy, including effectively resuscitating a zero-rate policy--a message that it will continue relaxing its grip on credit for a long time--and for establishing a fund to buy Japanese government bonds and other assets. The decision, ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's possible further easing, was timely.\par \par \uc2 But, at present, there is no mighty means of ending long-term deflation. It is important that the BOJ continue sending a message to the market that it will do whatever it can do to contain it.\par \par \uc2 Q: How do you evaluate the decision to purchase JGBs through a fund?\par \par \uc2 A: Inside the BOJ, there is deep-seated opposition to increasing purchases of JGBs due to the so-called "BOJ rule" (of keeping its holdings of long-term JGBs below the amount of bank notes in circulation). A device of this kind (the fund scheme) is therefore understandable.\par \par \uc2 The fund will be an effective credit-easing tool as it will be able to buy a wide variety of assets, including exchange-traded funds and real estate investment trusts.\par \par \uc2 Not only these but all other measures must be considered under protracted deflation.\par \par \uc2 Q: But the yen continues to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 A: Market participants are probably expecting the yen to continue rising as the gap in interest rates between Japan and the U.S. will not stop narrowing in view of the Fed's move toward further monetary easing. The yen's appreciation relative to not only the dollar but also the won and yuan is starting to have adverse effects, especially on exporters.\par \par \uc2 The government and BOJ implemented a yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-September. They should maintain a firm attitude, indicating they will resolutely do it again if they need to. When the yen rises by 2 to 3 yen in a short time, they should step into the market without hesitation because such a steep appreciation is caused by speculation.\par \par \uc2 A country's economic fundamentals determine its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate on a medium- to long-term basis. The U.S. economy will likely get on a recovery path before the Japanese economy. Some 50% of U.S. government bonds are held by non-Americans, who do not want a weak dollar. I think the U.S. will return to a strong-dollar policy when its economy picks up.\par \par \uc2 Q: How about Japan's economic outlook?\par \par \uc2 A: The Japanese economy will continue leveling off until mid-2011 and the consumer price index will stay in negative territory until then, I think. There have been almost no price increases on average since 1995 in Japan. Few other major countries have experienced such a long deflationary trend.\par \par \uc2 Fiscal spending, in addition to a loosening of monetary policy, is necessary to end long-lasting deflation. The effectiveness of the planned supplementary budget worth about Y5 trillion for the current fiscal year is being questioned. But given Japan's excessive fiscal deficit, it is unreasonable to increase the deficit further.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, JGB prices remain solid, bringing long-term interest rates to an abnormally low level of less than 1%. Some say there is a bubble in the JGB market caused by an inflow of surplus funds from businesses and households.\par \par \uc2 JGB prices may plunge and send interest rates soaring when companies increase capital spending and cut back on JGB purchases.\par \par \uc2 Q: What should be done to avert such consequences?\par \par \uc2 A: The BOJ will have to decide whether it should sharply increase purchases of JGBs. The worst scenario for Japan is for deflation to persist amid a rise in long-term interest rates. To avoid this, a market-oriented JGM management policy is necessary.\par \par \uc2 Relations between the government and BOJ appear sour from time to time. If the market has this impression, fiscal and monetary policies cannot generate their intended effects. The government and BOJ should create the impression that they are cooperating.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 11, 2010 18:17 ET (22:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101011e6ab00473\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
