\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-TAKE A LOOK-Five world markets themes next week\b0\par\par\uc2 957 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats item that ran on Friday with no changes)\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 8 (Reuters) - Following are five big themes likely to dominate the thinking of investors and traders in the coming week and the Reuters stories related to them.\par \par \uc2  1/ ESCALATING \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  WAR \par \par \uc2  Barring a Plaza Accord-style agreement, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tension will be high in the run-up to the Group of 20 finance ministers' meeting on Oct 22. With U.S. authorities keeping up the pressure for a stronger Chinese yuan, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen and several emerging countries acting to drive down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or imposing capital controls, competitive devaluation seems to be the name of the game. There is little respite in prospect for the U.S. dollar as the Federal Reserve prepares the ground for more quantitative easing, with the Bank of England also possibly heading down that route. On the other hand, the euro, Australian dollar and some emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have surged as a wave of yield-seeking capital hits them, testing the tolerance of policymakers towards what they see as "beggar your neighbour" policies from some of the big players.   > PDF on global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/dyw27p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/dyw27p}}}\uc2   > G20 proximity talks needed to avert \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  war [ID:nLDE69321O]  > Investors caught in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war crossfire [ID:nN07213765]\par \par \uc2  2/ JAPANESE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  STILL LOOMS\par \par \uc2  The yen is back at 15-year highs against the dollar and breaching levels at which the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15 to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many expect more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming days, although it looks increasingly clear the Japanese have drawn no line in the sand and are not targeting any specific level. The market consensus appears to be that if Tokyo steps in again it is likely to suffer from the law of diminishing returns. The BOJ has also cut rates and pledged to pump more funds into the economy, all of which sets the stage for a return of yen carry trades. Japanese policymakers are hoping such trades will make a comeback as happened after the 2004 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Federal Reserve will also play a key role in shaping the future of dollar/yen. It is still unclear whether It will opt for a 'drip-feed' or "shock and awe" approach to QE.  > Investors bet on initial yen win for Japan [ID:nLDE68R1PE]  > Fed mulls controversial inflation idea [ID:nN07125991]  > Bullard says Fed easing decision tough call [ID:nN08199744]\par \par \uc2  3/ EURO SWINGS AND ROUNDABOUTS\par \par \uc2  China's pledge to buy Greek government bonds when Athens returns to market and Asian central banks' drive to diversify their reserves in part into euros have driven euro zone peripheral bond yield spreads over German benchmarks tighter and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly over $1.40. However, problems remain in other peripheral states such as Ireland and Portugal, and with the euro rebounding 7.5 percent in September and 2.5 percent this month, European companies struggling with austerity measures in the domestic market face more hurdles in competing against firms from the United States and emerging markets. Germany's blue chip index, which has outperformed in part owing to a weaker euro, may lag other major stock indexes from here on if the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps rising.  > Euro at $1.40 a concern but no big threat [ID:nLDE6970SA]  > German export drop adds to strong euro worry [ID:nLDE6970A5]  > Euro too strong at $1.40 - Juncker [ID:nWEA1610]\par \par \uc2  4/ CROSSING OVER\par \par \uc2  The prospect of more QE from major central banks has lifted shares around the world, with German government bonds and some European stocks also benefiting even though the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved ahead with its gradual withdrawal of extraordinary stimulus. With the Fed seen poised for a fresh bout of QE, German and U.S. 10-year bond yields are rapidly converging and could even cross over with Treasuries yielding less than Bunds for the first time since mid-2009. The extent to which Bunds underperform Treasuries will depend largely on how long the ECB keeps monetary conditions tighter in the euro zone than in the United States and whether yield-hungry investors are prepared to dump Bunds in favour of the region's fringe issuers.  > ECB warns on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, holds rates [ID:nLDE6950HJ]  > U.S. Treasuries [US/], euro govt bond [GVD/EUR] reports  > Graphic on U.S., German yields {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/vuc67p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/vuc67p}}}\par \par \uc2  5/ DON'T BANK ON IT\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2  kick off the third quarter earnings season for U.S. banks on Oct. 13. Concern is growing that earnings from banks that are heavily reliant on investment banking and trading could come under pressure because of low trading volumes in July and August. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , for one, could be among companies that could disappoint this earnings period, according to a StarMine analysis of profit estimates. Weak U.S. growth may also be reflected in other sectors in this quarterly reporting period, and investors will be keeping an eye on outlooks as more companies report their results. However, the Q3 earnings season may be a sideshow as investors look to central banks to boost growth with more quantitative easing. One-year forward earnings for the U.S. S&P 500 have been hovering around 12 times since May, while those of the MSCI Europe have been trading at between 10 and 11 times since May. > Trading slump seen hitting M.Stanley, Goldman [ID:nN07166445]  > S&P 500 earnings diary for week ahead [SP500/WK]  > Investors eye dividend surprises for return [ID:nLDE6951KY]\par \par \uc2  (Compiled by Nigel Stephenson)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS/THEMES (TAKEALOOK)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|NAT|J|UKI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101014e6ab000hk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
