\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : US Dollar Pressure Continues, Hits New 15-Year Low Against Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Geoffrey Rogow   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have picked up where they left off last week, selling the U.S. dollar, after Friday's U.S. jobs data proved weaker than expected and the International Monetary Fund's annual meeting at the weekend failed to ease \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles roiling markets.\par \par \uc2 In the first Asian session since Friday's U.S. employment data helped reinforce expectations the Federal Reserve will announce new quantitative easing asset buying, the U.S. dollar was once again falling against nearly all major crosses early Monday.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has been under pressure for weeks as market watchers continue to ratchet up their expectations the Fed will use quantitative easing to add more monetary stimulus to the U.S. economy\par \par \uc2 With little of that changed Monday, there has been more selling of the U.S. dollar, which hit another 15-year low against the yen and traded near an eight-month low against the euro.\par \par \uc2 "The market is saying this employment report on Friday wasn't enough to keep the Fed from quantitative easing, the trend is still lower bond yields and a weak dollar," said Richard Grace, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In recent trading, the U.S. dollar traded at Y81.96, just off its 15-year low of Y81.40 hit earlier in the day. The euro reached above US$1.400 for only the second time in eight months.\par \par \uc2 Trading is expected to be light Monday, and potentially volatile, with both Japan and the U.S. on holiday.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. dollar falls against the Japanese yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk has already ratcheted up on trading desks in early trading, said Grace.\par \par \uc2 "We may see BOJ anytime in the market with Dollar-Yen falling," said Grace. If BOJ comes into the market, that would get a dollar bid in the market."\par \par \uc2 Traders had gone into the weekend paring some of their short U.S. dollar holdings, with the U.S. dollar staging a late Friday bounce bank ahead of the weekend meeting of the IMF and the World Bank in Washington.\par \par \uc2 But although China came under criticism from some countries, whose officials characterized the Yuan as undervalued and called for its revaluation, there was no major accord.\par \par \uc2 Notably, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Zhou Xiaochuan, said he figures U.S. and European pressure will diminish once unemployment there begins to decline. He also dismissed the suggestion by some Chinese academics that Beijing should offer to limit exports to the U.S. temporarily as a way to defuse tensions, in the same way that Japan limited auto exports to the U.S. in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 "That's an opinion of a small group of economists," Zhou said. "It's not seriously considered."\par \par \uc2 With the IMF meeting providing little direction, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate ongoing between governments is expected to pick up again at a Seoul summit of leaders of the G-20 industrial and developing nations in mid November.\par \par \uc2 Before then, the early trading on Monday could be a harbinger of more to come this week. Net downside speculative bets, called shorts, against the U.S. dollar increased 8% last week to US$31 billion. Speculators now hold the largest short U.S. dollar position since November of 2007.\par \par \uc2 That weakness has been particularly helpful for the Australian dollar, which now sits on the precipice of hitting parity for the first time since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in 1983. Early Monday, the Australian dollar traded at US$0.9906, just off a post-float high of US$0.9918 hit late last week after an ongoing mining boom, robust labor market, carry-trade benefits and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in tightening mode drove a month-and-a-half surge for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, such a robust short position in the U.S. dollar has some traders early Monday saying the market may have gotten overextended ahead of some crucial data and speeches later this week.\par \par \uc2 "Should this week's U.S. data throw up a positive surprise, a short-covering rally in the (U.S. dollar) may well result. In this respect, the Fed's minutes on Wednesday morning and Friday's slew of U.S. data will be worth keeping an eye on. In the short-term, support on the USD index is expected on dips towards last week's 76.90 low, with initial resistance around 77.90," said \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index, a measure weighing the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recently traded at 76.954, off 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Geoffrey Rogow, Dow Jones Newswires; +61-2-8272-4686; geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Bob Davis in Washington and Rebecca Howard in Wellington contributed to this report.) [ 11-10-10 0038GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101011e6ab0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
