 "Market liquidity is still good and there hasn't been any signals that foreign investors are switching their positions," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Lee Jae-mahn, who tips 1910 resistance for day; foreign inflows have boosted domestic market in recent sessions. Securities stocks like \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE), Woori Investment (005940.SE) may rise as strong trading activity lift their earnings, while automakers like \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) may also gain on expectations for strong 3Q earnings. Domestic earnings season kicks off this week. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 2.1 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 4th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 18.8 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 16.7 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 3.5% at HK$4.791 billion yesterday, 5.0% of total market volume vs Friday's 5.7%, suggesting bears still deterred by market strength, with HSI in 6-day winning streak, up 23 of last 27 sessions overall. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) led with HK$436.39 million, followed by \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$273.21 million, HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$272.80 million. China Life (2628.HK), Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$255.66 million, HK$212.84 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise despite weaker USD/JPY (now 82.05), due to speculation about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese government; investors also relieved about relative firmness in U.S. stocks despite weaker-than-expected September jobs data released on Friday. Nikkei range for today: 9500-9700 vs 9588.88 (down 1.0%) Friday (Japanese markets closed Monday for public holiday). "Since there was no (open) criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (September 15) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the G-7 (meeting), the government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the dollar falls (sharply and stays under 82 yen)," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Adds investors turning attention to U.S. earnings season with Intel (INTC) expected to report 3Q results later in global trading day. Commodity-linked shares likely to trade firmly after gold futures rose to fresh record high on Monday. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9625 yesterday vs Osaka close at 9580 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher, as JPY's sharp rise increase worries over export-driven Japan economy, boosts demand for safe-haven government bonds; weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data released Friday also raises expectations for additional easing from Fed, which could pressure down JGB yields, analysts say; \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano notes that even during volatile trade last week, dip-buying demand was solid when 10-year yield above 0.9%. Adds, however, oversupply concerns linger in superlong zone, expects yield curve to be biased toward steepening. Lead December JGB futures tipped in 143.81-144.22 band vs 143.95 last close on Friday, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.845%-0.870% vs 0.855%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet, albeit with short-term downside risk, after Wall Street stalled despite expectations of U.S. quantitative easing at the November 2-3 FOMC meeting. That's consistent with developing 5-point reversal pattern on S&P/ASX 200, after spiked above last month's peak at 4698.0, but failed to close above that level yesterday. Risk of a 5-point reversal pattern is highlighted by the fact that 4700 is potentially major resistance (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of April-May rise), according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Potential short-term peak is supported by fresh sell signal on daily stochastics. As such, traders can consider taking short positions in anticipation of some profit taking over the next two days. Stop-losses can be placed above Monday's high at 4713.0. However, any 5-point reversal pattern won't be triggered unless index breaks the 4559.0 trough. Moreover, the inverse head & shoulders pattern which targets the April peak at 5025.0 will remain intact while index stays above 4559.0. Index down 7 points at 4690.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to drift, trade on local cues; little cue from Wall Street with main indexes mostly flat in holiday-thinned trade. Markets now looking to 3Q corporate results for guidance, expectations of further quantitative easing from Fed likely to support. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, look to equities for cues; with USD/JPY below where Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY, traders remain on watch for possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from Monday's holiday. USD/JPY at 82.11 vs 82.13 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3879 vs 1.3877, EUR/JPY at 113.96 vs 113.97. On data slate: Australia NAB business survey at 0030 GMT, Philippines exports at 0100 GMT, Japan consumer confidence survey at 0500 GMT, India industrial production at 0530 GMT. Later there's German CPI, WPI, U.K. trade, inflation DCLG house price index, Nationwide consumer confidence index, U.S. ICSC-\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chain store sales, Johnson Redbook retail sales, employment trends index, economic optimism index, ABC News consumer comfort index, Fed of Kansas president Hoenig speaks, ECB president Trichet speaks, U.K. BOE MPC external member Posen speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2245 GMT [Dow Jones] USD the real driver of base metals overnight, so worth keeping an eye on EUR/USD "as this should provide some price direction for base metals," says major market participant that requests anonymity. Prospect of further U.S. quantitative easing continues to weigh on USD vs euro, which in turn supports complex, it says, adding that LME week underway in London historically generates volatility, tends to set tone for 4Q. Separately, Triland Metals posited interesting concept overnight, noting that copper broadly held near Friday's high levels after bad U.S. jobless figures. "These led to market speculation of further monetary easing by the Federal Reserve, which in turn drove the market higher. This could go on for a little while but maybe the market could also realize that bad news is actually bad news and interpret figures the other way around," Triland pondered, adding sentiment at LME events positive. Overnight LME 3-month copper eased $20 on day yesterday to $8,290/metric ton, aluminum lost $20 at $2,400, nickel slipped $75 to $24,325, tin fell $150 at $26,195 but lead rose $43 at $2,315, zinc gained $40 at $2,330. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian financial markets have placed 50/50 bets on an RBA rate hike at November policy meeting, according to analysis by \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . Lack of certainty in markets likely reflects surprise decision by RBA in October to hold rates steady at 4.50%. Despite recent hawkish rhetoric from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , markets only expect two 25 bps rate hikes over the next two years, CS report shows. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2224 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite offshore macro headwinds, Australian corporate earnings risk remains skewed to the upside, particularly in light of strong domestic macro environment, featuring strong employment, record trade surplus and double-digit nominal GDP growth, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "Historically, increases to year-on-year GDP growth of greater than 150 basis points, particularly following a large earnings decline, have been consistent with double-digit Industrials EPS growth," says broker. Expects key themes from upcoming AGM season to include appreciating Australian dollar, increasing cost pressures and commodity prices, accelerating domestic economy, weakening developed-market economies and balance sheet strength/capital deployment. S&P/ASX 200 last 4697.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 1938ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101011e6ab000br\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
