. Index down 7 points at 4690.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to drift, trade on local cues; little cue from Wall Street with main indexes mostly flat in holiday-thinned trade. Markets now looking to 3Q corporate results for guidance, expectations of further quantitative easing from Fed likely to support. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, look to equities for cues; with USD/JPY below where Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY, traders remain on watch for possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from Monday's holiday. USD/JPY at 82.11 vs 82.13 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3879 vs 1.3877, EUR/JPY at 113.96 vs 113.97. On data slate: Australia NAB business survey at 0030 GMT, Philippines exports at 0100 GMT, Japan consumer confidence survey at 0500 GMT, India industrial production at 0530 GMT. Later there's German CPI, WPI, U.K. trade, inflation DCLG house price index, Nationwide consumer confidence index, U.S. ICSC-\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chain store sales, Johnson Redbook retail sales, employment trends index, economic optimism index, ABC News consumer comfort index, Fed of Kansas president Hoenig speaks, ECB president Trichet speaks, U.K. BOE MPC external member Posen speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2245 GMT [Dow Jones] USD the real driver of base metals overnight, so worth keeping an eye on EUR/USD "as this should provide some price direction for base metals," says major market participant that requests anonymity. Prospect of further U.S. quantitative easing continues to weigh on USD vs euro, which in turn supports complex, it says, adding that LME week underway in London historically generates volatility, tends to set tone for 4Q. Separately, Triland Metals posited interesting concept overnight, noting that copper broadly held near Friday's high levels after bad U.S. jobless figures. "These led to market speculation of further monetary easing by the Federal Reserve, which in turn drove the market higher. This could go on for a little while but maybe the market could also realize that bad news is actually bad news and interpret figures the other way around," Triland pondered, adding sentiment at LME events positive. Overnight LME 3-month copper eased $20 on day yesterday to $8,290/metric ton, aluminum lost $20 at $2,400, nickel slipped $75 to $24,325, tin fell $150 at $26,195 but lead rose $43 at $2,315, zinc gained $40 at $2,330. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian financial markets have placed 50/50 bets on an RBA rate hike at November policy meeting, according to analysis by \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . Lack of certainty in markets likely reflects surprise decision by RBA in October to hold rates steady at 4.50%. Despite recent hawkish rhetoric from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , markets only expect two 25 bps rate hikes over the next two years, CS report shows. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2224 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite offshore macro headwinds, Australian corporate earnings risk remains skewed to the upside, particularly in light of strong domestic macro environment, featuring strong employment, record trade surplus and double-digit nominal GDP growth, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "Historically, increases to year-on-year GDP growth of greater than 150 basis points, particularly following a large earnings decline, have been consistent with double-digit Industrials EPS growth," says broker. Expects key themes from upcoming AGM season to include appreciating Australian dollar, increasing cost pressures and commodity prices, accelerating domestic economy, weakening developed-market economies and balance sheet strength/capital deployment. S&P/ASX 200 last 4697.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2213 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold $1,355.30/oz, up $1.40 from late NY, just shy of yesterday's intraday high of $1,355.50. ScotiaMocatta says there are no reversal signals on charts so bias remains for another push higher, with important supports pegged at $1,320, then $1,312. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 1938ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101011e6ab000bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
