later in global trading day. Commodity-linked shares likely to trade firmly after gold futures rose to fresh record high on Monday. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9625 yesterday vs Osaka close at 9580 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher, as JPY's sharp rise increase worries over export-driven Japan economy, boosts demand for safe-haven government bonds; weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data released Friday also raises expectations for additional easing from Fed, which could pressure down JGB yields, analysts say; \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano notes that even during volatile trade last week, dip-buying demand was solid when 10-year yield above 0.9%. Adds, however, oversupply concerns linger in superlong zone, expects yield curve to be biased toward steepening. Lead December JGB futures tipped in 143.81-144.22 band vs 143.95 last close on Friday, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.845%-0.870% vs 0.855%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet, albeit with short-term downside risk, after Wall Street stalled despite expectations of U.S. quantitative easing at the November 2-3 FOMC meeting. That's consistent with developing 5-point reversal pattern on S&P/ASX 200, after spiked above last month's peak at 4698.0, but failed to close above that level yesterday. Risk of a 5-point reversal pattern is highlighted by the fact that 4700 is potentially major resistance (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of April-May rise), according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Potential short-term peak is supported by fresh sell signal on daily stochastics. As such, traders can consider taking short positions in anticipation of some profit taking over the next two days. Stop-losses can be placed above Monday's high at 4713.0. However, any 5-point reversal pattern won't be triggered unless index breaks the 4559.0 trough.