\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:JGBs Likely Up On JPY Gains;Steepening Bias Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,808 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 01:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher, as JPY's sharp rise increase worries over export-driven Japan economy, boosts demand for safe-haven government bonds; weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data released Friday also raises expectations for additional easing from Fed, which could pressure down JGB yields, analysts say; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano notes that even during volatile trade last week, dip-buying demand was solid when 10-year yield above 0.9%. Adds, however, oversupply concerns linger in superlong zone, expects yield curve to be biased toward steepening. Lead December JGB futures tipped in 143.81-144.22 band vs 143.95 last close on Friday, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.845%-0.870% vs 0.855%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet, albeit with short-term downside risk, after Wall Street stalled despite expectations of U.S. quantitative easing at the November 2-3 FOMC meeting. That's consistent with developing 5-point reversal pattern on S&P/ASX 200, after spiked above last month's peak at 4698.0, but failed to close above that level yesterday. Risk of a 5-point reversal pattern is highlighted by the fact that 4700 is potentially major resistance (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of April-May rise), according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Potential short-term peak is supported by fresh sell signal on daily stochastics. As such, traders can consider taking short positions in anticipation of some profit taking over the next two days. Stop-losses can be placed above Monday's high at 4713.0. However, any 5-point reversal pattern won't be triggered unless index breaks the 4559.0 trough. Moreover, the inverse head & shoulders pattern which targets the April peak at 5025.0 will remain intact while index stays above 4559.0. Index down 7 points at 4690.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to drift, trade on local cues; little cue from Wall Street with main indexes mostly flat in holiday-thinned trade. Markets now looking to 3Q corporate results for guidance, expectations of further quantitative easing from Fed likely to support. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, look to equities for cues; with USD/JPY below where Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY, traders remain on watch for possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from Monday's holiday. USD/JPY at 82.11 vs 82.13 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3879 vs 1.3877, EUR/JPY at 113.96 vs 113.97. On data slate: Australia NAB business survey at 0030 GMT, Philippines exports at 0100 GMT, Japan consumer confidence survey at 0500 GMT, India industrial production at 0530 GMT. Later there's German CPI, WPI, U.K. trade, inflation DCLG house price index, Nationwide consumer confidence index, U.S. ICSC-\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chain store sales, Johnson Redbook retail sales, employment trends index, economic optimism index, ABC News consumer comfort index, Fed of Kansas president Hoenig speaks, ECB president Trichet speaks, U.K. BOE MPC external member Posen speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2245 GMT [Dow Jones] USD the real driver of base metals overnight, so worth keeping an eye on EUR/USD "as this should provide some price direction for base metals," says major market participant that requests anonymity. Prospect of further U.S. quantitative easing continues to weigh on USD vs euro, which in turn supports complex, it says, adding that LME week underway in London historically generates volatility, tends to set tone for 4Q. Separately, Triland Metals posited interesting concept overnight, noting that copper broadly held near Friday's high levels after bad U.S. jobless figures. "These led to market speculation of further monetary easing by the Federal Reserve, which in turn drove the market higher. This could go on for a little while but maybe the market could also realize that bad news is actually bad news and interpret figures the other way around," Triland pondered, adding sentiment at LME events positive. Overnight LME 3-month copper eased $20 on day yesterday to $8,290/metric ton, aluminum lost $20 at $2,400, nickel slipped $75 to $24,325, tin fell $150 at $26,195 but lead rose $43 at $2,315, zinc gained $40 at $2,330. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian financial markets have placed 50/50 bets on an RBA rate hike at November policy meeting, according to analysis by \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . Lack of certainty in markets likely reflects surprise decision by RBA in October to hold rates steady at 4.50%. Despite recent hawkish rhetoric from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , markets only expect two 25 bps rate hikes over the next two years, CS report shows. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2224 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite offshore macro headwinds, Australian corporate earnings risk remains skewed to the upside, particularly in light of strong domestic macro environment, featuring strong employment, record trade surplus and double-digit nominal GDP growth, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "Historically, increases to year-on-year GDP growth of greater than 150 basis points, particularly following a large earnings decline, have been consistent with double-digit Industrials EPS growth," says broker. Expects key themes from upcoming AGM season to include appreciating Australian dollar, increasing cost pressures and commodity prices, accelerating domestic economy, weakening developed-market economies and balance sheet strength/capital deployment. S&P/ASX 200 last 4697.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2213 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold $1,355.30/oz, up $1.40 from late NY, just shy of yesterday's intraday high of $1,355.50. ScotiaMocatta says there are no reversal signals on charts so bias remains for another push higher, with important supports pegged at $1,320, then $1,312. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2205 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mostly flat, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 3 points at 4718.0 after basically steady night in offshore markets, with U.S. bond market, government offices closed for Columbus Day. DJIA flat at 11010.11, S&P 500 flat at 1165.30, Nasdaq flat at 2402.33. CBOE VIX fell 8.5% to 18.96. OECD leading economic indicators pointed to global slowdown. NABE survey showed economists expect U.S. GDP to rise 2.6% this year and next. Kansas Fed's Hoenig said sustained low interest rates will create new imbalances, possibly causing inflation and be harmful to economic recovery. S&P 500 Financials down 0.1%. S&P 500 Materials down 0.1, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 0.3%, Vale up 1.0%, Freeport down 0.1%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 0.7%, Barrick Gold up 0.3%. BHP ADRs at A$41.20 vs local close at A$41.45. London listed BHP down 0.5%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 1.1%. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.3%. S&P 500 Energy up 0.2%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 0.1%. Spot gold at US$1,353.65 vs US$1,3486.0. Nymex crude oil down 0.5% at US$82.21. LME copper down 0.3%, zinc up 1.9%, aluminum down 0.8%, nickel down 0.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3876 vs 1.3992. AUD/USD at 0.9834 vs 0.9894. S&P/ASX 200 last 4697.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian government bonds likely quiet in Asia session with holidays in key markets overnight providing traders with no fresh leads. A senior trader at a European bank says a watch will be on NAB business survey data at 0030 GMT but the bond market really wants to know more about whether the RBA will hike rates at its next policy meeting in November. Says traders still see strong chance hike will be delivered, taking cash rate target to 4.75% from 4.50%. December 3-year bonds at 95.10, up 1 bp. Expected range 95.08 to 95.11. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2144 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD remains on track for parity by year end as RBA looks likely to hike rates by then, China's growth rate has re-accelerated and the U.S. Fed looks likely to restart quantitative easing, says NAB in a market commentary. Notes that when U.S. Fed first implemented quantitative easing in March 2009, the U.S. Dollar index dived by almost 8% before retracing half this decline and then plunging to fresh lows. Adds, this time, U.S. Dollar index has already fallen almost 8% on quantitative easing expectations but some further fall in the USD on the Fed announcing QE in November likely, although the size of QE will be important to the USD's prospects. AUD/USD now 0.9829. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2137 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat faces downward pressure from a fall in U.S. wheat prices, which stumbled overnight on a lack of fresh supportive fundamental news, despite influence of soaring corn prices. Wheat doesn't share same bullish fundamentals that propelled corn and soybean futures, leaving market in an overbought situation once corn eased back from an initial push to market's exchange-imposed daily trading limits. Most-active Chicago Board of Trade December wheat fell 10 cents, or 1.4%, to US$7.0925/bushel. AUD/USD last 0.9834, near lower end of yesterday's trading range, down from yesterday's high at 0.9908. On Monday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales soared A$22 to A$276/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia leapt A$17.50 to A$308.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2134 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD still has some upside but the turn is nigh, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in a market commentary. Says positioning has become an issue with IMM data showing the USD short position has reached 30 billion. Adds, at one point there will be short squeeze, which could be triggered by several factors such as USD/JPY supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the release of stronger-than-expected U.S. data or an agreement between the U.S. and China on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Says European money market rates have been rising as banks reduce demand for ECB funds. Hawkish ECB talk has also intensified amid speculation about inflation. EUR/USD now 1.3874. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 1907ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101011e6ab000bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
