\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Weekend Mtgs Have Little Impact On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkts - \uc2 HIFX\b0\par\par\uc2 1,765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 01:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] Raft of meetings at weekend not having much bearing on EUR, JPY as they march higher vs USD, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HIFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  senior trader Stuart Ive. Points to "fair degree of choppiness in market" so far today but largely due to same story as past several weeks, with USD taking beating as market pricing in further QE in U.S. "Has the IMF, World Bank, G-7 changed anything? No." Expects increasing chatter about impact of high EUR/USD on European exports, says market keenly focused on USD/JPY and whether Japanese opt to intevene around 80.00 mark. EUR/USD last 1.3995, tips minor resistance at 1.4030, support around 1.3940. USD/JPY last 81.87, tips support around 80.80 although notes it's in uncharted territory, resistance at 82.30. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.5% at 4705.2, hit fresh 5-month high of 4713.0, with miners and banks leading broad-based gains in cyclical stocks. Follows strength in U.S. equities, commodity prices and Australian dollar as weak U.S. jobs data fuelled expectations of quantitative easing gains. Traders generally expect further gains above the 4700 resistance level this week. "It doesn't seem to matter what economic news comes out in the U.S. because the market is fixated on quantitative easing," says RBS Morgans investment adviser Danny Dreyfus. He says offshore views on the Australian dollar will be key for equities this week. "I think the biggest issue that offshore investors have at the moment is the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Do they plough back into the Aussie market, having pulled out when the resources super profits tax was proposed and the Aussie dollar was 10% or 15% lower?" Among miners, Fortescue (FMG.AU) up 5.3% on debt restructure, while BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) up 0.8%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.1% after LME copper rose 2.6%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 1.9% as gold rose 1.6%, and Alumina (AWC.AU) up 3.3% after aluminum rose 4.0% and \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  rose 5.7% on Wall Street. Defensive sectors lagging, with Woolworths (WOW.AU) down 2.2%. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 2219 GMT, misstated the EUR/USD's current and Friday levels. The correct version is as follows:) ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely lifted by Wall Street gains Friday; worse-than-expected payrolls data heightened expectations of more quantitative easing from Fed, cheering market. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, investors on guard for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities after USD/JPY touches new 15-year low in early Asia, though Japan markets shut for Health-Sports Day, should curtail trading volumes in Asian session, possibly make for more volatility. USD/JPY at 81.46 vs 82.08 in late NY trade Friday; EUR/JPY at 114.40 vs 114.33, EUR/USD at 1.3987 vs 1.3929. On data slate: Australia housing finance at 0030 GMT, Malaysia manufacturing sales, industrial production at 0400 GMT. Later there's France industrial production, Italy industrial production, UK RICS housing market survey, BRC-KPMG retail sales monitor, New York Fed president Dudley speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely to remain within narrow ranges in subdued trading amid dearth of leads. Last week, benchmark MGSs barely budged, with yields between flat to 2 bps higher for week. "We don't foresee much action in the market in the early part of this week as players will be mostly waiting for cues from the auction and the budget," dealer says, referring to September 2017 MGS auction Thursday, release of government's 2011 budget Friday. Last Friday, August 2015 MGS ended at yields of 3.25%, September 2017 at 3.55%, November 2019 at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] Friday's HK short-selling volume nearly same as Thursday's at HK$4.964 billion, as percentage of total market volume tad lower at 5.7% vs Thursday's 6.1%, suggesting bears still largely on sidelines, amid HSI's 22nd session of gain out of 26. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) in top spot with HK$794.04 million, as stock set another more than 2-year high Friday, followed by China Life (2628.HK) with HK$302.05 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$265.94 million. Tencent (0700.HK), HKEx (0388.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$181.25 million, HK$167.89 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD will be back on its familiar down slope in Asia after IMF meeting over weekend failed to address any of the lingering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market concerns, says Phil Burke, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 ; "It's a case of carry on selling the U.S. dollar." Weak payrolls data now has market comfortable with idea of imminent fresh round of QE by Fed. Says weakness in USD/JPY (fresh 15-year low of 81.40) adds to likelihood of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds if it was not a holiday in Tokyo, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a near certainty. Sees USD/JPY trading in 81.30 to 82.30 range during Asian day. Now 81.82. EUR/USD to trade between 1.3950 and 1.4040. Now 1.3997. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to fall vs 1,120.3 late Friday in Seoul as USD continued to slip vs major rivals late last week on prevailing view Fed will opt for extra easing measures in near term. Spot tipped to open lower after 1-month NDFs last end around 1,115.5 in NY. "The dollar is likely to remain broadly weak for a while, with positive U.S. economic data having a positive impact (on risk appetite) in markets, while negative data boosts expectations for quantitative easing," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,105-1,120 range with downward bias, though speed of pair's fall likely moderated by high chance of USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi may rise on back of positive U.S. leads Friday on hopes Fed will introduce additional stimulus to boost economic growth. But Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo says market gains likely limited ahead of Intel 3Q results, BOK rates decision due later this week; "The market moves have been liquidity-based, and we're starting to see some weakening in foreign inflows; there have been some cyclical buying of Asian markets recently and we may see the foreign investors' momentum weaken for South Korea." Tips 1900-1910 range for day vs 1897.07 close Friday. \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) may rise on report that \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra Ltd\uc2 . (500520.BY) plans to complete acquisition of South Korean automaker by December or January, while securities plays like \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE) might gain on hopes that stronger market sentiment will boost their earnings ahead. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares inching into positive territory, last +0.1% at 3236.41. \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley says market largely taking weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data in stride; "There is further consolidation today, with no-one pushing things one way or the other. There is no conviction either way from investors in New Zealand today." Says market may "get dragged up as the afternoon goes on" if Aussie markets ticks higher. Bellwether Telecom flat at NZ$2.05, \uc2 Pike River Coal\uc2  (PRC.NZ) +4.4% at NZ$1.18 as investors cheered by news hydro mining getting underway, \uc2 NZ Oil & Gas\uc2  +0.8% at NZ$1.33 after news Pateke-3H well resumes production. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] Heading into LME week, limelight shifts to base metals, which appear in fine form, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says in weekly note Saturday. "Demand has been much stronger than the headline economic data suggest, accompanied by a slowdown in supply reflected most acutely in tin, which has hit all-time highs," it says. Copper supplies extremely tight with almost no growth in global mine output this year, while rising costs also contributing to slower supply growth, it says. Meanwhile, powerful gain in copper price on Friday suggests upbeat market participants remain in control, overwhelming the many skeptics who materialized on Thursday, Triland Metals said Saturday, noting market volatility usually high in LME week. But another major market participant, which always requests anonymity, raises question about sustainability of base metals uptrend. Base metals jumped Friday as USD fell, plumbing a 15-year low against JPY, with decrease in inventory levels in LME-approved warehouses also providing some support to prices. "One question to consider, however, is how much are base metals rallying on the back of the USD versus true fundamental supply and demand reasons? Is the current rally to such high levels sustainable?" LME 3-month copper surged $215 on day to settle Friday at $8,310/metric ton, while tin soared $850 to $26,350, nickel $500 to $24,400, lead $63 to $2,272, zinc $29 to $2,290, and aluminum $95 to $2,420. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia August industrial output likely +5.0% on-year vs July's +3.2%, according to median forecast of 11 economists polled by Dow Jones; but economists say broader trend of slowing growth still intact. Joanna Tan, an economist at Forecast in Singapore, says with rising uncertainties about global economic growth, moderation in growth in industrial production likely to continue. Growth in manufacturing component will continue to slow in tandem with exports, which will also weigh on electricity production component, says Affin Investment Bank chief economist Alan Tan. Data expected 0401 GMT.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] Lack of any specific official criticism from IMF meetings over weekend of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the likelihood of a further round of JPY selling should it continue to appreciate, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says in a market commentary. Targets USD/JPY at 85.00 in one month and three month horizon. Now 81.80. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 10-10-10 1958ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101010e6aa00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
