\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD Likely On Familiar Downward Slope Again - JPM\b0\par\par\uc2 2,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 01:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD will be back on its familiar down slope in Asia after IMF meeting over weekend failed to address any of the lingering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market concerns, says Phil Burke, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; "It's a case of carry on selling the U.S. dollar." Weak payrolls data now has market comfortable with idea of imminent fresh round of QE by Fed. Says weakness in USD/JPY (fresh 15-year low of 81.40) adds to likelihood of BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Adds if it was not a holiday in Tokyo, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be a near certainty. Sees USD/JPY trading in 81.30 to 82.30 range during Asian day. Now 81.82. EUR/USD to trade between 1.3950 and 1.4040. Now 1.3997. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to fall vs 1,120.3 late Friday in Seoul as USD continued to slip vs major rivals late last week on prevailing view Fed will opt for extra easing measures in near term. Spot tipped to open lower after 1-month NDFs last end around 1,115.5 in NY. "The dollar is likely to remain broadly weak for a while, with positive U.S. economic data having a positive impact (on risk appetite) in markets, while negative data boosts expectations for quantitative easing," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,105-1,120 range with downward bias, though speed of pair's fall likely moderated by high chance of USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2338 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi may rise on back of positive U.S. leads Friday on hopes Fed will introduce additional stimulus to boost economic growth. But Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo says market gains likely limited ahead of Intel 3Q results, BOK rates decision due later this week; "The market moves have been liquidity-based, and we're starting to see some weakening in foreign inflows; there have been some cyclical buying of Asian markets recently and we may see the foreign investors' momentum weaken for South Korea." Tips 1900-1910 range for day vs 1897.07 close Friday. \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) may rise on report that \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra Ltd\uc2 . (500520.BY) plans to complete acquisition of South Korean automaker by December or January, while securities plays like \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE) might gain on hopes that stronger market sentiment will boost their earnings ahead. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares inching into positive territory, last +0.1% at 3236.41. \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley says market largely taking weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data in stride; "There is further consolidation today, with no-one pushing things one way or the other. There is no conviction either way from investors in New Zealand today." Says market may "get dragged up as the afternoon goes on" if Aussie markets ticks higher. Bellwether Telecom flat at NZ$2.05, \uc2 Pike River Coal\uc2  (PRC.NZ) +4.4% at NZ$1.18 as investors cheered by news hydro mining getting underway, \uc2 NZ Oil & Gas\uc2  +0.8% at NZ$1.33 after news Pateke-3H well resumes production. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] Heading into LME week, limelight shifts to base metals, which appear in fine form, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says in weekly note Saturday. "Demand has been much stronger than the headline economic data suggest, accompanied by a slowdown in supply reflected most acutely in tin, which has hit all-time highs," it says. Copper supplies extremely tight with almost no growth in global mine output this year, while rising costs also contributing to slower supply growth, it says. Meanwhile, powerful gain in copper price on Friday suggests upbeat market participants remain in control, overwhelming the many skeptics who materialized on Thursday, Triland Metals said Saturday, noting market volatility usually high in LME week. But another major market participant, which always requests anonymity, raises question about sustainability of base metals uptrend. Base metals jumped Friday as USD fell, plumbing a 15-year low against JPY, with decrease in inventory levels in LME-approved warehouses also providing some support to prices. "One question to consider, however, is how much are base metals rallying on the back of the USD versus true fundamental supply and demand reasons? Is the current rally to such high levels sustainable?" LME 3-month copper surged $215 on day to settle Friday at $8,310/metric ton, while tin soared $850 to $26,350, nickel $500 to $24,400, lead $63 to $2,272, zinc $29 to $2,290, and aluminum $95 to $2,420. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia August industrial output likely +5.0% on-year vs July's +3.2%, according to median forecast of 11 economists polled by Dow Jones; but economists say broader trend of slowing growth still intact. Joanna Tan, an economist at Forecast in Singapore, says with rising uncertainties about global economic growth, moderation in growth in industrial production likely to continue. Growth in manufacturing component will continue to slow in tandem with exports, which will also weigh on electricity production component, says Affin Investment Bank chief economist Alan Tan. Data expected 0401 GMT.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] Lack of any specific official criticism from IMF meetings over weekend of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the likelihood of a further round of JPY selling should it continue to appreciate, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says in a market commentary. Targets USD/JPY at 85.00 in one month and three month horizon. Now 81.80. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2252 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to follow bullish lead of Wall Street, commodity prices and Australian dollar after weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data Friday fuelled expectations of U.S. quantitative easing when Fed meets Nov. 2/3. IG Markets tips 0.4% opening rise in S&P/ASX 200 to 4699, with materials and energy sectors expected to lead gains on offshore peer strength. On the charts, a sustained break of major resistance at 4700 (61.8% retracement of April-May fall) could trigger a rise to the April peak at 5025, which is also the inverse head & shoulders pattern target, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. The bulk of any rise to 5025 may occur before the next FOMC meeting, after which time a "sell-on fact" reaction to any quantitative easing is possible. In the short-term, there's some chance of a bearish 5-point reversal pattern forming, with resistance from that pattern projected at 4720, which is also upper Bollinger band resistance. If the index makes a new 5-month high above 4698 today but closes below that level, profit-taking could see last week's low at 4559.0 tested. A break of that level would trigger the 5-point reversal, which could generate a more significant drop. That could occur if offshore investors take profit as AUD/USD rises toward parity. But it's premature to predict such a move just yet. Index last 4681.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold $1,353.85/oz, up $6.90 early today, driven by weaker USD. ScotiaMocatta's chart analysis shows gold last week posted its ninth up-week in past 10 weeks, in a move that saw it run to $1,365 from $1,157. "There are no reversal signals on the weekly chart so the bias remains for another push higher," with $1,320, $1,312 as important supports, bank says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Queensland government to test investor appetite for IPOs by offering 1.464 billion-1.684 billion QR National shares at A$2.50-A$3.00, giving company a market value of A$6.1 billion-A$7.32 billion, with an expected additional A$500 million of drawn debt. QR National has released ambitious targets, forecasting EBITDA to grow to A$894 million this FY, from A$628 million in FY10, and to A$1.10 billion in FY12, although dividends will be a more modest 3.7c/share in FY11, 8.3c in FY12. The rail haulage company is pinning its growth prospects on sustained demand for coal from fast-developing Asian nations like China. FY12 earnings forecast gives company enterprise value-to-EBITDA ratio of 6.0-7.1 times, which is a discount to valuation of competitor Asciano (AIO.AU), which, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 , is trading at FY12 EV/EBITDA ratio of 7.4 times. That discount, plus incentives to retail investors, who will pay no more than A$2.80 a share and receive 10 cent discount to price paid by institutional investors, may help ease concerns float will struggle to overcome recent aversion to IPOs, with department store Myer (MYR.AU) losing value immediately after floating in November and termination of construction company Valemus float in July. Last biggest company to float on Australia's stock exchange was \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) and its shares hit a record low last week. (ross.kelly@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2220 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.5% at 4717.0 after weak U.S. jobs data fuelled expectations of quantitative easing in the U.S. DJIA up 0.5% at 11006.48, S&P 500 up 0.6% at 1165.15, Nasdaq up 0.8% at 2401.91. Over the weekend, IMF/G7 meetings failed to resolve conflict over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Fortescue Metals\uc2  signed a US$2.04 billion corporate bank facility. On Friday, U.S. September nonfarm payrolls fell 95,000 vs 10,000 fall expected, and private sector jobs rose just 64,000 while jobless rate remained at 9.6%. S&P 500 Financials up 0.1%, with KBW Bank index down 0.2%. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  fell 1.0% after placing a moratorium on foreclosures. S&P 500 Materials up 2.0%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 5.7% on strong 3Q revenue and improved 2010 alumina demand forecast. Freeport up 4.5%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  up 3.3%, Barrick Gold up 1.9%. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$41.41 vs local close at A$41.00. London-listed BHP up 1.8%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.8%. S&P 500 Industrials up 0.8%, with Caterpillar up 2.1%. S&P 500 Energy up 1.1%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 1.1%, with \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  up 1.8%. Spot gold at US$1,346.85 vs US$1,333.00. Nymex crude oil up 1.2% at US$82.66. LME copper up 2.6%, zinc up 1.5%, aluminum up 4.0%, nickel up 2.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  [ 10-10-10 1942ET ]\par \par \uc2 markets, EUR/USD at 1.3992 vs 1.3926 this time Friday, AUD/USD 0.9894 vs 0.9826. S&P/ASX 200 last 4681.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2219 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely lifted by Wall Street gains Friday; worse-than-expected payrolls data heightened expectations of more quantitative easing from Fed, cheering market. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, investors on guard for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities after USD/JPY touches new 15-year low in early Asia, though Japan markets shut for Health-Sports Day, should curtail trading volumes in Asian session, possibly make for more volatility. USD/JPY at 81.46 vs 82.08 in late NY trade Friday; EUR/JPY at 114.40 vs 114.33, EUR/USD at vs 1.3929 vs 1.3987. On data slate: Australia housing finance at 0030 GMT, Malaysia manufacturing sales, industrial production at 0400 GMT. Later there's France industrial production, Italy industrial production, UK RICS housing market survey, BRC-KPMG retail sales monitor, New York Fed president Dudley speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 10-10-10 1942ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101010e6aa00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
