\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Bashers Pause As Payrolls Data Loom\b0\par\par\uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 11:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Dollar drubbing took a breather in European trading hours Friday as traders braced for the key U.S. employment data due later in the day--a figure seen as crucial in determining the fate of U.S. monetary policy and the greenback over the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The non-farm payrolls reading, due at 1230 GMT, is always a highlight of the monthly flow of U.S. economic data. But right now, with the dollar suffering persistently heavy selling across the board, they have assumed even greater weight.\par \par \uc2 A signal that the U.S. jobs market is in better shape than feared could scupper many investors' near-certain expectations for further monetary easing from the Federal Reserve--which have been hammering the buck for much of this week. A weak reading is likely to add further momentum to the dollar's decline. A big surprise in either direction could prompt a violent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market reaction.\par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires poll of economists forecasts that the September U.S. non-farm payrolls report will show a 10,000 drop in the number of jobs, after a slide of 54,000 jobs in August.\par \par \uc2 "I think either way--a really good number or a really bad number--there would be a big reaction," said Daragh Maher, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Credit Agricole in London.\par \par \uc2 A surprisingly strong reading that pushed the buck higher against the yen could even, somewhat counter-intuitively, spark a fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, as the Japanese authorities, who have recently held back from a yen-weakening sting despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resurgence, piggyback on a broader move.\par \par \uc2 Those expecting the Bank of Japan, on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf, to buy dollars at the Y82.90 area, as it did Sept. 15, have been disappointed. But few think that the Japanese authorities have given up.\par \par \uc2 "If the dollar jumped against the yen after payrolls, the MOF could come in and get a really massive run up," Maher said.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the data release, this kind of uncertainty kept trading muted, with little movement for major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen moved little from the Y82.30 area against the dollar, while the euro hovered just above $1.39 for most of the session.\par \par \uc2 In earlier Asian trading, the yuan rose to its strongest level against the dollar since it began trading regularly in 1994. The gains were made as China's seven-day national holiday ended and as the country's trading partners stepped up their calls for yuan appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity, a daily reference rate, at a fresh low of 6.6830 Friday, down from 6.7011 Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 The other focal point was Australia, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Deputy Governor Ric Battellino said "higher interest rates will be required" if "economic conditions evolve as currently expected," suggesting that the monetary tightening cycle remains in place despite the decision to hold monetary policy steady earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar fell against the greenback despite the hawkish comments, nudging below $0.9750 against its U.S. counterpart for a short time after reaching a new post-float high of $0.9920 during Thursday's session. This pullback might only be temporary particularly if the U.S. data disappoint.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar is a "natural beneficiary" of quantitative easing-induced U.S. dollar weakness, said \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Chief Economist Bill Evans, noting the Australian unit has risen around 11% since early September, "almost all" of which can be attributed to weakness in the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Sterling was little changed against the dollar, showing no reaction to slightly stronger than expected U.K annual producer price inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Office for National Statistics said factory gate prices rose 4.4% on the year, weaker than the 4.7% gain in August, but stronger than the 4.3% forecast by economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires last week.\par \par \uc2 At 0918 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.3906, compared with $1.3912 late in New York Thursday, according to trading system EBS. The dollar was little changed at Y82.34 against the yen, from Y82.40, while the euro was at Y114.55 from Y114.65. The pound was at $1.5873 from $1.5862.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 77.457 from 77.464 in New York.\par \par \uc2 -By Clare Connaghan, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 20 7842 9496, clare.connaghan@dowjones.com (Rachel Pannett in Canberra contributed to this article.) [ 08-10-10 0939GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101008e6a80010n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
