\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Dollar Hovers Near Low Vs Yen On Weak US Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 22:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2004 GMT [Dow Jones] Dollar weakens against its major rivals after a weak US jobs report lends credence to expectations the Fed will launch a new stimulus program. Greenback dropped to a series of 15-year lows against the yen, weakening as far as Y81.72 and putting investors on alert for signs of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; traders said there were no signs of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market. Though in the initial wake of the jobs numbers the dollar fell steeply against the euro, it recouped its losses after an EU official talked down the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength. The euro emerged from volatile trading to remain virtually unchanged on the day after having knocked on the door of $1.40. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2001 GMT [Dow Jones] Some market watchers say late-day Treasury futures slide shouldn't be much of a surprise even as traders brace for QE2. That's because the market was so heavily long before Fri's jobs report and traders took profits ahead of the Columbus Day weekend. Dec 10-yr Tsy note futures hit morning contract high of 127-21. Settled at 127-10, up 5+/32, near 2.375% cash yield equivalent. Dec 30-yr Tsy bond futures settled down 4/32 at 134-10, having failed earlier to take out Wed's 1 1/4-month high of 135-12. Dec ultra long Tsy bonds settled 21/32 lower at 140-05. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2000 GMT [Dow Jones] You'd think retail sales would improve as the bulk of the large servicers stop foreclosure proceedings in half of the U.S. Folks who haven't paid their mortgage in this long should, by now, be spending their money on something... but it won't be suitcases until this is sorted. Maybe the Fed should just buy the foreclosed houses right from these homeowners, keep them there, collect rent and become a big property management company instead of trying to move the 10-yr down another 10 BP. Housing assets could then be sold off slowly. Oh wait, they'd be competing with the GSEs, who already own a ton of homes.\par \par \uc2 (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1957 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures prices higher, particularly for 2012 and beyond, as this morning's jobs report pointed to a still anemic jobs market and heightened speculation about quantitative easing. Quarterly 2012 through 2013 contracts settled up 4 to 8 BP. Nearest 1-yr calendar spread continues to narrow, signaling no end in sight to the current low short-term rate environment. At Fri's settlement, Dec 2011 Eurodollar contract priced in a rate only 25 BP above rate priced into Dec 2010 contract. Same spread at 27.5 BP Thurs, and 34 BP exactly one week ago. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1909 GMT [Dow Jones] Credit default swaps on debt issued by \uc2 J.C.Penney\uc2  widen 31.19BP to 248.5BP, according to Markit data. That's a 14.4% day-over-day widening after \uc2 Pershing Square Capital\uc2  Managemen disclosed it had assumed a 16.5% stake in the retailer, an investment now worth more than $1.1 billion. "Free cash flow and financial flexibility remain strong, and we expect debt reduction to remain a top management priority," said Carol Levenson of research firm GimmeCredit in a note. "But the disclosure...changes our view of the credit meaningfully and negatively." (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1904 GMT [Dow Jones] While most market participants assume the Fed will undertake more quantitative easing, some say it is hardly an easy choice. Propping back up the US economy no small feat, and more QE amounts to a "Hail Mary," says Peter Schiff of Euro Pacific Capital. "The Fed now faces the hardest choices in its history. It can either redouble its past efforts to re-inflate America's bubble economy (risking the destruction of the US dollar)," he says. "Or it can stop pumping and let the economy deflate to a self-sustaining level." Either decision seems wrought with peril, says Schiff. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1856 GMT [Dow Jones] Agency mortgage bonds gave back some of their gains from earlier in the day as supply entered into the fray. Slower prepayment on higher coupons have made them popular, and their prices picked up on buying. Risk premiums on the current coupon widened 4 bps to 149 bps, and the yield is at 3.23%. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1813 GMT [Dow Jones] To avoid a prolonged jobless recovery, some countries should continue to stimulate their economies, IMF's Strauss-Kahn said Fri, also warning about the rising risk that the momentum for global cooperation could vanish. Strauss-Kahn speaking on sidelines of the IMF/World Bank annual meetings in a recorded BBC debate. On one of the hottest issues of the IMF meetings, exchange rates, the IMF chief said using \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an economic weapon to ward off the effects of other countries' economic policies could lead to "catastrophe." (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1809 GMT [Dow Jones] The world needs to move toward freer-floating market-based exchange rates, UK Chancellor of the Exchequer George Osborne said Fri. "On \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...I think we do need to move toward market-oriented exchange rates that reflect fundamentals and I think the G-20 and the IMF have a role in...helping to make that come about and addressing other imbalances," Osborne said. "So it is the big topic of discussion here." His remarks underline the extent to which this week's Washington meetings have been overshadowed by the tensions between the US and China over exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1800 GMT [Dow Jones] China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Friday reiterated that the country's exchange-rate reform will be gradual and how fast the yuan would move depends on China's balance of payments condition. Speaking at the BBC debate held on the sideline of the IMF and World Bank annual meetings, PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan also said China needs a market-based exchange rate regime, but he fell short of making predictions on the yuan's direction or China's economic growth. He also avoided a question by the debate host on whether he was feeling "resentment" toward the global pressure on the yuan issue.\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1751 GMT [Dow Jones] The most likely scenario is the Fed starts QE2 in November, BoA Merrill analysts say. The relevant question is no longer if, but what it will look like, the analysts say, and it's effect. BoA expects a program of $500B over the six months, with more if necessary. "In our view, the program will be flexible enough to assure a further decline in rates. As such, we expect the 10-year Treasury rate to fall to 2.0% by the end of this year." (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1649 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were unchanged in NY Fri from Tokyo after the dollar fell against the yen. One-month at-the-money USD/JPY options implied volatilities were at 12.10%/12.80%, unchanged from 12.10%/12.80% in Tokyo and from 11.85%/12.55% Thu in NY. One-month EUR/USD implied vols were at 13.50%/13.75%, from 14.10%/14.35% in Tokyo and from 14.10%/14.35% Thu in NY. One-month EUR/JPY implied vols were at 13.75%/15.75% unchanged from 13.75%/15.75% in Tokyo and from 13.75%/15.75% in NY Thu. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) [ 10-08-10 1604ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a8000e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
