\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/JPY May Retest 82.11 Ahead Of US Payrolls Data\b0\par\par\uc2 1,632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 07:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0516 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test 15-year low of 82.11 several times ahead of U.S. non-farm payrolls data as investors believe USD-weakening trend to persist regardless of single piece of data's result, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Last 82.36, next target in 81.00-81.50 band. Adds, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in that range unlikely due to spreading view that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's MOF may wait until USD/JPY gets closer to 80.00 before stepping in, he says.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] Fixed-income markets have mostly spent last night and today waiting for U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due tonight, says Commonwealth Bank quantitative strategist Philip Brown; "payrolls is on everyone's mind." Aussie bond markets rallied slowly in morning before consolidating in afternoon trade; bonds reacted little to RBA Deputy Governor Ric Battellino's speech, which stuck largely to recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  script. December 3-year bonds now 95.07, up 5bps, December 10-year bonds now 94.98, up 5.5 bps. With so much emphasis lately on slowing U.S. recovery, implications for further quantitative easing, market "could react very strongly" to an unexpected payrolls result, he says. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] No near-term fundamental trigger for India equities to have meaningful upside from current levels, says Macquarie; notes MSCI India index, at 17X forward P/E, significant premium to peers. However, says strong foreign institutional inflows ($15 billion since April 2010) should continue to support market; "we would view any near-term correction as an opportunity to accumulate quality stocks." Expects margins to expand in metals, power; to contract in cement, properties, consumer, autos on high raw material costs. Has Outperform rating on Jindal Steel & Power (532286.BY) with INR968 target, GVK Power (532708.BY) with INR53 target, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY), INR1,290 target. Has Underperform on Ultratech (532538.BY), INR831 target, \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  (500696.BY), INR210 target, \uc2 JSW Energy\uc2  (533148.BY), INR105 target. (raghavendra.upadhyaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD falls sharply after relatively strong trading start in Asia, currently at 1.3042 after rising to 1.3087 earlier; traders say pair rangebound, with greenback weakness to persist. "Notwithstanding small spurts here and there, the U.S. dollar will remain under pressure," trader says, tips 1.3030-1.3080 range ahead of U.S. employment data tonight. Pair touched record low 1.3034 yesterday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.8% at 23,056.69 midday, supported by rise in China markets after resuming trade following a week's break (Shanghai Composite Index +3.3% midday). Alvin Cheung, associate director at Prudential tips consolidation pressure near-term after index set fresh year-to-date high of 23,093.77 earlier today; adds, index may stay in 23,000-23,200 band near-term, but any pullback may see index test 22,000 in this month. HK developers up ahead of land auction Tuesday, with Mirae Asset expecting "another record-breaking auction;" house forecasts Ede Road site to fetch HK$1.507 billion for HK$16,614/GFA, will likely set new record for land prices in Kowloon area. Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +4.2% at HK$121.80, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +2.8% at HK$137.70. Elsewhere, Cnooc (0883.HK) down 1.0% at HK$16.06 after crude prices fall overnight. Volume strong at HK$51.02 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0454 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD a "natural beneficiary" of quantitative easing-induced USD weakness, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Chief Economist Bill Evans; notes pair, last at 0.9835, up around 11% since beginning of September, almost all of which can be attributed to weakness in USD. U.S. Federal Reserve "seems bent on quantitative easing," while RBA, in stark contrast, retains strong tightening bias, is likely to raise rates November 2. At today's rate above 0.9800, AUD only needs to keep pace with fall in USD of 2% to register parity; "that looks extremely likely over the next two months or so: conservative in fact," Evans says. Adds, likely continuation of quantitative easing policies should support AUD through mid-2011, June target of $1.05 "seems reasonable". (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0302 GMT, misstated the EUR/USD's and EUR/JPY's tipped resistances as their supports. The correct version is as follows:) EUR/USD rises to intraday high at 1.3960 from 1.3919 early, EUR/JPY to 114.85 from 114.66 as investors view report of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  placing China's A1 bond rating on review for possible upgrade as positive sign for global economic recovery, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . But believes investors unlikely to pile up large positions ahead of U.S. non-farm payrolls due 1230 GMT, which viewed as key indicator of strength of U.S. economy, therefore will have implications for USD. Tips resistance for EUR/USD at 1.4000 vs last 1.3949, EUR/JPY at 115.20 vs last 114.80. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI flat midday at 1482.01; benchmark index moves in tight range between positive, negative territories in absence of leads. Local dealer says property stocks top gainers (+1.0%) on rotational interest, earnings hopes, outlook. "Like yesterday, the market seems to be trapped in a consolidation phase...we don't think that's likely to change ahead of the weekend," says dealer. Tips 1475-1485 range rest of day. Market breadth marginally positive with 308 advancers vs 299 decliners. UEM Land (5148.KU) +2.1% at MYR2.44, \uc2 IJM Land\uc2  (5215.KU) +3.1% at MYR2.69; Proton (5304.KU) down 1.0% at MYR4.86 after firm recalls some Gen2, Satria Neo models to rectify malfunction. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY narrows losses tad midday from fresh all-time low under current system, as suspected PBOC-guided flows in automatic price matching system supports USD. Pair now at 6.6734 vs 6.6703 earlier, 6.6912 at September 30's close. Two traders says demand for dollar emerges in price matching system when pair approaches 6.6700, which suspected of being guided by PBOC. PBOC usually suspected to offer bid/ask quotes via state-owned banks in rarely used system to send signals. "We have strong desire to short the dollar but we need to watch how the central parity moves," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Central parity at 6.6830 vs 6.7011 September 30 after dollar's sharp losses during China's 7-day National Day holiday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.7% at 3560.341 midday in moderate volume, off earlier low of 3553.757, amid profit-taking by foreign funds in most bank, automotive stocks after recent gains, traders say; tip support at 3530. "Bargain buying by some local institutional funds, however, has lifted the main index from its low amid generally weak sentiment," says trader at BNI Securities. Foreigners so far net sellers of IDR245 billion worth of shares vs IDR224 billion net buyers yesterday. Among decliners, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 3.6% at IDR6,750, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 1.9% at IDR10,500, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 2.2% at IDR57,150 on profit taking. Bucking trend, coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) +2.3% at IDR2,2275 on bargain buying. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] ANZ says concerns about "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war"--as central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' rise vs USD to keep exporters competitive--are "quite valid." Tamara Henderson, bank's head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research, Asia, says at best, such series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "reap transient gains as other countries follow suit," negating initial impact, "or as inflation sets in and negates the impact in real terms. At worst, global trade and capital flows are disrupted and the global economy contracts as a result." Henderson expresses hope policy makers will avert such folly, but says "the reality is that economic policy in many countries is set by politicians beholden to the election cycle and by voters beholden to the state of the business cycle." Notes, scenario could have largest impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  most dependent on trade, capital inflows; in Asia, cites Singapore, Malaysia, Vietnam, Taiwan, Thailand; all with export-to-GDP ratios above 70%. Says INR, VND vulnerable to disruption in capital inflows, with IDR, KRW at risk as well. But adds, increased significance of intra-Asian flows complicate picture; more export-dependent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher export exposures to China, i.e. SGD, TWD, MYR likely to be more sensitive to movements in CNY, "and, hence, come under less pressure to depreciate against the US dollar." (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Usually hawkish, somewhat straight-shooting RBA deputy governor Ric Battellino largely sticking to script in Brisbane speech today "probably betrays moderate uncertainty within the walls of the RBA," says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  strategist Michael Turner. Large chunks of speech lifted directly from either October meeting statement or previous minutes. Still, notes RBA has sharpened tightening bias in recent months, thus RBC continues to expect further 25bps hike this year. See: =UPDATE: RBA Deputy Governor Says Likely Higher Interest Rates Will Be Required (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com) [ 10-08-10 0116ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a80003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
