\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Shares Extend Gains;Property Stocks Rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 05:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend gains as property developers reverse earlier losses. Shanghai Composite Index last +2.8% at 2729.93, tipped to test immediate resistance at 2750. "Shanghai's new tightening package for the real-estate market was less harsh than some had expected as it did not include a property tax," says Zhang Suyu at Dongxing Securities. Shanghai yesterday limits new home buys to 1 per family. Last week, Shenzhen limited households to buying 2 new homes. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Vanke\f28 \uc2 \f2  (000002.SZ) +1.3% at CNY8.51 vs CNY8.15 intraday low; Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) +4.4% at CNY12.90 vs CNY11.98 earlier. Shenzhen Composite Index +2.7% at 1200.28. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] ANZ taking profit on long AUD/SGD trade; "a nice run of profits combined with key topside resistance and significant event risk ahead has prompted us to book profits," says Tamara Henderson, bank's Head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research, Asia. Notes, tonight's U.S. payrolls, weekend's IMF-related meetings "could well make markets twitchy." Bank went long on pair August 1, entered at 1.2257 with target at 1.300, stop at 1.195; exits today at 1.283 with 4.7% realized. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.9% at 3554.867 in light volume, weighed by weaker IDR, losses in regional markets, traders say; tip support at 3530. "Selling by foreign funds has also made things worse," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Foreigners net sellers so far today, unloading IDR63 billion worth of local shares vs IDR224 billion net purchase yesterday. Among decliners: \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 2.9% at IDR6,800, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 1.4% at IDR10,550, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 2.0% at IDR57,250. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.2% at 4683.5, having recovered from 4666.8 intraday low with help from \uc2 Moody's\uc2  comments on China. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  put China's bond rating on review due to economic resilience, citing the "quick, determined and effective" stimulus program. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said upgrade possible if local government off-budget financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are contained and manageable. AUD/USD also reacting positively to \uc2 Moody's\uc2  comments, recovering to 0.9833. S&P/ASX 200 might test major chart resistance at 4700 on these comments, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. However, China's economic recovery is hardly new information--the main focus of China's policy makers this year has been to slow the economy to a sustainable pace. As such, expect S&P/ASX 200 to remain capped at 4700 before the main event, which is tonight's U.S. payrolls report. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD rises to intraday high at 1.3960 from 1.3919 early, EUR/JPY to 114.85 from 114.66 as investors view report of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  placing China's A1 bond rating on review for possible upgrade as positive sign for global economic recovery, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . But believes investors unlikely to pile up large positions ahead of U.S. non-farm payroll due 1230 GMT, which viewed as key indicator of strength of U.S. economy, therefore will have implications for USD. Tips support for EUR/USD at 1.4000 vs last 1.3949, EUR/JPY at 115.20 vs last 114.80. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan exporters, mutual fund sold Y200 million of USD/JPY from 82.30 in past 10 minutes, driving pair as low as 82.15, says Singapore-based dealer; adds, strong buy orders staggered from 82.15 down to 82.00 stalling further USD/JPY losses for now. USD/JPY last 82.27. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off earlier 1,124.0 high following news USD/CNY central parity set at record low Friday, re-igniting hopes for stronger yuan and stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general, traders say. Pair now tad higher at 1,117.8 vs 1,114.5 late yesterday in Seoul. "Offshore players may trim their short dollar positions ahead of the U.S. jobs data later," providing support, but barring USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pair likely to head for lower levels from here, as market remains generally biased towards broadly weaker USD, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,113-1,121 range rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] News Moody's Investors Service has placed China's credit ratings on review for possible upgrade should have little-to-no impact on China's credit, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says Singapore-based analyst. "The news is definitely positive, but I think there are other factors having more relevance in Chinese markets," analyst adds. As main reasons for the decision, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cites resilient performance of the Chinese economy, government's "quick, determined and effective stimulus program, the unwinding of which has begun," and solid credit fundamentals. China's 5-year CDS at 55.9, down from 57 intraday high vs 56.3 close yesterday. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD aroused from a sleepy pre-weekend session by news \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has China's A1 bond rating on review for a possible upgrade. Pair rises from around 0.9800 to 0.9825 after report. China is Australia's biggest export destination and pair highly sensitive to any reports from Asian giant. Greg Gibbs, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS, says news likely gave pair a solid boost. Resistance likely around 0.9850, a former long-term record high for pair. All eyes now on imminent speech by RBA Deputy Governor Battellino amid widespread expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise rates at its next policy meeting in November. Pair now 0.9826. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds may open higher on rise in U.S. Treasurys, but likely to give up gains ahead of INR110 billion auction, inflation concerns, says trader with primary dealership. Finance Minister Mukherjee says in Washington yesterday that high inflation continues to be of concern, but he's "more concerned with inflationary pressures on food items." Adds government's measures to open up importing of food items that are in short supply as well as recent interest rate increases by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would bring inflation down to 6% by FY end. Dealer says "the recent comments from RBI also signal that the hiking cycle is not over." But expects bond auction to be fully subscribed, trading interest in 8.13% 2022 paper (which auction bond). RBI to sell INR40 billion of 7.99% 2017 bond, INR40 billion of 8.13% 2022 and INR30 billion of 8.30% 2040. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0247 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.5% at 464.51, with investors buying on hopes that banks will start lowering interest rates from mid-October to boost liquidity, support companies which are facing borrowing rates above 15%; state-run Vietnam Bank Association asks banks to lower interest rates for VND deposits to below 11% from Oct. 15 earlier in week. "Sentiment is also improving because foreigners have increased their buying of blue chips these days," says Saigon Securities brokerage trader. Adds, foreigners have bought shares valued at VND840 billion month-to-date vs VND220 billion in same period last month. Tips key index in 465-470 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] High chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb JPY strength later in global day, even ahead of G-7 gathering, if U.S. September non-farm payrolls (due 1230 GMT) turn out considerably worse than expected and a USD/JPY break below 80.00 comes into sight, writes Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Japan for Barclays Bank. View exists among Tokyo traders that it's difficult for Japan to act before G-7 gathering in Washington, but Yamamoto says that G-7 gathering now less important than before because power shifting to G-20, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is common among member countries. "As long as (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) stays within the realm of competitive 'non-appreciation', and does not become simple 'competitive devaluation', that would not cause a huge problem." Adds players will likely trim their USD/JPY short positions before 3-day weekend for Tokyo market; "the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective if that is done when the dollar is bouncing in the market's natural tide." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 2311ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a80002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
