\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Recovers As \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Praises China\b0\par\par\uc2 1,533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 05:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.2% at 4683.5, having recovered from 4666.8 intraday low with help from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  comments on China. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  put China's bond rating on review due to economic resilience, citing the "quick, determined and effective" stimulus program. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  said upgrade possible if local government off-budget financial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are contained and manageable. AUD/USD also reacting positively to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  comments, recovering to 0.9833. S&P/ASX 200 might test major chart resistance at 4700 on these comments, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. However, China's economic recovery is hardly new information--the main focus of China's policy makers this year has been to slow the economy to a sustainable pace. As such, expect S&P/ASX 200 to remain capped at 4700 before the main event, which is tonight's U.S. payrolls report. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD rises to intraday high at 1.3960 from 1.3919 early, EUR/JPY to 114.85 from 114.66 as investors view report of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  placing China's A1 bond rating on review for possible upgrade as positive sign for global economic recovery, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . But believes investors unlikely to pile up large positions ahead of U.S. non-farm payroll due 1230 GMT, which viewed as key indicator of strength of U.S. economy, therefore will have implications for USD. Tips support for EUR/USD at 1.4000 vs last 1.3949, EUR/JPY at 115.20 vs last 114.80. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan exporters, mutual fund sold Y200 million of USD/JPY from 82.30 in past 10 minutes, driving pair as low as 82.15, says Singapore-based dealer; adds, strong buy orders staggered from 82.15 down to 82.00 stalling further USD/JPY losses for now. USD/JPY last 82.27. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off earlier 1,124.0 high following news USD/CNY central parity set at record low Friday, re-igniting hopes for stronger yuan and stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general, traders say. Pair now tad higher at 1,117.8 vs 1,114.5 late yesterday in Seoul. "Offshore players may trim their short dollar positions ahead of the U.S. jobs data later," providing support, but barring USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pair likely to head for lower levels from here, as market remains generally biased towards broadly weaker USD, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,113-1,121 range rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] News Moody's Investors Service has placed China's credit ratings on review for possible upgrade should have little-to-no impact on China's credit, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says Singapore-based analyst. "The news is definitely positive, but I think there are other factors having more relevance in Chinese markets," analyst adds. As main reasons for the decision, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cites resilient performance of the Chinese economy, government's "quick, determined and effective stimulus program, the unwinding of which has begun," and solid credit fundamentals. China's 5-year CDS at 55.9, down from 57 intraday high vs 56.3 close yesterday. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD aroused from a sleepy pre-weekend session by news \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has China's A1 bond rating on review for a possible upgrade. Pair rises from around 0.9800 to 0.9825 after report. China is Australia's biggest export destination and pair highly sensitive to any reports from Asian giant. Greg Gibbs, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS, says news likely gave pair a solid boost. Resistance likely around 0.9850, a former long-term record high for pair. All eyes now on imminent speech by RBA Deputy Governor Battellino amid widespread expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise rates at its next policy meeting in November. Pair now 0.9826. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds may open higher on rise in U.S. Treasurys, but likely to give up gains ahead of INR110 billion auction, inflation concerns, says trader with primary dealership. Finance Minister Mukherjee says in Washington yesterday that high inflation continues to be of concern, but he's "more concerned with inflationary pressures on food items." Adds government's measures to open up importing of food items that are in short supply as well as recent interest rate increases by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would bring inflation down to 6% by FY end. Dealer says "the recent comments from RBI also signal that the hiking cycle is not over." But expects bond auction to be fully subscribed, trading interest in 8.13% 2022 paper (which auction bond). RBI to sell INR40 billion of 7.99% 2017 bond, INR40 billion of 8.13% 2022 and INR30 billion of 8.30% 2040. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0247 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.5% at 464.51, with investors buying on hopes that banks will start lowering interest rates from mid-October to boost liquidity, support companies which are facing borrowing rates above 15%; state-run Vietnam Bank Association asks banks to lower interest rates for VND deposits to below 11% from Oct. 15 earlier in week. "Sentiment is also improving because foreigners have increased their buying of blue chips these days," says Saigon Securities brokerage trader. Adds, foreigners have bought shares valued at VND840 billion month-to-date vs VND220 billion in same period last month. Tips key index in 465-470 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] High chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb JPY strength later in global day, even ahead of G-7 gathering, if U.S. September non-farm payrolls (due 1230 GMT) turn out considerably worse than expected and a USD/JPY break below 80.00 comes into sight, writes Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Japan for Barclays Bank. View exists among Tokyo traders that it's difficult for Japan to act before G-7 gathering in Washington, but Yamamoto says that G-7 gathering now less important than before because power shifting to G-20, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is common among member countries. "As long as (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) stays within the realm of competitive 'non-appreciation', and does not become simple 'competitive devaluation', that would not cause a huge problem." Adds players will likely trim their USD/JPY short positions before 3-day weekend for Tokyo market; "the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective if that is done when the dollar is bouncing in the market's natural tide." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tad lower, with profit-takers starting to hold sway in market; benchmark PSEi off 0.3 to 4230.69 in moderate volume. Market breadth negative with decliners beating gainers 62 to 46. Among losers, PLDT (TEL.PH) down 1.1% at PHP2,730, property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH) off 3.3% at PHP2.34, Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH) down 0.5% at PHP65.00. Still, Grace Cerdena, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia.com, says excess liquidity as well as foreign fund inflows will likely lend support. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,330.60/oz, down $3 since NY close after dropping 1.2% overnight on a long-awaited bout of profit taking. Gold could drift lower through the day with gold buyers likely absent ahead of U.S. September non-farm payrolls at 1230 GMT, says Phillip Futures. "After the sudden tumble in gold prices yesterday, investors may be hesitant to enter new long positions ahead of the key U.S. non-farm payrolls report," says report. Dow Jones Survey expects drop of 10,000 in September payrolls vs 54,000 lost in August; tips unemployment rate to edge up to 9.7% from 9.6% in August. "Should there be any upside surprises in the data, this may cause a surge in the dollar, exerting further downward pressure on gold," says Phillip. Broker tips support for gold at $1,320. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil falls on overnight USD strength, profit-taking, as market awaits U.S. September employment report due later in global day, according to analysts; job data "will set the tone for a number of sessions to come...(it) will be the definitive employment figure," advisory firm Cameron Hanover says; while forecast picture bearish, last week's reduced first-time claims data surprise on upside. Meanwhile, "dollar is the key short-term factor and the reinstatement of the linkage with the dollar will stay for a while yet," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . November Nymex crude down 23 cents at $81.44/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 2302ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a800023\par }\page {\pa