ises in the data, this may cause a surge in the dollar, exerting further downward pressure on gold," says Phillip. Broker tips support for gold at $1,320. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil falls on overnight USD strength, profit-taking, as market awaits U.S. September employment report due later in global day, according to analysts; job data "will set the tone for a number of sessions to come...(it) will be the definitive employment figure," advisory firm Cameron Hanover says; while forecast picture bearish, last week's reduced first-time claims data surprise on upside. Meanwhile, "dollar is the key short-term factor and the reinstatement of the linkage with the dollar will stay for a while yet," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . November Nymex crude down 23 cents at $81.44/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.2% at 3228.77, likely to drift sideways in "lackluster" market after interest in stock exchange's new dairy futures market disappoints, says First NZ Capital broker James Lee; "the market was looking for a few trades on the new dairy futures market and so far we are nought for nought." The first cash-settled dairy futures market launched by exchange at 1900 GMT yesterday but no bids have been made. "Until we get a trade and it all settles it is all theoretical." Largest stock Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) remains flat at NZ$8.05 as market lacks cues. Lee says \uc2 DNZ Property Fund\uc2  +0.9% at NZ$1.14 after entering NZX-50; "it shouldn't be a surprise (it's in the index), the market was looking for that sometime this month." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 2302ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a800022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
