\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/USD Up On \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Review Of China Bond Rating\b0\par\par\uc2 1,652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 04:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD aroused from a sleepy pre-weekend session by news \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  has China's A1 bond rating on review for a possible upgrade. Pair rises from around 0.9800 to 0.9825 after report. China is Australia's biggest export destination and pair highly sensitive to any reports from Asian giant. Greg Gibbs, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at RBS, says news likely gave pair a solid boost. Resistance likely around 0.9850, a former long-term record high for pair. All eyes now on imminent speech by RBA Deputy Governor Battellino amid widespread expectations \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will raise rates at its next policy meeting in November. Pair now 0.9826. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds may open higher on rise in U.S. Treasurys, but likely to give up gains ahead of INR110 billion auction, inflation concerns, says trader with primary dealership. Finance Minister Mukherjee says in Washington yesterday that high inflation continues to be of concern, but he's "more concerned with inflationary pressures on food items." Adds government's measures to open up importing of food items that are in short supply as well as recent interest rate increases by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would bring inflation down to 6% by FY end. Dealer says "the recent comments from RBI also signal that the hiking cycle is not over." But expects bond auction to be fully subscribed, trading interest in 8.13% 2022 paper (which auction bond). RBI to sell INR40 billion of 7.99% 2017 bond, INR40 billion of 8.13% 2022 and INR30 billion of 8.30% 2040. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0247 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.5% at 464.51, with investors buying on hopes that banks will start lowering interest rates from mid-October to boost liquidity, support companies which are facing borrowing rates above 15%; state-run Vietnam Bank Association asks banks to lower interest rates for VND deposits to below 11% from Oct. 15 earlier in week. "Sentiment is also improving because foreigners have increased their buying of blue chips these days," says Saigon Securities brokerage trader. Adds, foreigners have bought shares valued at VND840 billion month-to-date vs VND220 billion in same period last month. Tips key index in 465-470 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] High chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb JPY strength later in global day, even ahead of G-7 gathering, if U.S. September non-farm payrolls (due 1230 GMT) turn out considerably worse than expected and a USD/JPY break below 80.00 comes into sight, writes Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Japan for Barclays Bank. View exists among Tokyo traders that it's difficult for Japan to act before G-7 gathering in Washington, but Yamamoto says that G-7 gathering now less important than before because power shifting to G-20, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is common among member countries. "As long as (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) stays within the realm of competitive 'non-appreciation', and does not become simple 'competitive devaluation', that would not cause a huge problem." Adds players will likely trim their USD/JPY short positions before 3-day weekend for Tokyo market; "the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective if that is done when the dollar is bouncing in the market's natural tide." (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tad lower, with profit-takers starting to hold sway in market; benchmark PSEi off 0.3 to 4230.69 in moderate volume. Market breadth negative with decliners beating gainers 62 to 46. Among losers, PLDT (TEL.PH) down 1.1% at PHP2,730, property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH) off 3.3% at PHP2.34, Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH) down 0.5% at PHP65.00. Still, Grace Cerdena, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia.com, says excess liquidity as well as foreign fund inflows will likely lend support. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,330.60/oz, down $3 since NY close after dropping 1.2% overnight on a long-awaited bout of profit taking. Gold could drift lower through the day with gold buyers likely absent ahead of U.S. September non-farm payrolls at 1230 GMT, says Phillip Futures. "After the sudden tumble in gold prices yesterday, investors may be hesitant to enter new long positions ahead of the key U.S. non-farm payrolls report," says report. Dow Jones Survey expects drop of 10,000 in September payrolls vs 54,000 lost in August; tips unemployment rate to edge up to 9.7% from 9.6% in August. "Should there be any upside surprises in the data, this may cause a surge in the dollar, exerting further downward pressure on gold," says Phillip. Broker tips support for gold at $1,320. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil falls on overnight USD strength, profit-taking, as market awaits U.S. September employment report due later in global day, according to analysts; job data "will set the tone for a number of sessions to come...(it) will be the definitive employment figure," advisory firm Cameron Hanover says; while forecast picture bearish, last week's reduced first-time claims data surprise on upside. Meanwhile, "dollar is the key short-term factor and the reinstatement of the linkage with the dollar will stay for a while yet," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . November Nymex crude down 23 cents at $81.44/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.2% at 3228.77, likely to drift sideways in "lackluster" market after interest in stock exchange's new dairy futures market disappoints, says First NZ Capital broker James Lee; "the market was looking for a few trades on the new dairy futures market and so far we are nought for nought." The first cash-settled dairy futures market launched by exchange at 1900 GMT yesterday but no bids have been made. "Until we get a trade and it all settles it is all theoretical." Largest stock Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) remains flat at NZ$8.05 as market lacks cues. Lee says \uc2 DNZ Property Fund\uc2  +0.9% at NZ$1.14 after entering NZX-50; "it shouldn't be a surprise (it's in the index), the market was looking for that sometime this month." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI stubbornly refuses to correct, +0.2% at 22,929.19, rising for 22nd session out of 26, as A-shares' gains after week-long holiday support. Still, Taifook Securities tips "a meaningful correction" may soon appear, although given that ultra-low interest rates in developed countries fuelling massive fund flows into emerging countries, any downward adjustment in local market "may prove minor and temporary." Mainland developers falling after Shanghai's latest property tightening measures; China Overseas Land (0688.HK) down 1.4% at HK$16.64, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 2.3% at HK$15.84. Market volume remains solid at HK$10.80 billion; psychological 23,000 remains immediate cap. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9646.59 midday as investors shy away from exporters on weak USD/JPY (now 82.36) and global cyclical stocks before release of U.S. jobs data later in global trading day, market analysts say. "But we may not see heavy selling because investors don't want to take large positions before the three-day weekend," says Japanese brokerage manager, noting Monday Japan holiday. Nikkei may stay in range of 9550-9750, market analysts say. 20/33 Topix subindexes lower, Elpida Memory (6665.TO) down 3.1% at Y864 on dilution fears after convertible bond issue report. Strong yen beneficiaries such as papermakers higher as they are expected to cut costs to import raw materials; \uc2 Nippon Paper Group\uc2  (3893.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,200. Banks slightly higher; \uc2 Sumitomo Trust & Banking\uc2  (8403.TO) up 0.9% at Y469. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 2.9% at Y1,170 after withdrawing additional share issue shelf registration. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds mixed, bond futures little moved ahead of key U.S. jobs data later Friday, while 5-year bonds relatively weak ahead of government auction for those bonds next week; finance ministry to sell KRW1.2 trillion worth of 5-year treasuries Monday. 3-year yield quoted down 1 bp at 3.30%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 3.64%, 10-year yield +2 bps at 4.02%, December futures +2 ticks at 112.64. Bond manager at local securities firm tips futures in 112.50-112.80 range for day, with foreign buying likely providing support. Notes, longer-tenor bonds relatively weak today, while demand for shorter bonds quite solid; but overall, big moves unlikely as investors sidelined ahead of U.S. data. Adds, KRW moves also eyed, as weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may weigh on bonds. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD off early at 30.820 vs 31.042 late yesterday; hit intraday low at 30.675 (lowest since August 2008) yesterday, but local trader says pair stabilizing today. "There is of course still pressure for the Taiwan dollar to rise, but many are holding off after hitting the 2-year low yesterday," he says. Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in day--as it's done frequently over recent weeks--to push pair to 31.000, tips 30.700-31.000 range for day. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 2252ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a80001y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
