ket awaits U.S. September employment report due later in global day, according to analysts; job data "will set the tone for a number of sessions to come...(it) will be the definitive employment figure," advisory firm Cameron Hanover says; while forecast picture bearish, last week's reduced first-time claims data surprise on upside. Meanwhile, "dollar is the key short-term factor and the reinstatement of the linkage with the dollar will stay for a while yet," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . November Nymex crude down 23 cents at $81.44/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.2% at 3228.77, likely to drift sideways in "lackluster" market after interest in stock exchange's new dairy futures market disappoints, says First NZ Capital broker James Lee; "the market was looking for a few trades on the new dairy futures market and so far we are nought for nought." The first cash-settled dairy futures market launched by exchange at 1900 GMT yesterday but no bids have been made. "Until we get a trade and it all settles it is all theoretical." Largest stock Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) remains flat at NZ$8.05 as market lacks cues. Lee says \uc2 DNZ Property Fund\uc2  +0.9% at NZ$1.14 after entering NZX-50; "it shouldn't be a surprise (it's in the index), the market was looking for that sometime this month." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI stubbornly refuses to correct, +0.2% at 22,929.19, rising for 22nd session out of 26, as A-shares' gains after week-long holiday support. Still, Taifook Securities tips "a meaningful correction" may soon appear, although given that ultra-low interest rates in developed countries fuelling massive fund flows into emerging countries, any downward adjustment in local market "may prove minor and temporary." Mainland developers falling after Shanghai's latest property tightening measures; China Overseas Land (0688.HK) down 1.4% at HK$16.64, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) down 2.3% at HK$15.84. Market volume remains solid at HK$10.80 billion; psychological 23,000 remains immediate cap. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9646.59 midday as investors shy away from exporters on weak USD/JPY (now 82.36) and global cyclical stocks before release of U.S. jobs data later in global trading day, market analysts say. "But we may not see heavy selling because investors don't want to take large positions before the three-day weekend," says Japanese brokerage manager, noting Monday Japan holiday. Nikkei may stay in range of 9550-9750, market analysts say. 20/33 Topix subindexes lower, Elpida Memory (6665.TO) down 3.1% at Y864 on dilution fears after convertible bond issue report. Strong yen beneficiaries such as papermakers higher as they are expected to cut costs to import raw materials; \uc2 Nippon Paper Group\uc2  (3893.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,200. Banks slightly higher; \uc2 Sumitomo Trust & Banking\uc2  (8403.TO) up 0.9% at Y469. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 2.9% at Y1,170 after withdrawing additional share issue shelf registration. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds mixed, bond futures little moved ahead of key U.S. jobs data later Friday, while 5-year bonds relatively weak ahead of government auction for those bonds next week; finance ministry to sell KRW1.2 trillion worth of 5-year treasuries Monday. 3-year yield quoted down 1 bp at 3.30%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 3.64%, 10-year yield +2 bps at 4.02%, December futures +2 ticks at 112.64. Bond manager at local securities firm tips futures in 112.50-112.80 range for day, with foreign buying likely providing support. Notes, longer-tenor bonds relatively weak today, while demand for shorter bonds quite solid; but overall, big moves unlikely as investors sidelined ahead of U.S. data. Adds, KRW moves also eyed, as weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may weigh on bonds. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD off early at 30.820 vs 31.042 late yesterday; hit intraday low at 30.675 (lowest since August 2008) yesterday, but local trader says pair stabilizing today. "There is of course still pressure for the Taiwan dollar to rise, but many are holding off after hitting the 2-year low yesterday," he says. Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in day--as it's done frequently over recent weeks--to push pair to 31.000, tips 30.700-31.000 range for day. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares up sharply as trade resumes after week-long break as metal names catch up with gains in U.S. stocks. Shanghai Composite Index +2.1% at 2710.63, tipped to trade around 2700 for now. "The morning's sharp catch-up rally will likely be sustained, though internal factors such as Shanghai's new property tightening measures should become a dominant factor, weighing the market in coming sessions," says Li Jing at Huatai Securities. Among actives, Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) +9.2% at CNY33.99, coal-miner \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) +6.2% at CNY25.07. Zijing Mining (601899.SH) limit-up 10% at CNY8.00. Shenzhen Composite Index +1.8% at 1189.96. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up amid improved liquidity after week-long National Day holiday. "Big banks, which were short of cash before the holiday, are starting to lend money to other institutional investors now as holiday-related demand for cash fades," says Shanghai-based trader with local insurer. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.75 vs 126.68 last close on Sept. 30, while interbank weighted average 7-day repo down 15 bps at 2.70%. Trader tips bond index to consolidate in 126.65-126.80 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 2247ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM0000002010100