ower, Elpida Memory (6665.TO) down 3.1% at Y864 on dilution fears after convertible bond issue report. Strong yen beneficiaries such as papermakers higher as they are expected to cut costs to import raw materials; \uc2 Nippon Paper Group\uc2  (3893.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,200. Banks slightly higher; \uc2 Sumitomo Trust & Banking\uc2  (8403.TO) up 0.9% at Y469. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 2.9% at Y1,170 after withdrawing additional share issue shelf registration. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds mixed, bond futures little moved ahead of key U.S. jobs data later Friday, while 5-year bonds relatively weak ahead of government auction for those bonds next week; finance ministry to sell KRW1.2 trillion worth of 5-year treasuries Monday. 3-year yield quoted down 1 bp at 3.30%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 3.64%, 10-year yield +2 bps at 4.02%, December futures +2 ticks at 112.64. Bond manager at local securities firm tips futures in 112.50-112.80 range for day, with foreign buying likely providing support. Notes, longer-tenor bonds relatively weak today, while demand for shorter bonds quite solid; but overall, big moves unlikely as investors sidelined ahead of U.S. data. Adds, KRW moves also eyed, as weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may weigh on bonds. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD off early at 30.820 vs 31.042 late yesterday; hit intraday low at 30.675 (lowest since August 2008) yesterday, but local trader says pair stabilizing today. "There is of course still pressure for the Taiwan dollar to rise, but many are holding off after hitting the 2-year low yesterday," he says. Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in day--as it's done frequently over recent weeks--to push pair to 31.000, tips 30.700-31.000 range for day. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares up sharply as trade resumes after week-long break as metal names catch up with gains in U.S. stocks. Shanghai Composite Index +2.1% at 2710.63, tipped to trade around 2700 for now. "The morning's sharp catch-up rally will likely be sustained, though internal factors such as Shanghai's new property tightening measures should become a dominant factor, weighing the market in coming sessions," says Li Jing at Huatai Securities. Among actives, Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) +9.2% at CNY33.99, coal-miner \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) +6.2% at CNY25.07. Zijing Mining (601899.SH) limit-up 10% at CNY8.00. Shenzhen Composite Index +1.8% at 1189.96. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up amid improved liquidity after week-long National Day holiday. "Big banks, which were short of cash before the holiday, are starting to lend money to other institutional investors now as holiday-related demand for cash fades," says Shanghai-based trader with local insurer. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.75 vs 126.68 last close on Sept. 30, while interbank weighted average 7-day repo down 15 bps at 2.70%. Trader tips bond index to consolidate in 126.65-126.80 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Palladium likely to outperform in 2011, says BMO Capital Markets analyst Bart Melek who raises bank's 2011 average price forecast by 33% to $700/oz. BMO says that on the demand side palladium is benefiting, like gold, silver and platinum, from its quasi-monetary properties with investors concerned over the U.S. Fed's indications of renewed quantitative easing, long-term concerns surrounding sovereign debt defaults and fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchasing power. Palladium is also most direct commodity beneficiary of boom in Chinese auto production. "Palladium is the main beneficiary of strong vehicle output, since China is mostly a gasoline auto market, which mainly uses palladium in catalytic converters," it says. On supply side lower shipments of Russian palladium to Switzerland are also supportive. "If Russian official stocks are nearing exhaustion like some observers believe and BMO Research is starting to expect, palladium can go much higher than ($700/oz)." Spot palladium at $581/oz, down $2.00 since NY close but well off yesterday's $601.50/oz, 9-year high. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 10-07-10 2243ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a80001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
