\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Weakens As China Bond News Boosts Sentiment\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 07:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar weakened across the board in Asia Friday as a possible rating upgrade on Chinese sovereign debt and hawkish comments from an Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official prompted investors to dump the greenback for higher-yielding units.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Services Inc. said earlier in the day that it's reviewing China's sovereign bond rating for a possible upgrade from the current A1.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  Deputy Gov. \uc2 Ric Battellino\uc2  said "higher interest rates will be required" if "economic conditions evolve as currently expected," suggesting the RBA remains in a tightening cycle despite its decision to hold monetary policy steady earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The developments gave a boost to the outlook for global economies other than the U.S., dealers said, leading investors to sell the greenback and buy the risk-sensitive euro\par \par \uc2 As of 0450 GMT, the euro was $1.3950 from $1.3912 in New York Thursday. Against the yen, the dollar was at Y82.36 from Y82.40 overnight.\par \par \uc2 Despite the yen's already-low interest rates, investors are increasing their holdings of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they think the Federal Reserve will take aggressive monetary easing measures while the Bank of Japan's room for further policy moves is limited, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 To better gauge when the Fed will act, investors will pay attention to non-farm payrolls data for September, investors said. The data, due at 1230 GMT, are likely to show a 10,000 decline in jobs from August, when jobs fell by 54,000, according to a Dow Jones poll of economists.\par \par \uc2 If the result shows the U.S. labor market is worsening, investors will likely push the dollar down to Y81.00, said Kenichi Nishii, a senior dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. Even at that level, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise is unlikely, he added.\par \par \uc2 Other dealers in Tokyo echoed that view.\par \par \uc2 "Many investors have given up portfolios constructed to make profits on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar fell clearly below Y82.50," said Satoshi Tate, a senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Because the Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September was conducted around Y82.90, markets had speculated the Y82.50-Y82.90 range to be the "danger zone", he added.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said earlier Friday that Japan would take action when necessary, adding that the policy would not change before or after this weekend's meeting of financial chiefs and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations.\par \par \uc2 But few Tokyo dealers were taking the comment seriously, and even if Japan does take action, "we would punch it back with large yen-buying," said a hedge fund manager in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The yen was weaker against the euro, though, due to increasingly positive views over global economies. Against the euro, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y114.88 from Y114.65 Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 77.308 from 77.464.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              82.35-38   82.40-43   -0.06  82.51   82.16   -11.53 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.3948-52  1.3918-23  +0.22  1.3962  1.3904   -2.57 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.5889-93  1.5872-76  +0.11  1.5899  1.5861   -1.70 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      0.9661-66  0.9660-64  +0.01  0.9676  0.9650   -6.69 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.0158-64  1.0174-80  -0.16  1.0193  1.0157   -3.38 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.9827-30  0.9821-26  +0.06  0.9846  0.9793   +9.40 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.7523-33  0.7497-07  +0.35  0.7539  0.7493   +3.68 \par EUR/JPY Yen              114.88-95  114.70-75  +0.16  114.91  114.54  -13.79 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 08-10-10 0535GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101008e6a8000ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
