\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan May Be Using Stealth To Slow Yen's Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Flint  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 21:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Japan may be turning to guerilla tactics in its battle to stem the yen's appreciation: Talk of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through large Japanese banks is once again circulating in markets.\par \par \uc2 With further direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  deemed highly unlikely ahead of this weekend meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading economies in Washington, it isn't unreasonable that Japanese authorities could use less-orthodox methods to hinder yen gains.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is now trading well below the Y82.87 level at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets on Sept. 15 to the tune of Y2 trillion. The dollar dipped to a fresh 15-year low at Y82.11 in New York Thursday, but subsequently returned to about Y82.40, in line with a rebound against most of its rivals.\par \par \uc2 Last month's foray into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will have been in vain unless Japan takes further yen-weakening steps. But another round of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as off the table until Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda has had a chance to rally support for his government's market action in September.\par \par \uc2 Japan is eager to head off international criticism of its resumption of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a six-year pause, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 So, if Japanese authorities are hesitant to bring out the heavy artillery and shell markets with officially acknowledged dollar-buying once more, what can they do to halt the dollar's grind to a new post-World War II low below Y79.75?\par \par \uc2 They can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but stealthily, using Japanese banks as their agents. That way, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are left guessing whether the dollar is rising versus the yen because of real demand or because of government-ordered buying.\par \par \uc2 Chatter about covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began emerging about 10 days after the mid-September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As prevailing anti-dollar sentiment deprived the dollar of its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains above Y85, any temporary spike higher for the dollar got investors wondering as to whether Japanese banks had received an official mandate to snap up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it dipped below certain levels.\par \par \uc2 "Sure, it's possible," said Win Thin, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York. "But there's really no way to know."\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities decline to talk about stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and banks are under strict nondisclosure contracts, so there is no way to determine if the Bank of Japan had a role until monthly or quarterly statistical reports are released.\par \par \uc2 That is what makes such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  effective. Any time the dollar rallied a bit against the yen or Japanese banks were seen placing large orders, investors couldn't help but think about covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , perhaps even as a prelude to large-scale direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This type of action doesn't pack anywhere near the wallop of large-scale official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is analogous to a military frontal assault, stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more like behind-the-lines partisan activity. But just like partisan attacks, covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is intended to harass, not to retake a fixed position.\par \par \uc2 Covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been used by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, most notably in 2003 and 2004, when the BOJ spent an estimated Y35 trillion in a long campaign against yen strength that yielded mixed results.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ, acting on orders from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , couldn't reverse the dollar's slide against the yen back then, but it did manage to slow the dollar's rate of descent, according to analysts.\par \par \uc2 It may be playing that same role today.\par \par \uc2 (Robert Flint, a news editor in the money group, has covered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past 12 years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia. He can be reached at 212-416-2216 or Robert.Flint@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 10-07-10 1512ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020101007e6a7000ik\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
