\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3-Japan, S.Korea talk cooperation on  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 887 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 23:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Japan PM: to cooperate with G7 on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues\par \par \uc2 * S.Korea President: G20 should discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordination\par \par \uc2 * Zoellick: should consider steps to control flows- Nikkei\par \par \uc2 * IMF warns such actions are undesirable\par \par \uc2 (Adds Zoellick quotes on capital controls in paras 10-13 to clarify his position)\par \par \uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 7 (Reuters) - Japan and South Korea on Thursday sought to calm a brewing global row over exchange rates with pledges to work closely with their peers on challenges posed by economic imbalances driving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew fire from its partners last month when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own to cap the yen's rise, while South Korea has repeatedly bought U.S. dollars in markets to keep its won down, buying an estimated $11 billion just in the past two weeks.\par \par \uc2 As the yen shrugged off last month's action and scaled another 15-year high against the dollar on Thursday, Japan's Prime Minister Naoto Kan warned that Tokyo was ready to act decisively in response to sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 But he also said Japan would cooperate with its Group of Seven partners on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. [ID:nTKF107034] [ID:nTKG006895] [FRX/]\par \par \uc2 In similar vein, South Korean President Lee Myung-bak, who will chair next month's summit of the Group of 20 leaders in Seoul, said it should discuss coordination of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. [ID:nTOE696043]\par \par \uc2 The divisive issue whether and how nations should influence their exchange rates is pitching rich nations against mostly developing economies in Asia and Latin America and is due to be a hot topic at the Group of Seven and the International Monetary Fund meetings starting on Friday in Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 Western leaders are worried efforts by emerging economies to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exports could derail the fragile global economic recovery. Officials from developing markets say ultra-low interest rates in rich countries are fuelling massive fund flows into their markets, pushing up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and inflating prices of stocks, property and other assets.\par \par \uc2 <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Graphic showing trade weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2007:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/qun86p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/qun86p}}}\par \par \uc2 For a PDF on the so-called "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" click on:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/dyw27p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/dyw27p}}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 FEAR OF "\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  WAR"\par \par \uc2 To limit the rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , nations from South Korea to Brazil have moved to restrain capital flows or relied on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fanning fears that such isolated actions could escalate into devastating "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in Japan, South Korea and Taiwan swelled to record highs in September, largely reflecting authorities' efforts to curb rises in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  via market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Taiwan joined the fray when its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  inspected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards trades at banks as part of its campaign to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check and protect the economy from what it sees as the destabilising effects of speculation, a source said. [ID:nTOE69602U]\par \par \uc2 World Bank President Robert Zoellick said policymakers should be careful if considering capital controls.\par \par \uc2 "I think countries should move towards an open capital account position, but I think one can be pragmatic about how one regulates how money comes into a country," he said in an interview with Japanese newspaper Nikkei, according to a transcript issued by the World Bank.\par \par \uc2 "But one has to be careful because sometimes one person's regulation becomes another person's strangulation, so I would err on the side of being able to have fluidity in investment."\par \par \uc2 The World Bank issued the transcript of the interview after Nikkei quoted Zoellick as saying emerging market officials should consider measures to control short-term capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 IMF deputy managing director, Naoyuki Shinohara, said it was natural and welcome for money to shift into economies with strong growth and policymakers should not try to curb such flows or use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets.\par \par \uc2 "When there are occasionally volatile moves in the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be ruled out," he told Reuters in an interview in Washington on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "But it's totally undesirable for a country to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  consistently to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a certain level."\par \par \uc2 SLAP ON THE WRIST\par \par \uc2 Shinohara, who was Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tsar before assuming the IMF post, warned Tokyo faced a losing battle trying to go against the tide and weaken the yen as monetary conditions in the United States and Europe are expected to remain easy.\par \par \uc2 "This is not something that Japan can control. If Japan tries to adjust this, it will distort markets," Shinohara said, adding that Tokyo should instead focus on structural reforms and monetary easing to beat deflation.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold the yen for estimated $25 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years and has tried to placate its peers by highlighting how damaging the yen's climb was for an economy mired in deflation.\par \par \uc2 Deputy finance minister Mitsuru Sakurai admited on Thursday it would be hard to guide the G7 discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the way Tokyo wanted.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting Stanley White, Jeanny Kao in TAIPEI, Yoo Choonsik in SEOUL, Leika Kihara in WASHINGTON; writing by Tomasz Janowski; editing by Kazunori Takada and Bill Schomberg)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (WRAPUP 3, GRAPHIC)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|UKI|CANT|AUF|J|Z|ABX|BNX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101010e6a7000hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
