\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Mixed Ahead of Earnings, Payrolls Data\b0\par\par\uc2 2,874 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 00:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10948.58  -19.07  -0.17% \par Nasdaq     2383.67   +3.01  +0.13% \par S&P 500    1158.06   -1.91  -0.16% \par FTSE 100   5662.13  -19.26  -0.34% \par Xetra DAX  6276.25   +5.52  +0.09% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 1/32 +1/32 0.359% -2.4BP \par 5Y Tsy 100 18/32 +3/32 1.132% -2.2BP \par 10Y Tsy 102 2/32 +1/32 2.387% -0.5BP \par  \par USD/JPY       82.40-44     -0.66% \par Range         83.02-82.12 \par EUR/USD       1.3920-25    -0.07% \par Range         1.4028-1.3858 \par AUD/USD       0.9823-28    +0.52% \par Range         0.9920-0.9758 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    12.05-12.35% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    14.10-14.40% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    15.15-15.45% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,335.00 -$12.70 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,326.50-$1,366.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $81.67   -$1.56 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks ended mixed Thursday as jitters set in ahead of quarterly earnings reports and monthly jobs data, but better-than-expected employment and retail-sales numbers limited the decline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 19.07 points, or 0.17%, to 10948.58. \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  led the measure's drop with a decline of 1.4% ahead of the aluminum giant's third-quarter earnings report that kicked off the third-quarter earnings season. \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  rose 2.9% in after-hours trading as the company, which reported after the close, topped analysts' expectations for earnings and revenue.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index shed 1.91, or 0.16%, to 1158.06. The materials and telecommunications sectors led the decline, while consumer-staples companies were also weak as \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2  weighed with a 3% drop. The beverage-and-food giant reported a 12% rise in fiscal third-quarter profit, but it trimmed the top end of its 2010 earnings-growth forecast.\par \par \uc2 The market's reaction to the \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2  report prompted worries among investors about how stocks may be affected by other companies' quarterly results as the reporting season kicks off. That added to investors' nerves ahead of the government's monthly jobs report, due Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 "You're about to hit a couple big triggers," said Jason Pride, director of investment strategy at Glenmede, an investment and wealth-management firm. "If there's any point in time where you take some risk off the table, perhaps this is one."\par \par \uc2 Weekly jobs data released Thursday showed the number of U.S. workers filing new claims for jobless benefits unexpectedly fell last week, to their lowest level since July 10. Expectations are for the government on Friday morning to report fewer payroll cuts than reported in August, although analysts still anticipate a decrease of 10,000 U.S. jobs and a slight rise in unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Still, even if Friday's jobs report disappoints, as the monthly private-sector employment data did Wednesday, stocks could get a boost. Weak numbers could raise expectations for the Federal Reserve to hit the market with additional stimulus measures more quickly.\par \par \uc2 Pride said some investors might not want the report to look overly positive "because, if it's too optimistic, then the Fed will actually have a hard time arguing why they're doing [quantitative easing], which arguably is why the market's sitting at the levels we're at now."\par \par \uc2 Retailers' same-store sales results for September provided some encouragement, with many companies topping expectations for back-to-school sales. \uc2 American Eagle Outfitters\uc2 , \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch\uc2  and \uc2 Limited Brands\uc2  were among the standouts. American Eagle--which also boosted its fiscal third-quarter earnings outlook--climbed 8.1%, while Abercrombie jumped 8.9% and Limited added 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite edged up 3.01, or 0.13%, to 2383.67, boosted by a 12% jump in \uc2 Adobe Systems\uc2  after the New York Times reported that \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Steven Ballmer\uc2  was at the tech giant's headquarters for a "secret meeting." \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  edged up 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average ended marginally lower Thursday as chip-related stocks weakened on worries about declining chip prices, while retailers helped the Topix index end higher on brisk earnings results.\par \par \uc2 Both the Nikkei and the broader Topix stayed in narrow ranges for the better part of the day, with analysts citing investor caution ahead of U.S. jobs data Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Investors wanted to secure profits from early week before they see a potentially negative surprise," said Yoshinori Nagano, senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares rose for the fourth consecutive session Thursday due to continued optimism the U.S. Federal Reserve will opt to restart purchases of U.S. Treasury bonds at its board meeting in early November.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they are optimistic about the market's outlook, with Castor Pang, the research director at \uc2 Cinda International\uc2 , saying he expects the index to continue its uptrend this year, although at a slower pace.\par \par \uc2 "Consolidation is needed before we can see further gains," he said, adding he thinks the index could hit 23,800 by the end of 2010.\par \par \uc2 Tanrich Securities investment manager Jackson Wong said he expects consolidation pressure to remain mild, however, as the outlook for equity markets remains positive given the aggressive quantitative easing expected both in the U.S. and globally.\par \par \uc2 Mainland China markets reopen Friday after being closed this week.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures retreated from a five-month high Thursday in U.S. trading as a stronger dollar and still-high U.S. inventories stifled a move towards $85 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Comex gold prices tumbled from record highs Thursday as a surprisingly upbeat jobless claims report and caution ahead of Friday's monthly employment data led investors to sell the metal.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange reversed lower Thursday as investors sold risky assets across financial markets ahead of Friday's U.S. jobs data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar recovered from a broad slump against most of its rivals Thursday as investors pared bets on riskier assets ahead of the closely watched reading of monthly U.S. employment figures due Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar early Thursday dropped to a 15-year low against the yen, a record low against the Swiss franc and its lowest level since 1983 against the Australian dollar. As U.S. stocks took a turn lower, the dollar recovered much of its loss.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ahead of Friday's report on U.S. nonfarm payrolls, considered a key gauge of the pace of the economy, investors took some money off the risk table, helping the dollar, which is seen as a safe harbor, gain, though the greenback remains broadly weakened against its rivals, having fallen around 10% against the euro since July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Clients are hesitant to push too much" on the euro and other higher-yielding assets, said Amelia Bourdeau, senior G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Stamford, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.3912 from $1.3929 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose as high as $1.4030. The dollar was at Y82.40 from Y82.96, after falling as low as Y82.11, while the euro was at Y114.65 from Y115.55. The U.K. pound was at $1.5862 from $1.5885. The dollar was at CHF0.9669 from CHF0.9618.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 77.464 from 77.435, rebounding from a dip below 77, its lowest level since January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, investors continue to expect a fresh round of stimulus from the Federal Reserve just around the corner, with the broad trend of dollar weakness likely to remain in place, said Steven Barrow, head of G10 strategy at Standard Bank in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While investors continue to speculate that a Fed stimulus program is imminent, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, on the other hand, articulated a steady-on approach to monetary policy at the ECB's Thursday press conference; the ECB earlier left key rates unchanged. This contrast--the ECB on the path to eventual policy normalization, with the Fed possibly near to policy loosening--propelled the euro above $1.40.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro/dollar cross is unlikely to test topside Friday, with consolidation expected ahead of U.S. non-farms payroll data due out overnight, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  NZ chief manager Daniel Brdanovic said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think we will see EUR go to new highs today as we saw it test up there last night. It was sort of rejected there quite sharply and we've still got plenty of risk ahead with the payroll data."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar took advantage of broad dollar weakness and a much better-than-expected Australian jobs report by shooting to its highest level since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was floated in 1983, gaining above the US$0.99 mark, leading some analysts to point to possible parity. The U.S. dollar took back some of the Aussie's gains as U.S. stocks dipped into the red.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market is "waiting to see if (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Deputy Governor Ric) Battellino gives anything away" in a speech this afternoon, \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  head of market economics and strategy Khoon Goh said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If he does have hawkish rhetoric, certainly if he starts to flag that a hike in November is likely, or if there is any word of further hikes beyond that, it will be very, very supportive of the Australian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goh noted AUD/USD may be taking a bit of a breather; "It has gone up so far, so fast that we will always expect a corrective pullback. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets don't go up in a straight line."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar was last at US$0.9817 after touching post-float high of US$0.9918 overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before posting a gain against the Swiss franc, the dollar earlier fell to a new all-time low against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dropping as low as CHF0.9555, while the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also declined against the yen, dropping to the fresh 15-year low, at Y82.11, and keeping investors on alert for signs of Japan in the market to prop the dollar and stem yen strength. [ 07-10-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, said there were no signs of Japan in the market to stem yen strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors still think \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is possible, but they said the risk is decreasing for now because a meeting of financial ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations will be held this weekend. Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  just before the meeting would raise difficult questions for Japanese authorities in trying to explain that they aren't just pushing the yen lower to gain a trade advantage against other nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England also left its monetary policy unchanged Thursday, though speculation mounted whether the bank's Monetary Policy Committee would eventually enact another round of its own stimulus--asset purchases dubbed quantitative easing--which would likely weigh on the pound. The U.K. pound was down by about 0.3% against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, People's Bank of China Deputy Governor Yi Gang said Thursday that China is trying hard to avert a potential global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are already signs of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war, but China "is working hard against it," Yi said on the sidelines of an event during the annual meetings of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys maturing in 10 years or less rose Thursday as many investors continued to bet that the Federal Reserve will step up government debt purchases to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shorter-dated notes led the advance. At the peak of the buying, the two-year yield touched a record low of 0.355%, and the five-year note's yield hit an all-time trough of 1.119%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gains were moderate as investors awaited the key U.S. jobs report due Friday morning, the last monthly nonfarm payrolls data before the Federal Reserve's monetary policy meeting in early November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Fed policy expectations are still driving the market higher," said Chris Rupkey, chief financial economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1955 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was 1/32 higher to yield 2.387%. The 30-year bond was 26/32 lower to yield 3.714%. The two-year note was 1/32 higher to yield 0.359%, and the five-year note was 3/32 higher to yield 1.132%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond was the only loser, as many investors bet the Fed's bond buying is likely to concentrate on shorter-dated securities. The yield premium of the 30-year bond over the benchmark 10-year note increased to a record high of 132.7 basis points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Briggs, U.S. interest rate strategist at RBS Securities Inc in Stamford, Conn., said the 30-year bond also suffered amid speculation the Fed's stimulus will generate inflation in the longer term, which eats into bonds' fixed return over time. The 30-year maturity is the most sensitive to changes in inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The 30-year bond continues to suffer from the increased rhetoric on inflationary policy, and the market is giving credibility to the fact that if the Fed wants to generate inflation they will succeed," said Briggs. "The jobs data Friday may skew opinions on the size and scope of [quantitative easing], but it is hard to see the data taking it off the table at this point."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looming new debt supply weighed on long-dated bonds. The Treasury Department said Thursday it will sell $66 billion in new government notes and bonds next week, in line with market expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended lower Thursday, as market participants took profits after the release of the results of a closely watched key 10-year auction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However in the long run, "it is likely to remain difficult for players to sell JGBs until the next (Federal Open Market Committee meeting Nov. 2-3). Even if the market experiences a temporary downward correction, interest in JGBs will likely return," said Makoto Yamashita, a strategist at Deutsche Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yamashita tips the benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield to fall as low as 0.78% if key nonfarm payroll data for September due Friday show weakness in the U.S. economy, fueling speculation there could be an easing competition between the U.S. and Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's no more room for a further decline in five-year yields or shorter, which are affected by the BOJ's policy," said Nobuto Yamazaki, executive fund manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 DLIBJ Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The 10-year sector or longer are the only areas where players are still willing to buy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Friday, Japan's current account surplus likely fell 16.3% on the year in August to Y990 billion, according to the median forecast in a survey of economists by Dow Jones and Nikkei. Such a result would likely increase concern over the health of Japan's export-driven economy, and would also raise concerns the surplus may be in a declining trend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Jobless Claims Fall 11,000 To 445,000 \par US Consumers Cut Borrowing In August \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Falls 21%; Prices, Demand Up \par BOJ Gov: Don't Think Nations Competing To Ease Monetary Policy \par PBOC Deputy Governor Yi: There Are Signs Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  War \par Retailers' September Sales Strong \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Genzyme\f28 \uc2 \f2  Explores Options \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Adobe\f28 \uc2 \f2  Up On Report Of Ballmer Meeting \par ECB Leaves Interest Rates Unchanged \par Pimco Buys Tips In Bet Against Deflation \par Greece Sees Return To Primary Sur